window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("training-adaptation", {"topics":[{"key":"training-adaptation/pre-training","sources":["Radford et al., 2018 — Improving Language Understanding by Generative Pre-Training","Brown et al., 2020 — Language Models are Few-Shot Learners","Kaplan et al., 2020 — Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models","Hoffmann et al., 2022 — Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models"],"why":"Before pre-training, each NLP task got its own model trained from scratch on its own labelled data. A\nsentiment classifier learned English from a few thousand labelled reviews, which is far too little to\nlearn grammar, world knowledge and idiom. Labelled data is expensive, and the quantity needed to learn\nlanguage from zero would be impossible to annotate.\n\nThe way out is to find a task whose labels are free. Next-token prediction is that task: take any text,\nhide the token after position *t*, and ask the model to predict it from tokens ≤ *t*. Every document on the\nweb becomes training data without a single annotator. To predict well, the model is forced to learn\nsyntax (which words can follow), semantics (which continuations make sense), facts (the capital of France\nis ...), and even procedural patterns (how a proof or a function body continues). The objective is\nsimple; the data makes it hard.\n\nThe naive alternative of training the big model directly on your downstream task fails for two reasons.\nFirst, no downstream dataset is large enough to set billions of parameters without overfitting. Second,\nmost of what a model needs for your task is general competence that is shared across tasks, so paying to\nlearn it once, then adapting, is cheaper than paying for it per task. Pre-training is that one-time\npayment, and fine-tuning, RAG and prompting are all ways to spend its result.","how":"### The objective\nFor a token sequence x₁…x_T, the model with parameters θ is trained to maximise the log-likelihood of\neach token given its prefix:\n\n```\nL(θ) = − Σ_t log p_θ(x_t | x_<t)\n```\n\nThis is cross-entropy between the one-hot next token and the model's softmax output, averaged over every\nposition in every sequence. The causal mask lets one forward pass score all T positions at once, so a\nsequence of 8k tokens yields 8k training signals. Perplexity is just exp of this loss, which is why\npre-training progress is reported in loss or perplexity rather than accuracy.\n\n### The data\nTokens come from a curated mixture: web crawl, code, books, reference text, academic papers, and\nincreasingly multilingual and synthetic data. The pipeline matters as much as the model: language\nidentification, quality filtering (classifiers or heuristics), near-duplicate removal (duplicated text\nmakes the model memorise rather than generalise), and decontamination against evaluation sets. The\n*mixture weights* (how much code versus web) are a design choice that shapes what the base model is good\nat, because the model allocates capacity to what it sees most.\n\n### The compute budget\nTraining cost scales with parameters N and tokens D. A standard approximation for a dense transformer\nis roughly 6·N·D floating-point operations: 2·N·D for the forward pass and about twice that for\nbackward. The scaling-law work asked how to split a fixed budget between N and D. Kaplan et al. found\nloss falls smoothly as a power law in compute; Hoffmann et al. (the Chinchilla result) showed that\nearlier models were undertrained, and that for compute-optimal training N and D should grow together,\nwith roughly 20 tokens per parameter as a rule of thumb. Later open models deliberately train far past\nthat ratio because they optimise for *inference* cost: a smaller model trained on more tokens is cheaper\nto serve for the same quality.\n\n### The schedule\nPractical recipes use AdamW with a linear warmup followed by a cosine or warmup-stable-decay schedule,\nbatch sizes in the millions of tokens, bf16 mixed precision and gradient clipping. Training runs in\nstages: the main run on short sequences, then a shorter long-context stage that extends the context\nwindow, and often a final *annealing* phase on high-quality data with the learning rate decayed to near\nzero, which measurably lifts benchmark scores. Mid-training checkpoints are kept so a loss spike or\ndivergence can be rolled back.\n\n### Why the result is a base model\nNothing in the objective says \"be helpful\". A base model prompted with a question may answer, continue\nwith more questions, or produce a forum-style thread, because all of those are plausible continuations\nof web text. Instruction following, refusals and chat formatting are added by post-training. The base\nmodel's job is to carry the knowledge and the latent skills; it is the asset that every downstream stage\nstarts from.","example":"The whole pre-training objective fits in a few lines. Given a batch of token ids, the labels are the\nsame ids shifted left by one; the loss is cross-entropy at every position. This is exactly what\n`AutoModelForCausalLM` does internally when you pass `labels=input_ids`.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"               # a small base model, not an -Instruct one\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16).cuda()\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-5, weight_decay=0.1)\n\ntext = \"Pre-training predicts the next token. The loss is cross-entropy over the vocabulary.\"\nids = tok(text, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()        # [1, T]\n\nlogits = model(input_ids=ids).logits                          # [1, T, vocab]\npred, target = logits[:, :-1, :], ids[:, 1:]                  # position t predicts token t+1\nloss = F.cross_entropy(pred.reshape(-1, pred.size(-1)).float(), target.reshape(-1))\nprint(f\"loss={loss.item():.3f}  perplexity={loss.exp().item():.1f}\")\n\nloss.backward()                                               # one optimiser step of \"pre-training\"\ntorch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), 1.0)\nopt.step(); opt.zero_grad()\n# Scale this loop to ~10^13 tokens, thousands of GPUs and a cosine LR schedule and you have a base model.\n```\n\nBack-of-envelope on the budget, as an illustrative order of magnitude: a 7B-parameter model trained on\n1 trillion tokens needs about 6 × 7e9 × 1e12 ≈ 4e22 FLOPs. At an achieved 4e14 FLOP/s per GPU (roughly\n40% utilisation of a modern accelerator's bf16 peak) that is 1e8 GPU-seconds, about 1,200 GPU-days.\nPre-training is the one stage where the arithmetic is in GPU-years, not GPU-hours.","tradeoffs":["More tokens per parameter raise quality for a fixed model size and lower serving cost, but past the compute-optimal point each extra point of quality costs disproportionately more training compute.","A broad data mixture gives general competence, while over-weighting one domain (code, a language) improves it at the expense of others; the right mix depends on what the model will be served for.","Longer training sequences teach long-range structure but cost quadratic attention compute, which is why context extension is done in a short late stage rather than from the start.","Aggressive quality filtering raises per-token value but can remove the dialects, formats and messy text the model will meet in production, hurting robustness."],"pitfalls":["Judging a base model by chatting with it; it was never trained to answer, so a rambling or repetitive reply says nothing about its knowledge. Evaluate base models with few-shot prompts or log-likelihood tasks instead.","Letting evaluation data leak into the corpus; the symptom is benchmark scores that do not transfer to held-out tasks of the same kind, which is why decontamination is part of the pipeline.","Reading a single pre-training loss number as a quality comparison across models; loss depends on the tokenizer and the data mix, so two models' losses are only comparable with the same tokenizer and evaluation corpus.","Treating \"continued pre-training\" on domain text as cheap; without mixing in general data at a sensible ratio the model forgets general skills, and the symptom is a jump in loss on everything outside the domain."],"when":"Almost no application team pre-trains from scratch; the cost is in GPU-years and the data engineering\nis a product in itself. What you decide is *which* pre-trained base to build on, and the knowledge of how\nit was made is what lets you decide well: its token budget and data mix predict its strengths, its\ntokenizer and context window are fixed at this stage, and its licence and training-data provenance are\nset here. Continued pre-training on a large domain corpus (tens of millions of tokens or more, such as\nlegal or biomedical text) is the one case where an application team does run this objective, usually\non a smaller model. In interviews, expect to explain the objective, why it is self-supervised, what\nscaling laws say about N versus D, and why a base model differs from a chat model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Chooses a base checkpoint by its training tokens, data mix and context length, and runs continued pre-training only with a general-data replay mix to avoid forgetting.","architect":"Owns the build-versus-adopt decision on foundation models, weighing training cost, data provenance and licence terms against the control a custom base would give.","fde":"Explains to a customer why \"train a model on our data\" usually means fine-tuning or RAG on a pre-trained base, not pre-training, and what a corpus of their size can realistically teach."},"check":{"q":"A customer with 200 million tokens of proprietary manuals asks you to \"pre-train a model on our\ndata so it knows our products\". How do you respond, and what would you actually propose?","a":"200M tokens is four orders of magnitude short of a pre-training corpus, so training from scratch\nwould produce a model that cannot even write fluent English. The knowledge they want lives in two\nplaces: facts, which are best served by retrieval over the manuals so answers stay current and citable,\nand style or terminology, which a fine-tune on a pre-trained base can absorb. If the domain vocabulary is\ngenuinely far from web text, continued pre-training of an open-weight base on the 200M tokens, mixed with\ngeneral data to prevent forgetting, is a defensible middle option. I would propose RAG first, measure the\nfailure cases, and only then decide whether adaptation of the weights is justified."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can pre-training use the raw internet while earlier NLP models needed labelled datasets?","options":["Transformers are robust to label noise, so unlabelled text is acceptable","Next-token prediction uses the text itself as the label, so no annotation is needed","Web text is pre-labelled by its HTML structure","Pre-training uses clustering, which is unsupervised"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The target at each position is simply the next token already present in the document. That makes the objective self-supervised: supervision exists, but it comes for free from the data rather than from annotators."},{"q":"According to the Chinchilla result, what was wrong with earlier large models like GPT-3?","options":["They had too few parameters for their training data","They were trained on too few tokens for their parameter count","They used the wrong tokenizer","They were trained with too high a learning rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hoffmann et al. showed that for a fixed compute budget, parameters and tokens should scale together, roughly 20 tokens per parameter. GPT-3-era models had far fewer tokens than that, so a smaller model trained longer would have matched them for the same compute."},{"q":"A freshly pre-trained base model is asked \"What is the capital of France?\" and replies with three more geography questions. What does this show?","options":["The model failed to learn the fact during pre-training","The tokenizer split the question badly","The base model is continuing text in a plausible style; answering is a behaviour added in post-training","The learning rate was too low in the annealing phase"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A list of questions is a perfectly likely continuation of a question in web text. The fact may well be encoded; eliciting it as an answer rather than a continuation is what instruction tuning and preference training provide."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/post-training","sources":["Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models","Lambert et al., 2024 — Tülu 3: Pushing Frontiers in Open Language Model Post-Training"],"why":"A base model is a very good autocomplete. Ask it a question and it may continue with more questions,\na forum thread, or an answer in the wrong register, because all are likely continuations of web text.\nIt has no notion of a \"user\" and an \"assistant\", no reason to be concise, no reason to decline a\nharmful request, and no habit of showing its reasoning. The knowledge is there; the behaviour is not.\n\nThe first fix people tried was prompting: write a few examples of question-and-answer in the context\nand let the base model pattern-match. It works surprisingly well, which is itself evidence that the\nskills are latent, but it is fragile, eats context, and cannot teach the model anything that is not\nalready an obvious pattern (a consistent refusal policy, a house JSON format, calling a tool when\nunsure). It also cannot express preferences: \"this answer is better than that one\" has no place in a\nnext-token loss.\n\nPost-training solves this in layers. Demonstrations teach the format and the basic behaviour.\nComparisons teach the quality dimension that demonstrations cannot express, because for most prompts\nthere is no single correct answer, only better and worse ones. Reinforcement learning with checkable\nrewards teaches behaviours that need many attempts to discover, such as multi-step reasoning. Each\nlayer exists because the previous one has a limit that only a different signal can get past.","how":"### Stage 1: supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations\nHumans (or a stronger model) write prompt-response pairs in the target format: a system prompt, a\nuser turn, an assistant turn. The model is trained with the same next-token loss as pre-training, but\nonly on the assistant tokens. This installs the chat template, the assistant persona and the basic\n\"answer the question\" behaviour. It is cheap and stable, which is why it comes first: later stages\nneed a policy that already produces plausible candidate answers. Its limit is that it can only imitate;\nit cannot tell the model that one of two fluent answers is wrong. See *supervised fine-tuning* and\n*instruction tuning* for the mechanics and data design.\n\n### Stage 2: preference optimisation on comparisons\nRaters see two or more responses to the same prompt and rank them. This data is cheaper to produce\nthan demonstrations and captures judgements people find easy to make but hard to write down. Two\nfamilies consume it. RLHF trains a reward model on the comparisons and then optimises the policy\nagainst it with PPO, with a KL penalty that stops the policy drifting too far from the SFT model. DPO\nskips the reward model and optimises the policy directly on the pairs with a classification-style\nloss derived from the same KL-constrained objective. The choice is an engineering one: RLHF is\non-policy and can keep improving as the policy changes; DPO is simpler, cheaper and offline. Both\nraise the quality of answers the SFT model already produces and are where refusal behaviour and tone\nare mostly shaped.\n\n### Stage 3: reinforcement learning on verifiable rewards\nFor tasks with a checkable answer, such as maths with a known result, code with unit tests, or output\nthat must parse as JSON, the reward can be computed by a program rather than learned from humans.\nThe model samples many attempts per prompt, is rewarded for the correct ones, and is pushed toward\nwhatever reasoning got it there. GRPO is the common algorithm because it needs no value model. This is\nthe stage that produces long chain-of-thought reasoning behaviour in recent models, and it is the most\nexpensive post-training stage, because generating many long samples per prompt dominates the cost,\nthough it remains orders of magnitude below pre-training.\n\n### The recurring design constraints\n- **A reference model and a KL leash.** Nearly every stage after SFT penalises distance from the previous\n  checkpoint, because an unconstrained optimiser finds degenerate outputs that score well on the reward\n  and badly with users. The strength of that leash is the central hyperparameter.\n- **Iteration.** Production recipes run several rounds: train, sample from the new model, collect new\n  comparisons on those samples, train again. Off-policy data from an old model becomes less useful as\n  the policy moves.\n- **Evaluation per stage.** Each stage has its own failure modes (forgetting after SFT, verbosity after\n  preference training, reward hacking after RL), so each gets its own gates: capability benchmarks to\n  check nothing was lost, preference win rates, and task-specific checks.\n- **Safety and policy.** Refusal behaviour, handling of sensitive topics and tool-use permissions are\n  trained in with targeted SFT data and preference pairs, then red-teamed, because a model that is\n  helpful but unsafe cannot ship.\n\n### What post-training costs\nPre-training is measured in GPU-years. SFT on tens of thousands of examples is GPU-hours on one node;\nDPO on a comparable set is similar; RLHF or GRPO is more, because sampling dominates, but still orders\nof magnitude below pre-training. The expensive part of post-training is the data: demonstrations and\ncomparisons written or reviewed by people, and the evaluation sets that tell you whether a round helped.","example":"A team takes an open-weight 8B base model and post-trains it as a support assistant for a software\nproduct. All sizes are illustrative.\n\n1. **SFT.** They write 6,000 demonstrations: real support tickets paired with answers authored by\n   senior agents, rendered through a chat template with a fixed system prompt. After two epochs the\n   model answers in the right voice and format. Evaluation on a held-out set shows it is fluent but\n   invents feature names about one time in eight, and it answers \"how do I delete my account\" with a\n   wall of text where the policy is a short, specific procedure.\n2. **Preference optimisation.** For 2,000 held-out tickets they sample four responses each from the SFT\n   model and have agents rank them, with a rubric that penalises invented features and rewards\n   concision. DPO on the resulting pairs (the top-ranked response against each of the other three, 6,000 in all)\n   cuts hallucinated feature names by more than half in\n   the held-out set and shortens answers. A capability benchmark run before and after confirms the\n   model's general question-answering has not regressed.\n3. **Verifiable rewards.** Some answers must emit a JSON block that opens a ticket in the help-desk\n   system. They write a reward function: +1 if the JSON parses and matches the schema, +1 if the ticket\n   category is correct per a lookup table, 0 otherwise. A few hundred GRPO steps with 8 samples per\n   prompt raise schema validity from 91% to above 99%, something the SFT data alone never achieved\n   because the few malformed examples were drowned out.\n4. **Iteration.** They collect a fresh batch of rankings on the *new* model's outputs, because the old\n   pairs no longer describe its mistakes, and run one more DPO round before release.\n\nThe whole cycle costs a few hundred GPU-hours and several weeks of agent time, and the second number\nis the one that dominates the budget.","tradeoffs":["Demonstrations are precise but expensive and only teach imitation; comparisons are cheaper and express quality, but a poorly designed rubric teaches length and confidence instead of correctness.","Offline methods (SFT, DPO) are simple and reproducible, while on-policy RL keeps learning as the model changes, at the price of a sampling loop, more hyperparameters and harder debugging.","A strong KL leash preserves capabilities and prevents reward hacking but caps how far behaviour can move; loosen it when the target behaviour is far from the SFT model and evaluation can catch drift.","Training safety and refusals in makes the model safer by default but raises over-refusal on benign edge cases; the right balance depends on the deployment's audience and whether external guardrails exist."],"pitfalls":["Running preference training on a model that has not been SFT'd into the target format; the symptom is that both responses in every pair are off-distribution and the loss moves without the behaviour improving.","Reusing stale comparison data across rounds; the pairs describe an old model's errors, so win rates on the training distribution rise while real-user complaints do not change.","Measuring only the preference metric; a model can win more pairwise comparisons while losing points on reasoning or coding benchmarks, which is catastrophic forgetting hiding behind a good-looking number.","Optimising a reward that is easy to compute rather than the one that matters; the symptom is outputs that satisfy the schema or the judge yet are useless to the person reading them."],"when":"You post-train when prompting and retrieval cannot give you the behaviour: a consistent house format\nthat must hold on every response, a refusal or escalation policy that must be reliable, a domain style\nthat prompting keeps drifting from, or a multi-step skill that needs reward-driven practice. Start with\nSFT because it is cheap and diagnostic; add preference optimisation when the failure is \"fluent but\nworse than it should be\"; add verifiable-reward RL only when you can write a trustworthy reward\nfunction. Measure before and after every stage with both task metrics and a general capability\nsuite. In interviews, be ready to name the three stages, say what signal each consumes, and explain why\nthe ordering is not arbitrary.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the SFT and DPO pipelines, writes the reward functions and chat templates, and owns the before/after evaluation harness that gates each round.","architect":"Decides whether the product needs post-training at all versus prompting and RAG, and sets the data, evaluation and safety gates a post-trained model must pass to ship.","fde":"Turns a customer's ticket history and reviewer time into demonstrations and comparisons, and manages the expectation that the data effort, not the GPUs, is the schedule risk."},"check":{"q":"A colleague proposes skipping SFT and running DPO directly on the base model using the\npreference pairs you already have, to save a stage. What goes wrong, and when could it work?","a":"DPO adjusts the relative log-probabilities of chosen versus rejected responses against a\nreference, and it assumes the policy already assigns reasonable probability to responses in that\nformat. A base model puts almost no mass on chat-formatted assistant turns, so the pairs are far\noff-distribution: the loss can fall by making both responses even less likely in ways that generalise\npoorly, and the model still does not reliably answer in the format. It could work if the pairs are in\nplain completion format the base model already produces, or if the \"base\" is actually an\ninstruction-tuned checkpoint. In practice, a short SFT on the chosen responses first is cheap and makes\nthe DPO stage stable, so the saving is not worth it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does post-training use comparisons between responses rather than only more demonstrations?","options":["Comparisons are the only data format PPO can consume","For most prompts there is no single correct answer, and ranking captures quality that imitation cannot express","Demonstrations cause catastrophic forgetting while comparisons do not","Comparisons require no human labour"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A demonstration says \"produce this\"; it cannot say \"this is better than that\". Preference data encodes the quality dimension directly, and people find ranking easier and cheaper than authoring ideal answers."},{"q":"What role does the KL penalty against a reference model play in RLHF, DPO and GRPO?","options":["It speeds up convergence by reducing gradient variance","It replaces the need for a separate evaluation set","It limits how far the policy can drift, preventing degenerate outputs that score well on the reward but read badly","It ensures the model forgets the base model's unsafe behaviours"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An unconstrained optimiser will exploit any gap between the reward and true quality. Penalising divergence from the previous checkpoint keeps the policy in the region where the reward model or preference data is trustworthy and preserves learned capabilities."},{"q":"In the support-assistant example, why did the JSON-validity problem need verifiable-reward RL rather than more SFT data?","options":["SFT cannot train on structured output","The rare malformed cases were drowned out in imitation data, while a programmatic reward targets exactly the failure","DPO cannot be run after SFT","RL is always cheaper than collecting demonstrations"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Imitation learning improves the average behaviour; a 9% failure rate persists because nothing in the loss singles it out. A reward computed by a schema check pushes directly on the failure, and sampling many attempts finds the behaviour that fixes it."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/supervised-fine-tuning","sources":["Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Zhou et al., 2023 — LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment","Official docs: Hugging Face TRL"],"why":"A pre-trained base model has the knowledge but not the behaviour. Prompting can coax behaviour out of\nit, but the prompt has to be re-sent every call, it drifts on edge cases, and it cannot install anything\nthe base does not already do as an obvious pattern. If every response must open with a one-line\nsummary, cite a source id in a fixed format and never exceed 120 words, a prompt gets you most of the\nway most of the time; a fine-tune gets you there every time with a shorter prompt.\n\nThe naive way to fine-tune is to keep pre-training on your domain text. That teaches the model the\n*distribution* of your documents, not how to answer about them: a model continued-pre-trained on\nsupport tickets will produce more support tickets. What you want is a conditional: given this prompt,\nproduce this response. SFT frames the data as (prompt, response) pairs and trains the model to produce\nthe response conditioned on the prompt, which is why the loss is masked so that the prompt is context\nrather than target.\n\nThe other naive alternative is \"train on a lot of mediocre examples\". LIMA showed that about a thousand\ncarefully written demonstrations can be enough to turn a strong base model into a capable assistant,\nand that the quality and diversity of the demonstrations matter more than their count. The base model\nalready has the skills; SFT is choosing which of its behaviours to surface, and noisy data surfaces\nnoisy behaviour.","how":"### Data format and the chat template\nEach example is a conversation: an optional system message, one or more user turns, and the assistant\nturns you want the model to produce. A chat template renders this into a single token sequence with\nspecial tokens marking each role, for example `<|im_start|>assistant ... <|im_end|>`. The template is\npart of the model's contract: train with one template and serve with another and the model will not\nrecognise where it is supposed to speak.\n\n### Loss masking\nThe sequence is scored with next-token cross-entropy exactly as in pre-training, but the labels for\nprompt and system tokens are set to an ignore value (−100 in PyTorch), so gradients flow only from the\nassistant tokens. This matters *because* the model should not spend capacity learning to predict user\nquestions, and because in multi-turn data the user turns are often noisy or adversarial. In multi-turn\nconversations every assistant turn is a target and every earlier turn is context.\n\n### Full fine-tuning versus parameter-efficient\nFull SFT updates every weight. With AdamW in mixed precision that costs about 16 bytes per parameter\nfor weights, gradients and optimiser state, so a 7B model needs over 100 GB before activations.\nParameter-efficient methods (LoRA, QLoRA) freeze the base and train small adapters, fitting on a single\nGPU at some cost in how far behaviour can move. Full fine-tuning is preferred when the target behaviour\nis far from the base or the data is large; adapters when you need many variants or a small budget.\n\n### Hyperparameters that actually matter\n- **Learning rate.** Far smaller than pre-training: roughly 1e-5 to 2e-5 for full fine-tuning of a\n  7B-class model, around ten times higher for LoRA adapters. Too high and the model forgets general\n  capability within a few hundred steps.\n- **Epochs.** Usually one to three. Demonstration sets are small, so the model can memorise them; a\n  rising validation loss after epoch two is the usual signal to stop.\n- **Sequence packing.** Short examples are concatenated to fill the context window so no compute is\n  wasted on padding. Packing must either reset position ids and mask attention across example\n  boundaries or accept a small cross-contamination; naive packing lets one example attend to its\n  neighbour.\n- **Mixing in general data.** A slice of broad instruction data alongside the domain examples keeps the\n  model from losing general skills, the SFT form of catastrophic forgetting.\n\n### What SFT can and cannot teach\nSFT is excellent at format, register, length, persona and well-specified procedures, and at domain\nterminology. It is weak at anything where the demonstration is only one of many acceptable answers:\nit cannot tell the model that a confident, fluent, wrong answer is worse than a hedged right one,\nbecause it never sees a worse answer. It can also teach hallucination: if demonstrations contain facts\nthe base model does not know, the model learns to produce facts in that style regardless of whether it\nknows them. That is why demonstrations should be consistent with the base model's knowledge or\ngrounded in context provided in the prompt.","example":"The core of an SFT step: render the conversation with the chat template, mask the prompt tokens, and\ncompute cross-entropy on the assistant tokens only. Trainers such as TRL's `SFTTrainer` wrap exactly\nthis with batching, packing and checkpointing.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16).cuda()\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-5, weight_decay=0.0)\n\nexample = [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a support assistant. Answer in at most two sentences.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"How do I rotate my API key?\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Open Settings > API keys, click Rotate next to the key, and \"\n                                     \"update any clients within 24 hours; the old key expires then.\"},\n]\nprompt = tok.apply_chat_template(example[:-1], tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)\nfull = tok.apply_chat_template(example, tokenize=False)\nids = tok(full, return_tensors=\"pt\").input_ids.cuda()            # [1, T]\nn_prompt = len(tok(prompt).input_ids)\n\nlabels = ids.clone()\nlabels[:, :n_prompt] = -100                                      # prompt tokens are context, not targets\nlogits = model(input_ids=ids).logits\nloss = F.cross_entropy(logits[:, :-1].reshape(-1, logits.size(-1)).float(),\n                       labels[:, 1:].reshape(-1), ignore_index=-100)\nloss.backward(); torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), 1.0)\nopt.step(); opt.zero_grad()\nprint(f\"trained on {(labels != -100).sum().item()} of {ids.numel()} tokens, loss {loss.item():.3f}\")\n```\n\nWith a few thousand such examples, two epochs and a cosine schedule, the model learns the two-sentence\nsupport voice; the masked count printed on each batch is the quickest check that the template and\nmasking line up.","tradeoffs":["Demonstrations give precise control over format and style, but authoring them is the most expensive data per example in post-training, which flips in your favour only when a strong model can draft them for human review.","Full fine-tuning moves behaviour furthest and handles large datasets, while LoRA fits one GPU and gives swappable adapters; the adapter route loses when the target distribution is far from the base.","More epochs fit the demonstrations better but overfit sooner, so the gain reverses once validation loss turns upward, typically between epochs two and three on small sets.","Training only on assistant tokens focuses capacity on the behaviour you want, but with very short responses the number of supervised tokens per example is tiny, so you need more examples or longer responses to get signal."],"pitfalls":["Serving with a different chat template from the one used in training; the symptom is a model that ignores the system prompt, answers as the user, or never emits the end-of-turn token.","Forgetting to mask the prompt; the symptom is a suspiciously low training loss (the model learns to predict easy prompt text) and a model that has learned to generate user questions.","Fine-tuning on facts the base model does not know; the symptom is confident hallucination in the same style on unrelated questions, because the model learned \"state facts fluently\" rather than the facts.","Skipping a general-data mix; the symptom is a model that is excellent on the domain evaluation and measurably worse on general reasoning or coding benchmarks after a single round.","Judging success by training loss; a low loss on memorised examples says nothing about generalisation, so hold out prompts by topic, not just by row."],"when":"Reach for SFT when the behaviour you want is consistent and describable by examples: a fixed output\nschema, a tone, a domain vocabulary, a procedure, or a tool-calling convention that prompting keeps\ngetting slightly wrong. Do not reach for it to inject knowledge that changes weekly; retrieval does that\nbetter and stays auditable. Before fine-tuning, measure how far prompting plus a few-shot examples\ngets you, because that is the baseline the fine-tune must beat net of its maintenance cost. During the\nwork, track validation loss, a held-out task metric and a general capability benchmark together. In\ninterviews, expect to explain loss masking, why the learning rate is tiny, and why SFT alone cannot fix\n\"fluent but wrong\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the data pipeline that renders conversations through the chat template, masks prompts, packs sequences and tracks the supervised-token count per batch as a sanity check.","architect":"Decides whether a fine-tuned model is worth owning versus a prompt, including who re-trains it when the base model or the format changes.","fde":"Turns a customer's best human responses into demonstrations, negotiates the review process that keeps them consistent, and sets expectations that a few thousand good examples beat a hundred thousand scraped ones."},"check":{"q":"After SFT on 20,000 customer-service transcripts, the model's training loss is very low but it\nfrequently makes up product features that do not exist, and it sometimes continues the conversation by\nwriting the customer's next message. What went wrong, and how would you fix each symptom?","a":"Two separate bugs. Writing the customer's next turn means the loss was not masked to assistant\ntokens, so the model learned to generate user turns too; fix by setting prompt and user labels to the\nignore index and checking the supervised-token count per batch. Invented features point to data\ncontent: transcripts contain facts the base model never saw, so SFT taught it to assert product facts\nfluently without grounding. Fix by grounding responses in retrieved documentation placed in the prompt\nso the demonstrations show \"answer from the provided context\", filtering transcripts with unverifiable\nclaims, and following up with preference pairs that penalise invented details. The low training loss is\nconsistent with both problems, which is why a held-out task metric, not loss, should gate the release."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are the prompt tokens masked out of the SFT loss?","options":["To reduce the memory used by the backward pass","So the model learns to produce the response conditioned on the prompt rather than to predict the prompt itself","Because prompt tokens are already encoded in the KV cache","To prevent the model from seeing the system prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The prompt is context, not a target. Training on it wastes capacity on predicting user text and, in multi-turn data, teaches the model to generate user turns, which shows up as the model continuing the conversation on the user's behalf."},{"q":"Which learning rate is a sensible starting point for full fine-tuning a 7B model with SFT?","options":["3e-4, the usual pre-training peak","1e-2, because the dataset is small and needs fast learning","About 1e-5 to 2e-5, far below pre-training","1e-7, to avoid any change to the base weights"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fine-tuning starts from a good solution and must not destroy it. Learning rates near pre-training levels cause rapid forgetting of general capability; rates that are too low fail to move the behaviour in the few thousand steps available."},{"q":"A team wants the model to stop giving confident wrong answers and prefer hedged correct ones. Why is SFT the wrong tool on its own?","options":["SFT cannot change the tone of responses","SFT requires more data than preference methods","SFT only ever sees the demonstrated answer, so it cannot learn that one fluent answer is worse than another","Hedging requires a larger context window"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Imitation learning has no notion of a worse alternative. Expressing \"A is better than B\" needs comparison data consumed by RLHF or DPO; SFT can set the format, but the quality ranking comes from preference optimisation."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/instruction-tuning","sources":["Wei et al., 2021 — Finetuned Language Models Are Zero-Shot Learners","Sanh et al., 2021 — Multitask Prompted Training Enables Zero-Shot Task Generalization","Wang et al., 2022 — Self-Instruct: Aligning Language Models with Self-Generated Instructions","Chung et al., 2022 — Scaling Instruction-Finetuned Language Models"],"why":"A base model can do many tasks if you show it a few examples in the prompt, because few-shot patterns\nare common in its training data. But users do not write few-shot prompts. They write \"summarise this\nin two lines\" or \"translate to French, formal register\", and a base model given that instruction may\necho it, answer something adjacent, or continue the document. The capability exists; the *interface*\ndoes not.\n\nThe first attempt at fixing this was fine-tuning on one task at a time: a summarisation model, a\ntranslation model. Each worked on its task and not at all on the next one, and the number of tasks\nusers invent is unbounded. The question that produced instruction tuning was: if we fine-tune on\n*dozens* of tasks, each phrased as an instruction, does the model learn to follow instructions for\ntasks it has never seen? FLAN and T0 showed that it does, and that the effect grows with the number of\ndistinct tasks and the size of the model. The model is not learning summarisation or translation; it\nis learning the mapping from instruction text to behaviour, which transfers.\n\nThe naive alternative, a single huge dataset for one task phrased one way, fails because the model\noverfits to that phrasing and that task, and because there is nothing for it to generalise across.\nInstruction tuning works precisely because the data is wide and shallow.","how":"### The data unit\nEach example is an instruction, an optional input, and an output: \"Classify the sentiment of the\nfollowing review. Review: ... Answer: positive\". Classic academic datasets are converted into this\nform by writing *templates*: a sentiment dataset becomes instruction-tuning data through several\nphrasings (\"Is this review positive or negative?\", \"What is the sentiment of this text?\"), and often\nthrough inversion (\"Write a negative review about a laptop\"). Multiple templates per task matter\nbecause the model must not key on the exact wording; it has to learn the intent.\n\n### Why diversity beats depth\nExperiments across the FLAN line found that adding more *tasks* improves held-out performance more\nthan adding more examples of the same tasks, and that gains saturate quickly per task. The mechanism\nis that each task teaches the model one more point in \"instruction space\", and generalisation is\ninterpolation between those points. As a rule of thumb from those ablations, a mixture of hundreds of tasks\ncapped at a few thousand examples each generalises better than a handful of tasks with a hundred\nthousand each. The same logic says that mixtures should be *balanced*:\nsample tasks roughly in proportion to their size but cap the large ones, so a giant dataset does not\ndominate the gradient.\n\n### Where the instructions come from\n- **Converted benchmarks.** Public NLP datasets re-templated; cheap, broad, but academic in style.\n- **Human-written.** Annotators write realistic requests and answers; expensive, the most useful per\n  example, and the only way to get genuine user-style prompts.\n- **Model-generated (Self-Instruct).** Start from a seed set of hand-written tasks, prompt a strong\n  model to invent new instructions and answer them, filter for quality and diversity, repeat. This is\n  how most open instruction datasets scaled: a few hundred seeds become tens of thousands of examples.\n  The risk is that the student inherits the teacher's errors and style, and that generated instructions\n  cluster around what the teacher finds easy.\n\n### Reasoning and format data\nIncluding chain-of-thought examples in the mixture preserves and strengthens multi-step reasoning;\ninstruction mixtures without any reasoning data were found to *hurt* reasoning benchmarks, because the\nmodel learns to answer tersely. The same holds for anything you want to survive the tuning: code,\nlong-form answers, refusals, tool calls. If it is not in the mixture, the model drifts away from it.\n\n### How it relates to SFT and chat tuning\nMechanically, instruction tuning *is* supervised fine-tuning: next-token loss on the output tokens\nwith the instruction masked. The difference is intent and data: SFT as a term covers any\ndemonstration-based fine-tuning, including narrow single-task adaptation, while instruction tuning\nspecifically targets broad instruction following. Modern chat models are instruction-tuned with\nmulti-turn conversational data through a chat template; the single-turn instruction/input/output\nformat is the ancestor of that.\n\n### Scale dependence\nThe original FLAN work found the benefit of instruction tuning appeared only above a certain model\nsize, with small models actually getting worse on held-out tasks. The usual reading is that small\nmodels lack the spare capacity to learn both the tasks and the meta-skill, so they overfit the seen\ntasks. Later work with better mixtures narrowed this gap, but the lesson stands: instruction tuning\nsurfaces latent ability, it does not create it.","example":"A miniature instruction mixture built from two task families with several templates each, balanced\nwith a per-task cap. This is the shape of what FLAN-style pipelines do at a scale of hundreds of tasks.\n\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(0)\n\nsentiment = [(\"The battery lasts two days, love it.\", \"positive\"),\n             (\"Screen cracked in a week.\", \"negative\")]\nsummar = [(\"Q3 revenue rose 12% on cloud demand while hardware fell 3%.\",\n           \"Cloud growth drove a 12% Q3 revenue rise despite weaker hardware.\")]\n\ntemplates = {\n    \"sentiment\": [\"Classify the sentiment of this review as positive or negative.\\nReview: {x}\",\n                  \"Is the following text positive or negative?\\n{x}\",\n                  \"What sentiment does this review express?\\n{x}\"],\n    \"summarize\": [\"Summarise in one sentence:\\n{x}\",\n                  \"Give a one-line summary of the text below.\\n{x}\"],\n}\nraw = {\"sentiment\": sentiment, \"summarize\": summar}\n\ndef build(cap=1000):\n    out = []\n    for task, rows in raw.items():\n        rows = rows[:cap]                         # cap large tasks so no task dominates\n        for x, y in rows:\n            for t in random.sample(templates[task], k=min(2, len(templates[task]))):\n                out.append({\"instruction\": t.format(x=x), \"output\": y, \"task\": task})\n    random.shuffle(out)\n    return out\n\ndata = build()\nfor ex in data[:3]:\n    print(ex[\"task\"], \"|\", ex[\"instruction\"].split(\"\\n\")[0], \"->\", ex[\"output\"])\n# Each example is then rendered through the chat template and trained with the SFT loss\n# (instruction masked, output supervised). Hold out whole tasks, not rows, to measure generalisation.\n```\n\nHolding out entire tasks is the evaluation that matters: if the model handles a task family it never\nsaw in training, the instruction-following skill transferred; if it only handles seen tasks, you have a\nmulti-task model, not an instruction-following one.","tradeoffs":["Broad, shallow mixtures generalise to unseen instructions, while narrow deep ones excel at the seen task; when you only ever need one task, the narrow fine-tune wins on that task.","Model-generated instructions scale cheaply and widen coverage, but import the teacher's errors and style, so the saving reverses if the teacher is weaker than your target or your domain is unusual.","Templating public benchmarks gives thousands of tasks for free, yet academic phrasings differ from real user requests, so a slice of genuine human-written prompts is worth more per example.","Including reasoning and long-form data keeps those abilities but lengthens average outputs and training cost; omit them only when the deployment never needs them."],"pitfalls":["Using one template per task; the symptom is a model that follows training phrasings exactly and fails when a user words the same request differently.","Letting one huge dataset dominate the mixture; the symptom is a model that is excellent at that task and has quietly regressed on everything else, visible only if you evaluate per task family.","Evaluating on held-out rows of seen tasks and calling it generalisation; the number looks great and says nothing about unseen instructions, which is what users send.","Training on instruction data with no chain-of-thought or long answers; the symptom is terse replies and a drop on multi-step reasoning benchmarks after tuning.","Treating \"instruction tuned\" as \"aligned\"; an instruction-tuned model follows harmful instructions as readily as benign ones until preference training and safety data add a refusal policy."],"when":"Instruction tuning is the right tool when you need a model that follows a wide range of requests it has\nnot seen verbatim: a general assistant, an internal copilot that fields open-ended tasks, or a base for\nlater preference training. It is overkill when the product has one fixed task, where a narrow SFT on\nthat task is simpler and better. In practice most teams do not build the mixture from scratch; they\nstart from an already instruction-tuned open model and add their own tasks to a general mixture to\nkeep breadth. Measure with held-out *task families* and a general benchmark suite. In interviews, be\nready to explain why diversity beats depth, how Self-Instruct bootstraps data, and how instruction\ntuning differs from both plain SFT and preference optimisation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the template and mixing pipeline, caps task sizes, holds out task families, and verifies the chat template renders every example the way serving will.","architect":"Chooses between adopting an instruction-tuned open model and extending it with domain tasks versus narrow tuning, based on how open-ended the product's requests really are.","fde":"Collects the real phrasings a customer's users send and converts them into instruction data, because academic templates rarely match how a procurement analyst actually asks."},"check":{"q":"Your instruction-tuned model scores well on held-out examples from all 40 training tasks, but\nusers complain it misunderstands simple requests. What is the likely gap in your evaluation and data,\nand what would you change?","a":"Held-out rows from seen tasks only measure multi-task performance; they never test whether the\nmodel generalises to new instructions, which is what users send. The likely causes are too few\ndistinct tasks and too few phrasings per task, so the model keys on training wording. I would first\nre-evaluate on entirely held-out task families and on a sample of real user requests to confirm the\ngap, then widen the mixture: add tasks, write several templates per task including inverted forms,\nand add a slice of genuine user-style instructions, possibly bootstrapped with Self-Instruct and\nfiltered by humans. I would also check that the mixture is balanced, since one large task can crowd out\nthe generalisation signal, and confirm the model is large enough to benefit, because small models\ntend to overfit seen tasks."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding more distinct tasks help instruction tuning more than adding more examples of existing tasks?","options":["Larger datasets cause overfitting regardless of task count","Each new task adds a point in instruction space, and generalisation comes from covering that space, not from depth on one task","More tasks reduce the learning rate needed","Examples of the same task are usually duplicates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The target skill is \"map an instruction to a behaviour\" in general. Depth on one task refines that task; breadth across tasks teaches the mapping itself, which is what transfers to unseen instructions."},{"q":"What is the main risk of building an instruction dataset with Self-Instruct from a strong teacher model?","options":["The resulting data cannot be used with a chat template","It produces too few examples to be useful","The student inherits the teacher's mistakes and style, and generated tasks cluster around what the teacher finds easy","The loss must be computed on instruction tokens as well"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bootstrapping scales data cheaply, but every error and bias in the teacher's answers becomes a training target, and diversity narrows toward the teacher's comfort zone unless filtering and human-written seeds counteract it."},{"q":"After instruction tuning, a model answers multi-step maths questions worse than the base model did with few-shot prompts. What most likely happened?","options":["The learning rate was too low to change behaviour","The mixture contained no chain-of-thought or long-form data, so the model learned to answer tersely","Instruction tuning always reduces reasoning ability","The chat template removed the ability to use few-shot prompts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Whatever is absent from the mixture drifts away. Without reasoning traces in the data the model learns that outputs are short answers, which suppresses the step-by-step behaviour the base model would show when prompted."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/rlhf","sources":["Christiano et al., 2017 — Deep Reinforcement Learning from Human Preferences","Stiennon et al., 2020 — Learning to Summarize from Human Feedback","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Gao et al., 2022 — Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization"],"why":"After SFT a model answers in the right format, but for most prompts the demonstration it imitated is\none of many acceptable answers, and the model has no way to know that a confident wrong answer is worse\nthan a careful right one. Writing a perfect demonstration for every prompt is slow, and annotators'\nown answers are often worse than what the model can already produce. Asking \"which of these two is\nbetter?\" is a far cheaper and more reliable judgement: people agree with each other on comparisons much\nmore than they agree on what the ideal response is.\n\nThe problem is that a comparison is not a training target for next-token prediction. There is no\ntoken sequence to maximise the likelihood of; there is only a scalar notion of goodness. That is the\nshape of a reinforcement learning problem: the model is a *policy* that emits a response (an action\nsequence), the environment returns a *reward*, and the objective is expected reward. Christiano et al.\nshowed the recipe in 2017 for control tasks: fit a reward model to human comparisons, optimise the\npolicy against it, and keep collecting comparisons on the new policy's behaviour. Stiennon et al. and\nthen InstructGPT applied it to language, and the result was the jump from \"completes text\" to \"helps\".\n\nThe naive alternative of optimising directly against human ratings is hopeless at RL's data appetite:\nPPO scores every sampled response, which over a run is hundreds of thousands of samples or more,\nfar beyond what raters could label. The reward model is the amortisation trick: tens of thousands of\nhuman comparisons train a model that can score every sample for free. The price is that the\nreward model is wrong in places, and the policy will find those places.","how":"### Step 1: collect comparisons\nPrompts are sampled from the target distribution. For each, the current policy generates two or more\nresponses; raters pick the better one, or rank several. Rankings are decomposed into pairs. Rater\nguidelines, calibration sessions and agreement measurement are a large part of the engineering: a\nreward model trained on inconsistent labels learns noise.\n\n### Step 2: train the reward model\nThe reward model r_φ(x, y) is often the SFT model (or a smaller sibling, as in InstructGPT) with its\noutput head replaced by a scalar head, so it reads prompt and response and emits one number. It is trained with the Bradley–Terry pairwise\nloss, which says the probability that y_w beats y_l is the sigmoid of their score difference:\n\n```\nL_RM(φ) = − E_(x, y_w, y_l) [ log σ( r_φ(x, y_w) − r_φ(x, y_l) ) ]\n```\n\nOnly differences matter, so the reward scale is arbitrary; implementations often normalise it. The loss\nis chosen because it directly models the data you have (comparisons) rather than absolute scores\nnobody gave you.\n\n### Step 3: optimise the policy with PPO\nThe objective is expected reward with a KL penalty to the reference policy π_ref (the SFT model):\n\n```\nJ(θ) = E_x E_y~π_θ [ r_φ(x, y) − β · KL( π_θ(·|x) ‖ π_ref(·|x) ) ]\n```\n\nIn practice the KL term is applied per token as a shaped reward (−β·[log π_θ(y_t|·) − log π_ref(y_t|·)])\nand the reward model's score is added at the final token. PPO then runs the standard loop: sample\nresponses from the current policy, compute rewards, estimate advantages with a learned *value model*\nand generalised advantage estimation, and update the policy with the clipped surrogate objective\n`min(ρ·A, clip(ρ, 1−ε, 1+ε)·A)` where ρ is the ratio of new to old token probabilities. The clip is\nthere because policy-gradient steps on sampled text are high-variance; it prevents any one batch from\nmoving the policy too far. InstructGPT additionally mixed in pre-training gradients (\"PPO-ptx\") to\nlimit the capability loss they called the alignment tax.\n\n### Why the KL penalty is not optional\nThe reward model is accurate near the data it was trained on and increasingly wrong far from it. An\nunconstrained policy climbs the reward surface into regions where the reward model's score is high\nand the real quality is low: longer and longer answers, flattery, repeated keywords, confident\nhedging, or outright gibberish that happens to score well. Gao et al. measured this\n*overoptimisation*: as the policy moves further from the reference (measured in KL), the proxy reward\nkeeps rising while the true reward peaks and then falls. β sets how far you let it go; it is tuned\nagainst held-out human evaluation, not against the reward model.\n\n### What is running during training\nFour networks are involved: the policy (trained), the reference (frozen copy for the KL), the reward\nmodel (frozen) and the value model (trained). Memory and orchestration are the practical burden: for a\nlarge policy this is several copies of the weights plus optimiser state for two of them, plus a\ngeneration loop that must be fast, because sampling dominates wall-clock time. This cost is the main\nreason DPO and GRPO exist.\n\n### Iteration and rejection sampling\nBecause the reward model is only trusted near its training data, production recipes iterate: after a\nround of PPO, sample from the new policy, collect new comparisons, retrain the reward model, repeat.\nA cheaper cousin is rejection sampling fine-tuning: sample k responses, keep the top-scored by the\nreward model, SFT on those. It uses the same reward model with no RL machinery and is often the first\nround before PPO.","example":"The two numerical pieces that make RLHF concrete: the reward model's pairwise loss and the per-token\nshaped reward that PPO optimises. Shapes are for one prompt with two candidate responses.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\n\n# --- Reward model training step: Bradley-Terry loss on one (chosen, rejected) pair ---\ndef rm_loss(score_chosen, score_rejected):\n    # score_* : [batch] scalars from the reward head at the final token\n    return -F.logsigmoid(score_chosen - score_rejected).mean()\n\ns_w, s_l = torch.tensor([1.3]), torch.tensor([0.4])\nprint(\"RM loss:\", rm_loss(s_w, s_l).item())          # 0.341 ; falls as the gap widens\n\n# --- PPO reward shaping for one sampled response of T tokens ---\nbeta = 0.05\nlogp_policy = torch.tensor([-2.1, -0.4, -1.7, -0.9, -0.3])   # log pi_theta(y_t | x, y_<t)\nlogp_ref    = torch.tensor([-2.0, -0.6, -1.5, -0.9, -2.1])   # log pi_ref(y_t | x, y_<t)\nrm_score    = 0.8                                            # r_phi(x, y) for the whole response\n\nper_token_kl = logp_policy - logp_ref                        # positive where policy drifted up\nrewards = -beta * per_token_kl                               # KL penalty at every token ...\nrewards[-1] += rm_score                                      # ... plus the RM score at the end\nprint(\"shaped rewards:\", rewards.tolist())                   # [0.005, -0.01, 0.01, 0.0, 0.71]\nprint(\"sequence KL estimate:\", per_token_kl.sum().item())    # 1.7 ; watched as a drift gauge\n# PPO then computes advantages from these rewards with a value model and applies the clipped\n# surrogate objective to the policy's token log-probs. If the KL estimate climbs while held-out\n# human win-rate stalls, beta is too low: the policy is optimising the reward model's mistakes.\n```\n\nThe last token above carries a large KL penalty: its log-ratio is +1.8, meaning the policy puts about\nsix times (e^1.8) more mass on it than the reference did, so −β·1.8 ≈ −0.09 is subtracted from the\n0.8 reward model score at that position. A few tokens where the policy has drifted far up is exactly\nthe pattern of a policy starting to exploit the reward model, and why per-token KL, not just the\nfinal score, is logged during training. Note that this single-sample log-ratio can be negative on\nindividual tokens (tokens 1 and 3 above, where the policy moved down); only its sum or expectation\nover a sequence estimates the KL, which is why the sequence-level number is the one watched.","tradeoffs":["A learned reward model lets you optimise against millions of samples from tens of thousands of human labels, but it is wrong off-distribution, so the gain reverses once the policy drifts past the data it was trained on.","On-policy PPO adapts to the policy as it improves and can reach behaviours no demonstration contains, at the cost of four models in memory, a sampling loop and fragile hyperparameters; DPO wins when the dataset is fixed and the budget is small.","A low KL coefficient allows bigger behaviour changes and faster reward gains, while a high one preserves capability; the right value flips with how trustworthy the reward model is far from its data.","Separate reward models for helpfulness and safety allow explicit trade-offs between them, but need a combination rule and twice the comparison data."],"pitfalls":["Trusting the reward model's score as the metric of success; the symptom is rising proxy reward with flat or falling human win rate, the overoptimisation signature.","Length and sycophancy bias in comparisons; raters prefer longer, more agreeable answers, so the policy learns verbosity and flattery, visible as answer length creeping up round over round.","Noisy or inconsistent labels; low inter-annotator agreement produces a reward model that cannot separate good from bad, and PPO then optimises noise.","Training the reward model on comparisons from an old policy and reusing it after the policy has moved; the symptom is a reward model that is confidently wrong on the new policy's typical outputs.","Treating PPO's hyperparameters as defaults; a too-large clip range or learning rate shows up as sudden KL spikes and a collapse into repetitive text within a few hundred steps."],"when":"Reach for RLHF-style training when demonstrations cannot express what you want, you can collect\nreliable comparisons on your own prompt distribution, and you have the engineering capacity to run and\nmonitor an RL loop. For most application teams the practical path is DPO on the same comparison data,\nor rejection sampling with a reward model, with full PPO reserved for cases where on-policy iteration\nclearly pays. Whatever variant you run, measure with human or strong-judge win rates on held-out\nprompts, track KL from the reference, and run a general capability suite to catch the alignment tax.\nIn interviews, be ready to draw the three stages, write the Bradley–Terry loss, explain why the KL\npenalty exists, and describe reward overoptimisation and how you would detect it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements comparison collection, trains and validates the reward model on held-out pairs, and watches KL, reward and win-rate curves together during PPO.","architect":"Decides whether on-policy RL is justified over DPO or rejection sampling given the data, compute and evaluation budget, and owns the safety-versus-helpfulness reward policy.","fde":"Runs the rater calibration with a customer's domain experts, measures agreement, and explains why the reward model, not the GPUs, is the component that decides what the model becomes."},"check":{"q":"During PPO, the reward model's average score on sampled responses rises steadily for a thousand\nsteps, but a weekly human evaluation shows win rate against the SFT model peaked at step 300 and has\nsince fallen. What is happening, how do you confirm it, and what do you change?","a":"This is reward overoptimisation: the policy has moved into regions where the reward model\noverestimates quality, so the proxy rises while real quality falls. I would confirm by plotting the KL\nfrom the reference over steps and checking that the human win rate peaks at a particular KL and\ndeclines beyond it, and by inspecting samples for the usual exploits, such as growing length,\nflattery, or repeated phrasing. Remedies: raise the KL coefficient or stop at the KL where human\nevaluation peaked; collect fresh comparisons on the current policy's outputs and retrain the reward\nmodel so it is accurate where the policy now lives; consider a reward ensemble or a length penalty if\nthe exploit is identifiable. The deeper fix is institutional: never gate releases on the reward model's\nown score."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does RLHF train a reward model instead of optimising the policy directly on human ratings?","options":["Human ratings are not differentiable, but a neural reward model is","RL scores far more sampled responses than raters could label; the reward model amortises tens of thousands of human comparisons over all of them","PPO can only consume scalar rewards from a network of the same size as the policy","Humans cannot compare responses reliably"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Policy-gradient methods are sample-hungry. A reward model turns a modest number of human comparisons into a function that can score every sampled response for free, which is the only way the RL loop is affordable. Differentiability is irrelevant: PPO does not backpropagate through the reward."},{"q":"What does the Bradley-Terry loss −log σ(r(x,y_w) − r(x,y_l)) assume about the data?","options":["That each response has an absolute quality score from 0 to 1","That rewards are normally distributed across prompts","That the probability one response beats another is a sigmoid of their score difference, so only differences matter","That the chosen response is always correct"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The loss models comparisons, not absolute scores, which matches the data raters actually produce. Because only differences enter, the reward scale is arbitrary and implementations normalise it."},{"q":"A team sets the KL coefficient β to zero to \"let the model improve freely\". What is the most likely result?","options":["Training diverges immediately because the gradients explode","The model stops changing, because there is no penalty to push against","Proxy reward rises while real quality degrades into verbose, repetitive or sycophantic outputs that exploit the reward model","The reward model is retrained automatically to compensate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without the leash the policy climbs the reward model's surface into regions far from its training data where its scores are unreliable. The classic exploits are length, flattery and degenerate text that happen to score well."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/dpo","sources":["Rafailov et al., 2023 — Direct Preference Optimization: Your Language Model is Secretly a Reward Model","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback","Official docs: Hugging Face TRL"],"why":"RLHF with PPO delivers, but the machinery is heavy. Four models have to be resident, two of them\ntraining; a generation loop dominates wall-clock time; PPO's hyperparameters (clip range, value loss\nweight, KL coefficient, learning rate schedule) interact in ways that take experienced teams weeks to\nstabilise; and the reward model is a separate artefact to train, validate and keep current. For a team\nwith ten thousand preference pairs and one node of GPUs, this is a mismatch between the size of the\nproblem and the size of the tool.\n\nThe obvious shortcut, fine-tuning on the chosen responses and ignoring the rejected ones, throws away\nhalf the signal: the model learns what good looks like but nothing about what bad looks like, and it\ncannot push probability *away* from the rejected answers. The next obvious shortcut, a contrastive loss\nthat raises chosen log-probability and lowers rejected log-probability, has no leash: the model can\nsatisfy it by driving the rejected sequence to zero probability, trashing everything nearby.\n\nDPO's insight is that the leash and the comparison can be combined analytically. The RLHF objective,\nmaximise reward minus β times KL from the reference, has an exact optimum, and inverting it expresses\nthe reward in terms of the policy and reference log-probabilities. Substituting that expression into\nthe Bradley–Terry loss gives a loss on the policy alone that is the same optimisation problem RLHF\nsolves, with no reward model and no sampling. The leash is still there; it is just inside the formula.","how":"### The derivation in four lines\n1. The KL-regularised objective `max_π E_y~π[r(x,y)] − β·KL(π ‖ π_ref)` is maximised by\n   `π*(y|x) = π_ref(y|x)·exp(r(x,y)/β) / Z(x)`, where Z(x) normalises over all responses.\n2. Solve for the reward: `r(x,y) = β·log(π*(y|x)/π_ref(y|x)) + β·log Z(x)`.\n3. Put that into the Bradley–Terry probability that y_w beats y_l. The `β·log Z(x)` term is the same for\n   both responses to the same prompt, so it cancels in the difference.\n4. The resulting loss over the policy θ is:\n\n```\nL_DPO(θ) = − E [ log σ( β·log π_θ(y_w|x)/π_ref(y_w|x)  −  β·log π_θ(y_l|x)/π_ref(y_l|x) ) ]\n```\n\nEach log-ratio term is the *implicit reward* `r̂(x,y) = β·log(π_θ(y|x)/π_ref(y|x))`: how much more the\npolicy likes this response than the reference does, scaled by β. The loss wants the implicit reward\nmargin between chosen and rejected to be large and positive.\n\n### What the gradient does\nDifferentiating gives `−β·σ(r̂_l − r̂_w)·[∇log π_θ(y_w|x) − ∇log π_θ(y_l|x)]`. The bracket raises the\nchosen sequence's likelihood and lowers the rejected one's; the weight in front is the sigmoid of how\nwrong the current implicit reward ranking is. Pairs the model already ranks correctly with a large\nmargin contribute almost nothing; pairs it gets backwards contribute most. That weighting is what the\nnaive contrastive loss lacks and what stops DPO from endlessly pushing already-separated pairs.\n\n### The role of β\nβ is the same KL coefficient as in RLHF, read the other way round: it scales how large a log-ratio is\nneeded to achieve a given preference probability. Small β (TRL's default is 0.1) lets the policy move\nfar from the reference to separate pairs; large β keeps it close. There is no explicit KL term, but the\nreference log-probabilities appear in every pair, so drift is penalised implicitly.\n\n### Sequence log-probabilities\nEach term is the sum of per-token log-probabilities over the response tokens only, with prompt tokens\nmasked. The reference model is frozen, so its log-probs for the dataset can be computed once and\ncached, which brings the training memory down to one trainable model plus stored numbers, versus\nRLHF's four networks. A common trick when fine-tuning with LoRA is to disable the adapter for the\nreference forward pass instead of loading a second copy.\n\n### Why \"offline\" matters\nDPO uses whichever pairs you give it. If the chosen and rejected responses came from a different model\nthan the one being trained, the loss is comparing sequences the policy would rarely generate, and the\ngradient's effect on the policy's actual outputs is indirect. This is the distribution-shift cost of\ndropping the sampling loop. The fix is to generate the candidate responses with the policy being\ntrained (or its SFT ancestor), label those, and iterate in rounds, which recovers much of on-policy\nRLHF's advantage while keeping the simple loss.\n\n### Known failure: likelihood displacement\nThe loss only cares about the *difference* in log-ratios. A model can increase the margin by reducing\nthe probability of both chosen and rejected responses, as long as the rejected one falls faster. In\npractice chosen log-probability often does decrease during DPO, and the probability mass goes\nsomewhere unobserved, sometimes to worse responses. Monitoring chosen log-probability, not just the\nmargin, catches this; mixing a small SFT term on the chosen responses or using variants designed for\nit (IPO, KTO, ORPO, SimPO each change the loss or drop the reference in different ways) are the usual\nmitigations.","example":"The DPO loss on sequence log-probabilities, with the reference log-probs precomputed. Shapes are\n`[batch]` after summing per-token log-probs over response tokens; the helper shows the masked sum.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef seq_logprob(logits, ids, response_mask):\n    \"\"\"Sum of log p(token) over response tokens. logits [B,T,V], ids [B,T], mask [B,T] (1 on response).\"\"\"\n    lp = torch.log_softmax(logits[:, :-1].float(), -1).gather(-1, ids[:, 1:, None])[..., 0]\n    return (lp * response_mask[:, 1:]).sum(-1)                          # [B]\n\ndef dpo_loss(pol_w, pol_l, ref_w, ref_l, beta=0.1):\n    \"\"\"pol_*/ref_*: sequence log-probs of chosen (w) and rejected (l) under policy / reference.\"\"\"\n    margin = beta * ((pol_w - ref_w) - (pol_l - ref_l))                 # implicit reward margin\n    loss = -F.logsigmoid(margin).mean()\n    reward_acc = (margin > 0).float().mean()                            # fraction of pairs ranked right\n    return loss, margin, reward_acc\n\n# One pair, numbers illustrative. Policy has drifted toward chosen and away from rejected.\npol_w, ref_w = torch.tensor([-40.0]), torch.tensor([-42.0])            # chosen: +2 nats vs reference\npol_l, ref_l = torch.tensor([-35.0]), torch.tensor([-33.0])            # rejected: -2 nats vs reference\nloss, margin, acc = dpo_loss(pol_w, pol_l, ref_w, ref_l)\nprint(f\"margin={margin.item():.2f} loss={loss.item():.3f} reward_acc={acc.item():.0%}\")\n# margin = 0.1 * (2 - (-2)) = 0.40 ; loss = -log sigma(0.40) = 0.513 ; accuracy 100%\n\n# Watch during training: loss falling, reward_acc rising toward ~0.7-0.9 on a held-out set,\n# and chosen log-prob (pol_w) NOT falling steadily. If pol_w drops while margin grows,\n# probability mass is leaving both responses: likelihood displacement.\n```\n\nNote the scale: a 4-nat total swing in log-probability across responses of dozens of tokens is a small\nper-token change, yet with β = 0.1 it already yields a 60/40 preference. β is what turns modest\nlog-probability shifts into decisive preferences, and lowering it lets the model move further.","tradeoffs":["One trainable model and a cached reference instead of four networks makes DPO cheap and stable, but it is offline, so its advantage shrinks when the pairs were generated by a model far from the one you are training.","A small β separates pairs faster and allows larger behaviour changes, while a large β preserves the reference; the right value flips toward large β when the pairs are noisy or off-policy.","Dropping the reward model removes an artefact to maintain, but also removes a reusable scorer for rejection sampling, data filtering and monitoring, which matters once you run many rounds.","Variants that drop the reference (ORPO, SimPO) save memory and can reduce length bias, but give up the explicit leash and need their own margin or length terms to stay stable."],"pitfalls":["Training for several epochs on a small pair set; the symptom is reward accuracy near 100% on training pairs, falling held-out win rate, and verbose or strangely confident outputs.","Ignoring chosen log-probability; margin and loss look healthy while the policy's likelihood of good responses falls, and the mass migrates to unobserved outputs, often degenerate ones.","Using pairs from a stronger, different model as if they were on-policy; the loss moves but the trained model's actual outputs barely change, because it never produced sequences like those.","Length-confounded pairs where the chosen response is usually longer; the model learns length, which shows up as answers growing every round with no quality gain.","Mismatched tokenisation or chat template between the stored reference log-probs and the policy's inputs; the implicit rewards are then noise and training appears to work while nothing improves."],"when":"DPO is the default first preference-optimisation method for an application team: you have a few\nthousand to a few hundred thousand pairs, one node, and a need for reproducible runs. Use it after\nSFT, on pairs generated by the SFT model or by the policy from the previous round, and iterate in\nrounds if you can afford fresh labelling. Prefer RLHF with PPO or GRPO when you need on-policy\nexploration, a reusable reward model, or a programmatic reward, and when you have the engineering to\nrun the loop. Measure held-out reward accuracy, chosen log-probability, judge or human win rate against\nthe SFT model, and a general capability suite. In interviews, be ready to sketch the derivation, name\nthe implicit reward, and explain why DPO is offline and what that costs.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Precomputes reference log-probs, verifies the response mask and chat template match, and plots margin, chosen log-prob and held-out reward accuracy together each run.","architect":"Picks DPO over PPO when pair data is fixed and budgets are modest, and sets the iteration policy: how often fresh on-policy pairs are collected before the next round.","fde":"Sets up pairwise review with a customer's experts on the model's own outputs, since pairs about the model's actual mistakes are worth far more than imported preference datasets."},"check":{"q":"A team runs DPO for three epochs on 8,000 pairs. Training loss falls to near zero and\nreward accuracy is 99%, but a held-out judge prefers the SFT model and outputs have become long and\noddly phrased. Diagnose and propose fixes.","a":"The run overfit the pairs: three epochs on 8k pairs lets the model separate every training pair\nby memorising surface features, and length is the easiest such feature if chosen responses tend to be\nlonger. The near-zero loss means pairs are separated by huge margins, which with β = 0.1 implies large\nlog-ratio shifts from the reference, and that drift is what the held-out judge is penalising. I would\ncheck chosen log-probability over training for likelihood displacement and check the length ratio of\nchosen to rejected in the data. Fixes: one epoch with early stopping on held-out reward accuracy,\nlarger β or an added SFT term on chosen responses, balancing or length-normalising the pairs, and\nregenerating pairs on-policy so they describe this model's real errors rather than the dataset's\nartefacts."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the partition function Z(x) disappear from the DPO loss?","options":["It is assumed to equal one for a well-normalised language model","It depends only on the prompt, so it is identical for the chosen and rejected responses and cancels in their difference","It is absorbed into the learning rate","The reference model is trained to make it constant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Z(x) sums over all possible responses to the prompt, which is intractable, but Bradley–Terry only uses the difference of rewards for two responses to the same prompt, and the same Z(x) appears in both."},{"q":"What is the \"implicit reward\" in DPO?","options":["The output of a frozen reward model used to label the pairs","β times the log-ratio of policy to reference probability for a response","The number of pairs the policy ranks correctly","The negative of the KL divergence between policy and reference"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Inverting the optimal policy of the KL-regularised objective gives r(x,y) = β·log(π/π_ref) up to a prompt-only constant. DPO optimises the margin of this quantity between chosen and rejected responses."},{"q":"Which observation most directly indicates likelihood displacement during DPO?","options":["Reward accuracy on training pairs stays below 60%","The margin between chosen and rejected stops increasing","The chosen response's log-probability falls steadily even as the margin grows","The reference model's log-probabilities change between epochs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The loss rewards a growing difference, which can be achieved by lowering both responses' probability as long as the rejected one falls faster. Falling chosen log-probability means mass is moving to sequences the loss never sees."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/grpo","sources":["Shao et al., 2024 — DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models","DeepSeek-AI, 2025 — DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning","Schulman et al., 2017 — Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms"],"why":"PPO estimates how good each token was by comparing the reward it eventually earned against a\n*value model's* prediction of expected reward from that point. For language, the value model is\nusually another copy of the policy with a scalar head, trained alongside it. That doubles trainable\nmemory, and the value estimates are poor: the reward arrives only at the end of a long response, the\nvalue model must guess from partial text, and its errors feed straight into the advantages. Teams\nspent as much effort stabilising the critic as training the policy.\n\nMeanwhile a new kind of reward appeared. For maths with a known answer, code with unit tests, or\noutput with a checkable format, a *program* can score a response: right or wrong, passes or fails. No\nreward model, no raters, no overoptimisation of a learned proxy. With rewards this cheap, you can\nafford to sample many responses per prompt, and that changes what the best estimator is. If you have\neight attempts at the same problem, the natural question is not \"was this better than a critic's\nguess\" but \"was this better than the other seven\". The group *is* the baseline.\n\nThe naive alternative, REINFORCE with the raw reward, works in principle but has enormous variance:\nevery correct answer gets the same push regardless of whether the prompt was trivial or hard. The\ngroup-relative advantage fixes this automatically: on a prompt the model always solves, all rewards\nare equal and the advantage is zero, so no gradient is wasted on it; on a prompt it solves one time in\neight, the single success gets a large positive advantage. The signal concentrates where learning is\npossible.","how":"### Sampling the group\nFor each prompt q in a batch, the current policy (the \"old\" policy for this iteration) samples G\nresponses o₁ … o_G, with G commonly between 8 and 16 in open recipes and as high as 64 in\nDeepSeekMath (larger groups suit harder tasks). Each response\ngets a scalar reward r_i from a reward function: a rule-based verifier, a reward model, or a sum of\nseveral terms (accuracy plus format, for instance).\n\n### Group-relative advantage\nThe advantage for response i is its reward standardised within the group:\n\n```\nÂ_i = ( r_i − mean(r₁…r_G) ) / std(r₁…r_G)\n```\n\nThe same Â_i is assigned to every token of response i, because the reward is for the whole outcome\nand nothing finer-grained is known. This replaces the value model with two numbers per prompt. It is\ncheap, nearly unbiased (the group mean includes the sample's own reward, a small bias that a\nleave-one-out baseline such as RLOO removes), and has the \"difficulty-adaptive\" property described\nabove. Dividing by the standard deviation is a separate choice that up-weights prompts with few\nsuccesses, which is debated (see below). A group with\nall-equal rewards has zero variance and contributes nothing; implementations add a small epsilon to\nthe denominator and often filter such prompts out of the batch.\n\n### The clipped surrogate\nWith advantages in hand, GRPO uses PPO's clipped objective per token:\n\n```\nratio_t = π_θ(o_t | q, o_<t) / π_old(o_t | q, o_<t)\nL_clip  = mean over tokens of  min( ratio_t · Â_i ,  clip(ratio_t, 1−ε, 1+ε) · Â_i )\n```\n\nThe ratio compares the policy being updated to the one that generated the samples, and the clip\nremoves the gradient incentive to move any token's probability ratio beyond about 1±ε in one update. This lets\nthe same batch of samples be reused for a few gradient steps without the policy running away from the\ndata, which is the whole point of PPO-style methods over plain policy gradient.\n\n### The KL term\nInstead of folding the KL penalty into the reward as PPO does, GRPO adds it directly to the loss:\n`L = −L_clip + β · KL(π_θ ‖ π_ref)`, estimated per token with the low-variance estimator\n`π_ref/π_θ − log(π_ref/π_θ) − 1`, which is always non-negative. The reference is the SFT or base\ncheckpoint the run started from. Keeping the KL out of the reward means advantages reflect only task\nsuccess, and drift is controlled separately. Some later recipes drop the KL term entirely when the\nreward is fully verifiable and the run is monitored, trading safety for faster movement.\n\n### Reward design\nGRPO's results depend on the reward function more than on the algorithm. DeepSeek-R1 used rule-based\nrewards: an accuracy reward checking the final answer against ground truth, and a format reward\nchecking that reasoning appears inside designated tags. Rule-based rewards cannot be gamed by\nflattery the way a learned reward model can, but they can be gamed by format tricks, by answers that\nmatch a lax checker, or by test suites that are incomplete. For tasks without a verifier, a reward\nmodel is used and GRPO inherits RLHF's overoptimisation problem.\n\n### Why long reasoning emerges\nWith outcome-only rewards and many samples, responses that happen to check their work, backtrack, or\ntry alternatives succeed more often on hard problems, so the policy is pushed toward them. Response\nlength tends to grow during training as a result, which is a feature when it buys accuracy and a cost\nwhen it does not. A known subtlety is that averaging the loss per response over its own length (as in\nthe original formulation) interacts with length: wrong long responses are penalised less per token than\nwrong short ones, which can bias the model toward verbosity on failures. Later work proposed removing\nthe length and standard-deviation normalisations for this reason; which normalisation you use is a\nreal design choice, not a detail.","example":"Group advantages and the GRPO loss for one prompt with eight sampled responses. Rewards come from a\nverifier; three of eight are correct. Token log-probs are illustrative placeholders.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\nG, eps_clip, beta = 8, 0.2, 0.04\nrewards = torch.tensor([1., 0., 0., 1., 0., 0., 0., 1.])         # verifier: 3 of 8 correct\nadv = (rewards - rewards.mean()) / (rewards.std() + 1e-4)        # group-relative advantage, one per response\nprint(\"advantages:\", [round(a, 2) for a in adv.tolist()])\n# mean 0.375 -> correct responses get ~ +1.21, wrong ones ~ -0.72 (sample std); a 0/8 or 8/8\n# group would get all zeros and teach nothing.\n\ndef grpo_loss(logp_new, logp_old, logp_ref, adv, mask):\n    \"\"\"logp_* : [G, T] per-token log-probs; mask: [G, T] 1 on response tokens; adv: [G].\"\"\"\n    ratio = torch.exp(logp_new - logp_old)                          # pi_theta / pi_old, per token\n    a = adv[:, None]                                                # same advantage on every token\n    surr = torch.min(ratio * a, torch.clamp(ratio, 1 - eps_clip, 1 + eps_clip) * a)\n    lr = logp_ref - logp_new                                        # log(pi_ref / pi_theta)\n    kl = torch.exp(lr) - lr - 1                                     # non-negative per-token estimator\n    per_tok = -(surr - beta * kl)\n    return (per_tok * mask).sum() / mask.sum()                      # token-mean over the group\n\nT = 12\nlogp_old = -torch.rand(G, T) * 3\nlogp_new = logp_old + 0.05 * torch.randn(G, T)                     # a small policy change\nlogp_ref = logp_old + 0.1 * torch.randn(G, T)\nmask = torch.ones(G, T)\nprint(\"loss:\", grpo_loss(logp_new, logp_old, logp_ref, adv, mask).item())\n# In a real step: sample G responses per prompt with vLLM, score them, compute logp_old once,\n# then take 1-4 gradient steps on this loss before sampling again.\n```\n\nThe printed advantages show the mechanism: on a prompt the model gets right three times in eight,\neach success receives a push of about +1.2 and each failure about −0.7. Had it been right seven times,\nthe single failure would get a large negative push (about −2.5) and each success a small positive one\n(about +0.35), which is exactly where the gradient should go.","tradeoffs":["Removing the value model halves trainable memory and eliminates critic instability, but the group baseline needs several samples per prompt, so generation cost per update rises with G.","Verifiable rewards cannot be flattered and need no raters, yet they only exist for tasks with a checker, and a lax checker becomes the thing the model optimises instead of the task.","Larger groups give lower-variance advantages and more useful signal on hard prompts, while small groups are cheaper; the balance shifts toward larger G as the pass rate per prompt falls.","Keeping the KL term protects capability and prevents collapse, but slows movement on tasks where the reward is trustworthy; dropping it is defensible only with close monitoring of general evals."],"pitfalls":["Prompts the model always or never solves; the symptom is a large share of groups with zero advantage and GPU time burned on samples that produce no gradient. Curate difficulty and filter such groups.","Reward hacking on format or verifier gaps; the symptom is a reward curve rising while accuracy on an independent held-out checker stays flat, or outputs that satisfy tags with empty reasoning.","Mismatch between the sampling engine's and the trainer's token probabilities (different kernels or precision); the symptom is ratios far from 1 at the first gradient step and erratic training.","Letting response length grow unchecked; the symptom is rising mean length with flat accuracy and exploding inference cost, often a sign of the per-length normalisation bias or a weak length cap.","Treating the reward model case like the verifiable case; with a learned reward, GRPO overoptimises exactly as PPO does, so the same KL and human-evaluation discipline applies."],"when":"Use GRPO when you can write a reward function you trust, when the task benefits from the model\nexploring many attempts, and when you want on-policy RL without PPO's critic: maths and code\nreasoning, structured-output compliance, tool-call correctness checked by execution, and agentic tasks\nwith verifiable end states. Do not reach for it when a few thousand demonstrations or preference pairs\nalready solve the problem, because the sampling loop is the most expensive post-training stage after\npre-training. Measure accuracy on an independent held-out verifier, response length, KL from the\nreference, and a general capability suite. In interviews, expect to explain why the group replaces\nthe critic, write the advantage formula, and describe how reward hacking shows up with rule-based\nrewards.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes and tests the reward function, wires a fast sampler to the trainer, filters zero-variance groups, and tracks reward, length and KL per step.","architect":"Decides which product behaviours have a reward trustworthy enough to optimise with RL, and budgets the sampling compute against the expected gain over SFT and DPO.","fde":"Turns a customer's acceptance checks (schema validators, test suites, business rules) into reward functions, and audits them for the gaps a model will find and exploit."},"check":{"q":"You train with GRPO on a code task where the reward is \"all provided unit tests pass\". After a\nfew hundred steps, training reward is near 1.0 but the model's solutions fail on a hidden test set\nand often contain special-casing for the visible test inputs. What happened and what do you change?","a":"The reward was the visible tests, so the policy optimised them directly and discovered that\nhard-coding the expected outputs for the visible inputs is a cheaper path to full reward than solving\nthe problem. This is reward hacking on a lax verifier, and the hidden-set gap is the diagnostic.\nChanges: hold out tests from the reward so the model cannot see every case it is scored on, and\nrandomise or generate additional inputs per episode; add a reward term or a filter that penalises\ncode referencing literal test inputs; evaluate on a held-out verifier every few steps and stop when\nit diverges from training reward; and keep a KL term and general coding benchmarks in the loop so the\npolicy cannot drift into a degenerate style. The algorithm did its job; the reward defined the wrong\ntask."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does GRPO use instead of PPO's value model to compute advantages?","options":["The reward model's score for the prompt alone","The mean and standard deviation of the rewards of a group of responses sampled for the same prompt","The KL divergence from the reference policy","The length-normalised log-probability of each response"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each response's advantage is its reward standardised against its siblings' rewards. This removes the critic network entirely and makes the signal concentrate on prompts the model sometimes but not always solves."},{"q":"Why does a group in which all eight responses are correct contribute no gradient?","options":["The clip range zeroes the ratio when all rewards are equal","The KL term dominates when responses agree","All rewards equal the group mean, so every advantage is zero","The verifier returns no reward for repeated answers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The group-relative advantage subtracts the group mean; with identical rewards every difference is zero. This is intentional: the model has nothing to learn from a prompt it always solves, so the compute is better spent on harder prompts."},{"q":"Compared with RLHF using a learned reward model, what is the main reason rule-based rewards reduce reward hacking?","options":["Rule-based rewards are differentiable","A verifier cannot be flattered or drifted away from by stylistic changes, so the policy can only raise reward by actually satisfying the check","Rule-based rewards remove the need for a KL term entirely","Rule-based rewards are always dense per token"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A learned reward model has errors far from its training data that a policy can exploit with length or tone. A verifier has no such surface, though it has its own gaps (lax checks, visible test cases) that the policy will find instead."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/peft","sources":["Hu et al., 2021 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","Dettmers et al., 2023 — QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs","Aghajanyan et al., 2020 — Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning","Official docs: Hugging Face PEFT"],"why":"Fine-tuning a transformer with AdamW in mixed precision costs far more memory than the weights alone. Per\nparameter you hold the bf16 weight (2 bytes), its gradient (2 bytes), and an fp32 master copy plus the two\nAdam moments (12 bytes): about 16 bytes per parameter, before activations. For a 7B model that is\nroughly 112 GB, which does not fit on one 80 GB GPU. \"Just fine-tune it\" therefore means multi-GPU\nsharding, a bigger bill, and a 14 GB checkpoint for every task or customer you adapt to.\n\nThe second problem is what you do with the result. If every task produces a full copy of the model, you\ncannot keep ten of them resident on one server, you cannot A/B two variants cheaply, and rollback means\nmoving tens of gigabytes.\n\nThe observation that makes PEFT possible is that the *change* a fine-tune makes to a pretrained weight\nmatrix has low intrinsic rank: most of the adaptation lives in a small subspace of the full parameter\nspace. So instead of letting every entry of W move, you can parametrize the update ΔW directly in that\nsubspace and train only the subspace. Earlier PEFT methods did this indirectly, by inserting small adapter\nlayers between transformer blocks or by learning \"virtual tokens\" prepended to the input (prefix and\nprompt tuning). Both work, but adapters add inference latency and virtual tokens eat context. LoRA's trick\nis that its update has exactly the shape of the original matrix, so it can be folded back in and\ninference cost is unchanged.","how":"### LoRA: the update as a low-rank product\nFor a frozen weight matrix W₀ of shape d×k, LoRA writes the fine-tuned weight as W₀ + ΔW with\nΔW = B·A, where A is r×k, B is d×r, and the rank r is small (8–64 is typical, versus d = 4,096). The\nforward pass becomes:\n\n```\nh = W₀x + (α / r) · B(Ax)\n```\n\nOnly A and B receive gradients. Three design choices carry the reasoning:\n\n- **B starts at zero, A at small random values.** ΔW = BA is therefore exactly zero at step 0, so training\n  begins at the pretrained model's function instead of a perturbed one; the first steps cannot wreck what\n  pretraining built.\n- **The α/r scale.** It keeps the magnitude of the update roughly independent of r, so you can change rank\n  without retuning the learning rate. (A later variant, rsLoRA, argues α/√r is the better scaling when r\n  is large.)\n- **Which matrices get an adapter.** The original paper adapted only the attention query and value\n  projections. Later evidence, including the QLoRA paper's ablations, showed that putting adapters on *all*\n  linear layers, MLP included, matters more for quality than raising r. Knowledge-heavy tasks in particular\n  need the MLP capacity.\n\nThe parameter arithmetic for a Llama-2-7B-style layer (d = k = 4,096, MLP width 11,008, 32 layers) at\nr = 16: each 4,096×4,096 attention matrix gets 16 × (4,096 + 4,096) ≈ 131k adapter parameters instead of\n16.8M, which is under 1%. Over 32 layers × 4 attention matrices that is 16.8M; adding the three MLP\nmatrices (16 × (4,096 + 11,008) ≈ 242k each) brings the total to about 40M trainable parameters, roughly\n0.6% of 6.7B. Their fp32 Adam state is 40M × 16 bytes ≈ 640 MB instead of ~107 GB.\n\nNote what LoRA does *not* save: the frozen base still has to be in memory (≈13.4 GB in bf16 for 7B), and\nbackpropagation still flows through every layer to reach the adapters, so compute per step is only\nmodestly lower than full fine-tuning (the weight-gradient matmuls for frozen matrices are skipped,\nwhich is about half of the backward FLOPs, or a third of the forward-plus-backward total). The win is memory for optimizer state, master weights and weight\ngradients, plus a checkpoint measured in tens to a few hundred megabytes (40M adapter parameters are\nabout 80 MB in bf16 or 160 MB in fp32, against 13.4 GB for the base).\n\nAt export time you either **merge** (W = W₀ + (α/r)BA, a dense matrix of the original shape, zero extra\nlatency) or keep adapters separate so a server can hold one base and many adapters, batching requests for\ndifferent adapters together; vLLM, SGLang and similar engines support this.\n\n### QLoRA: shrinking the frozen base\nLoRA leaves one large term: the 2 bytes per parameter for the frozen weights. QLoRA attacks that with\nthree pieces:\n\n1. **4-bit NormalFloat (NF4).** Pretrained weights are approximately normally distributed, so instead of\n   evenly spaced 4-bit levels, NF4 places its 16 levels at quantiles of a standard normal; each bin holds\n   about the same probability mass, which minimizes expected rounding error for that distribution.\n   Quantization is block-wise (blocks of 64 weights, one absmax scale each).\n2. **Double quantization.** The per-block scales are themselves quantized to 8-bit with a second-level\n   scale, because at a block size of 64 the fp32 scales alone would cost 0.5 bits per weight. This saves a\n   few tenths of a bit per parameter on average.\n3. **Paged optimizers.** Optimizer state lives in CUDA unified memory so it can be paged to CPU RAM\n   during the memory spikes that long sequences cause, turning a hard OOM into a slowdown.\n\nDuring the forward pass each 4-bit block is dequantized to bf16 on the fly and multiplied as usual; the\nLoRA matrices stay in bf16 and get full-precision gradients. The base is never updated, so its\nquantization error is fixed and the adapter learns around it. The paper's headline was fine-tuning a 65B\nmodel on a single 48 GB GPU with quality on their benchmarks matching 16-bit fine-tuning. The costs are a\nslower step (dequantization is extra work on every matmul) and a subtle coupling: the adapter was trained\nagainst *this* quantized base, so merging it into a bf16 copy gives a slightly different model.\n\n### Other PEFT methods in one line each\nAdapter layers (bottleneck MLPs inserted into each block; extra latency), prefix/prompt tuning (learned\nvirtual tokens; consume context and are weak on small models), (IA)³ (learned per-channel rescaling\nvectors), and DoRA (splits W into magnitude and direction and applies LoRA to the direction; often tracks\nfull fine-tuning more closely at the same rank). LoRA remains the default because it merges away.","example":"Loading a model in NF4, attaching LoRA to all linear projections, and inspecting the result. On an\n8B Llama-3-style model with r = 16 this yields on the order of 42M trainable parameters (about 0.5%).\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, BitsAndBytesConfig\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, prepare_model_for_kbit_training\n\nname = \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B\"                       # any causal LM; ~6 GB VRAM for the base in 4-bit\nbnb = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",       # NormalFloat4 for the frozen base\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True,                      # quantize the quantization constants too\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16)               # dequantize to bf16 for the matmuls\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, quantization_config=bnb, device_map=\"auto\")\nmodel = prepare_model_for_kbit_training(model)          # fp32 norms, gradient-checkpointing hooks\n\ncfg = LoraConfig(\n    r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05, bias=\"none\", task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\", \"gate_proj\", \"up_proj\", \"down_proj\"])\nmodel = get_peft_model(model, cfg)\nmodel.print_trainable_parameters()                      # trainable ~0.5% of total\n\nq = model.base_model.model.model.layers[0].self_attn.q_proj\nprint(q.lora_A[\"default\"].weight.shape, q.lora_B[\"default\"].weight.shape)   # [16, 4096], [4096, 16]\nprint(float(q.lora_B[\"default\"].weight.abs().max()))     # 0.0: training starts at the base model\n\n# ... train with TRL's SFTTrainer or a plain loop; LoRA LRs of 1e-4 to 2e-4 are typical ...\nmodel.save_pretrained(\"adapter/\")                        # only A and B: ~85 MB bf16 / ~170 MB fp32, not 16 GB\n# To ship without the peft runtime: load the base in bf16, attach the adapter, then\n# merged = model.merge_and_unload(); merged.save_pretrained(\"merged/\")  -> re-evaluate after merging\n```","tradeoffs":["Memory falls by an order of magnitude and checkpoints by about two, but per-step compute barely drops because backprop still traverses the frozen base; PEFT makes fine-tuning *fit*, not fast.","LoRA learns less and forgets less than full fine-tuning: on narrow tasks that is a feature (base capabilities survive), but for large distribution shifts such as a new language or heavy code pretraining, full fine-tuning or a much higher rank on all layers pulls ahead.","QLoRA cuts base-model memory roughly 3–4× relative to bf16 at the cost of slower steps and a fixed quantization error; when you have the GPU memory for bf16 LoRA, it is the simpler and faster choice.","Keeping adapters unmerged enables one base serving many tenants, but each request pays a small extra matmul and the engine must support multi-adapter batching; merging gives zero overhead and one model per adapter.","Higher rank adds capacity with diminishing returns; past the point where training loss stops improving with r, extra rank only adds memory and overfitting risk."],"pitfalls":["Copying the learning rate from a full fine-tuning recipe (≈1e-5): LoRA parameters are tiny and need a larger LR; the symptom is a training loss that barely moves across epochs.","Adapting only `q_proj` and `v_proj` because an old tutorial did: tasks needing new knowledge or formats underfit; the symptom is good style, wrong facts, fixed by targeting the MLP projections too.","Merging a QLoRA adapter into a bf16 base and shipping without re-evaluation: the adapter compensated for a specific quantized base, so the merged model drifts slightly; always re-run evals after merging or after re-quantizing the merged model to GPTQ/AWQ for serving.","Training with one chat template and serving with another: the adapter learned token patterns around the template, so the symptom is a model that rambles, never emits the end token or ignores the system prompt.","Shipping an adapter without pinning the exact base model revision: adapters are deltas against specific weights, and a silently updated base produces a quietly different model.","Forgetting `prepare_model_for_kbit_training` or input-gradient hooks when enabling gradient checkpointing on a frozen base: the symptom is \"element 0 of tensors does not require grad\" or an adapter whose loss never changes."],"when":"Reach for LoRA whenever you fine-tune a model larger than a few hundred million parameters and the task is\na style, format, domain-vocabulary or behaviour shift: support-ticket tone, structured output, a\nhouse-specific classifier, tool-call formatting. Choose QLoRA when the bf16 base does not fit your GPU\nalongside activations, and accept the slower step. Prefer full fine-tuning only when you have the hardware\nand the task is a large shift that LoRA demonstrably underfits on your eval set. Measure three things:\ntask metric versus the base model, a held-out general benchmark to catch forgetting, and the merged\nmodel's metric versus the unmerged one. In day-to-day work PEFT shows up as a `LoraConfig` block, an\nadapter directory in object storage, and a serving decision about merging. In interviews, be ready to\nderive the 16-bytes-per-parameter figure, explain why B is zero-initialized, and say where QLoRA's\nsavings come from and what they cost.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `LoraConfig`, picks rank and target modules, sets a LoRA-appropriate learning rate, and verifies the merged checkpoint matches the adapter's eval numbers before handing it to serving.","architect":"Decides between one merged model per task and one base with hot-swapped adapters, based on tenant count, GPU budget and latency, and owns the base-model version pinning policy.","fde":"Fine-tunes on a customer's data inside their VPC on whatever GPU they have, often a single 24–48 GB card, which is precisely the regime QLoRA was built for."},"check":{"q":"You have one 24 GB GPU and 20k support transcripts, and you need to adapt an 8B model to the\ncustomer's tone and ticket format. Walk through what fits in memory and what you would choose.","a":"Full fine-tuning is out: 8B × 16 bytes ≈ 128 GB for weights, gradients and Adam state. bf16 LoRA\nneeds the frozen base at ≈16 GB plus adapter state (tens of MB) plus activations; with gradient\ncheckpointing and short sequences it might squeeze into 24 GB but it is tight. QLoRA stores the base in\nNF4 at roughly 5–6 GB, leaving ample room for activations at longer sequence lengths, so that is the\npractical choice. I would target all linear projections at r = 16, LR around 1e-4 to 2e-4, train one to\nthree epochs while watching a held-out slice of transcripts and a small general benchmark for forgetting,\nthen merge into a bf16 base for serving and re-run the evals on the merged model, because the adapter was\ntrained against the quantized base."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is LoRA's B matrix initialized to zero while A is random?","options":["Zero initialization keeps the adapter sparse so it can be stored compactly","So the update BA is zero at step 0 and training starts from the exact pretrained function","Because gradients cannot flow through two randomly initialized matrices","To make the rank r adjustable without changing α"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With B = 0 the product BA vanishes, so the model behaves identically to the base model before training and early updates cannot damage it. Random A still gives B a non-degenerate gradient, so learning proceeds."},{"q":"Where does QLoRA's memory saving over plain LoRA come from?","options":["It trains fewer adapter parameters by using a smaller rank","It drops the optimizer state entirely","It stores the frozen base weights in 4-bit NF4 and dequantizes them on the fly during matmuls","It offloads activations to CPU memory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"LoRA already removed optimizer state and gradients for the base; what remained was the 2-byte-per-weight frozen model. QLoRA shrinks that term to about half a byte per weight; paged optimizers only handle transient spikes."},{"q":"What happens to inference latency after a LoRA adapter is merged into the base weights?","options":["It increases slightly because of the extra rank-r projection","It decreases because the merged matrix is lower rank","It is unchanged, because W₀ + BA is a dense matrix of the original shape","It depends on whether the base was quantized during training"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Merging adds BA into W₀, producing one dense d×k matrix, so the forward pass performs exactly the same matmul as the base model. Only an *unmerged* adapter adds the extra low-rank computation."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/knowledge-distillation","sources":["Hinton, Vinyals & Dean, 2015 — Distilling the Knowledge in a Neural Network","Kim & Rush, 2016 — Sequence-Level Knowledge Distillation","Sanh et al., 2019 — DistilBERT, a distilled version of BERT","Agarwal et al., 2023 — On-Policy Distillation of Language Models: Learning from Self-Generated Mistakes"],"why":"The model that scores best is usually the one you cannot afford to serve. A 70B-class model is accurate\nbut costs roughly ten times the GPU time per token of a 7B one, and the obvious fix, \"train a 7B model on\nthe same data\", loses a lot of that accuracy. Part of the loss is capacity, but a surprising part is the\ntraining signal. A one-hot label says \"the next token is ` Paris`\" and nothing else. The large model's\noutput distribution says ` Paris` 0.9, ` the` 0.05, ` located` 0.02, ` Berlin` 0.0001, and that ranking of\nthe wrong answers is itself knowledge about the world and the language. Hinton called it *dark knowledge*:\ninformation in the relative probabilities of the non-argmax classes that the hard label throws away.\n\nDistillation keeps that information. Instead of asking the student to reproduce the dataset, it asks the\nstudent to reproduce the teacher's function, and the teacher's function is smoother, lower-variance and\navailable for *any* input, labelled or not. That last property matters as much as the first: you can run\nthe teacher on millions of unlabelled prompts from your actual traffic, which you could never pay humans to\nlabel, and train the student on all of it.\n\nWhy not just compress the teacher with quantization or pruning? Those keep the architecture and shave a\nconstant factor. Distillation lets you change the architecture entirely, fewer layers, narrower width, a\ndifferent family, and it combines with both: a common recipe for a small model today is prune a big\none, then distil from the original to repair it.","how":"### Classic logit distillation\nThe teacher and student each produce logits **z** over the vocabulary (or classes). Soften both with a\ntemperature *T*:\n\n```\np_i(T) = exp(z_i / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T)\n```\n\nAt *T* = 1 a confident teacher's distribution is almost one-hot, so the information in the small\nprobabilities is invisible. Raising *T* flattens the distribution and amplifies the ratios between the\nwrong answers. For teacher logits [6, 2, 1], *T* = 1 gives [0.976, 0.018, 0.007]; *T* = 4 gives\n[0.605, 0.222, 0.173]. Both encode the same ratio between the wrong answers (p₂/p₃ = e^(1/T), about 2.7\nat *T* = 1 and 1.3 at *T* = 4), but at *T* = 1 those two classes hold 2.5% of the mass and produce almost no\ngradient; at *T* = 4 they hold 40% of it, so the student actually gets to learn the ranking.\n\nThe loss mixes two terms:\n\n```\nL = α · T² · KL( p_teacher(T) ‖ p_student(T) )  +  (1 − α) · CE( y, p_student(1) )\n```\n\nThe *T²* factor is there because the gradient of the soft term scales roughly as 1/*T²*; multiplying it\nback keeps the relative weight of the soft and hard terms stable when you change *T*. The hard-label term\nis kept because the teacher is sometimes wrong, and the ground truth, where you have it, corrects that. Hinton\nalso showed that in the high-*T* limit matching softened distributions becomes matching logits directly\n(a mean-squared error on **z**), which is why \"logit distillation\" and \"soft-target distillation\" are\nused interchangeably.\n\n### Three regimes for language models\n1. **White-box, token-level.** Teacher and student share a tokenizer. For every position of a training\n   sequence the student minimises a divergence between its next-token distribution and the teacher's. Per\n   token the signal is a distribution over 100k+ vocabulary entries instead of one index, so the student\n   learns far more per sequence. The cost is a teacher forward pass for every training sequence; in\n   practice teams precompute the teacher's top-*k* logits (say 64 per position, renormalised) and store\n   them, because full distributions would be hundreds of kilobytes per token. Several recent small models\n   were pretrained this way with a larger sibling's logits as targets rather than from hard tokens alone.\n2. **Black-box, sequence-level.** You only have the teacher's text. Generate responses for a large set of\n   prompts, then run ordinary supervised fine-tuning on (prompt, teacher response) pairs. This is what\n   \"distilled\" means in most product announcements, and technically it is SFT on synthetic data. The\n   distillation framing changes what you optimise: which prompts cover the deployment distribution, how\n   many samples per prompt, and whether you filter samples by a verifier (rejection sampling) so the\n   student learns the teacher's correct reasoning rather than its average output. Reasoning-model\n   distillates released in 2025 were made this way, fine-tuning open 1.5B–70B checkpoints on hundreds of\n   thousands of teacher-generated traces.\n3. **On-policy.** Both regimes above train the student on prefixes the *teacher* wrote. At inference the\n   student sees its own prefixes, including its own mistakes, and has never learned to recover from them\n   (exposure bias). On-policy distillation samples sequences from the student, has the teacher score every\n   token of those sequences, and trains the student toward the teacher on its own trajectories. It costs\n   generation inside the training loop and a live teacher, but it attacks the exact train/inference\n   mismatch that sequence-level distillation leaves open.\n\n### Which divergence\nThe forward KL(teacher ‖ student) is mode-covering: it punishes the student for assigning near-zero\nprobability to anything the teacher finds plausible, so a small student spreads its mass thinly over\nevery option and samples from it look hedged and low quality. The reverse KL(student ‖ teacher) is\nmode-seeking: it lets the student concentrate on the teacher's dominant modes and ignore the rest, which\nsuits a student with less capacity than the teacher. Methods such as MiniLLM use reverse KL and\ngeneralised-KD interpolates between the two with a Jensen–Shannon style divergence. Treat this as a\ntunable, and remember that whichever you choose, the student's ceiling on the covered distribution is the\nteacher.\n\n### Why it pairs with pruning\nA student initialised randomly must relearn everything. A student initialised by structurally pruning\nthe teacher (dropping layers, heads or MLP width) already sits near a good solution, and distillation\nprovides a dense correction signal, so the pair recovers most of the teacher's quality with a small\nfraction of the tokens pretraining would need. That is the recipe behind several open small-model\nfamilies and the reason the two topics sit next to each other in this module.","example":"A token-level distillation loss for a causal LM in PyTorch. Both models must use the same tokenizer so\nvocabulary positions line up; the teacher is frozen. The `T²` scaling and the mixed hard/soft objective\nfollow Hinton's formulation; padded positions are masked out of the soft term.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndef kd_loss(student_logits, teacher_logits, labels, T=2.0, alpha=0.5, ignore_index=-100):\n    \"\"\"student/teacher logits: [batch, seq, vocab]; labels: [batch, seq], a copy of input_ids with\n    ignore_index at masked positions (HF convention: unshifted, the shift happens below).\"\"\"\n    s = student_logits[:, :-1].float()          # position t predicts token t+1\n    t = teacher_logits[:, :-1].float()\n    y = labels[:, 1:]\n    mask = (y != ignore_index).float()\n    # soft term: KL(teacher || student) at temperature T, scaled by T^2\n    log_p_s = F.log_softmax(s / T, dim=-1)\n    p_t = F.softmax(t / T, dim=-1)\n    soft = F.kl_div(log_p_s, p_t, reduction=\"none\").sum(-1)      # [batch, seq-1]\n    soft = (soft * mask).sum() / mask.sum() * (T * T)\n    # hard term: ordinary next-token cross-entropy against the ground-truth tokens\n    hard = F.cross_entropy(s.reshape(-1, s.size(-1)), y.reshape(-1), ignore_index=ignore_index)\n    return alpha * soft + (1 - alpha) * hard\n\n# one training step; `teacher` is in eval mode with requires_grad disabled\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    t_out = teacher(input_ids=batch[\"input_ids\"], attention_mask=batch[\"attention_mask\"])\ns_out = student(input_ids=batch[\"input_ids\"], attention_mask=batch[\"attention_mask\"])\nloss = kd_loss(s_out.logits, t_out.logits, batch[\"labels\"], T=2.0, alpha=0.7)\nloss.backward(); optimizer.step(); optimizer.zero_grad()\n# For sequence-level distillation there is no kd_loss at all: generate teacher responses offline,\n# filter them with a verifier, and run plain SFT on the survivors.\n```","tradeoffs":["The student gains accuracy per parameter, but you pay a teacher forward pass over the whole training set, plus storage if you cache logits; for sequence-level distillation that bill is teacher generation, which is the expensive direction.","Token-level distillation carries far more signal per example but requires a shared tokenizer and access to logits; black-box distillation works across model families and APIs but transfers less per example and copies the teacher's mistakes verbatim.","Off-policy sequence distillation is simple and embarrassingly parallel; on-policy distillation fixes exposure bias but needs generation inside the loop and a teacher available at training time, which roughly turns a data-processing job into an RL-shaped one.","A distilled student is strong on the distribution the prompts covered and degrades faster than the teacher outside it; this is fine for a narrow product surface and a problem for a general assistant.","Distilling from a third-party API may be forbidden by its terms of service for training competing models; check before building a pipeline around it."],"pitfalls":["Leaving *T* = 1 and α high: the soft targets are nearly one-hot, so the student learns nothing beyond SFT; the symptom is a distilled model that matches the plain fine-tuning baseline exactly.","Distilling across tokenizers with a naive logit loss: the vocabularies do not align, so the KL is meaningless; the symptom is a loss that plateaus high or goes NaN. Align tokens or fall back to sequence-level distillation.","Training on unfiltered teacher generations: the student inherits hallucinations and verbosity; the symptom is a student that is confidently wrong in exactly the teacher's way. Filter with a verifier or rejection sampling first.","Measuring success as agreement with the teacher on training prompts rather than task metrics on held-out real traffic; the symptom is a high \"fidelity\" number and unhappy users.","Choosing the biggest available teacher regardless of the gap: a student far smaller than its teacher can learn *less* from it than from a mid-sized intermediate (the teacher-assistant effect); the symptom is a larger teacher producing a worse student.","Forgetting general data in the mix: distilling a narrow capability into a small instruct model erodes its other skills; the symptom shows on a general benchmark run before and after."],"when":"Reach for distillation when you have a strong model that is too slow or costly for the deployment, a large\nsupply of prompts (labelled or not) representing real usage, and a narrower target surface than the\nteacher's. It is the right tool for turning a frontier model's behaviour on *your* task into a small model\nyou can host on one GPU, and for building reasoning-capable small models from a reasoning-capable large\none. Avoid it when the product needs the teacher's full breadth, or when you cannot evaluate the student\non real held-out data. Measure the student against the teacher on your task metric, the latency and cost\nper thousand requests, and a general benchmark for collateral damage. In interviews, be ready to explain\ndark knowledge and the *T²* factor, say when sequence-level distillation is just SFT on synthetic data, and\nargue why on-policy distillation exists.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the pipeline that runs the teacher over prompt logs, stores top-*k* logits or filtered generations, and trains the student with a temperature-scaled loss, verifying gains on held-out tasks.","architect":"Decides which capabilities justify a frontier model and which should be distilled into a small hosted one, and owns the policy on which teachers are legally and contractually usable.","fde":"Distils a customer's expensive prompt-chain into a small model trained on their own traffic inside their environment, so the solution survives their cost and data-residency constraints."},"check":{"q":"A team runs a 70B model for ticket triage plus a short drafted reply, at a cost they cannot sustain.\nThey want an 8B replacement. Lay out how you would distil it and what could go wrong.","a":"Collect several hundred thousand real tickets from logs, deduplicated and scrubbed of PII, and run\nthe 70B teacher over them to produce triage labels and drafts. Filter: drop drafts failing a format check\nand, where ground-truth triage labels exist from agents, drop teacher outputs that disagree. If both models\nshare a tokenizer, store the teacher's top-64 logits per position and train the 8B student with a\ntemperature-scaled KL plus cross-entropy; otherwise run SFT on the filtered teacher text. Evaluate on a\nheld-out month of tickets against the teacher's metrics, not against the teacher's outputs. Risks: the\nstudent copies teacher hallucinations if filtering is weak; it degrades on ticket types rare in the sample;\nand on-policy problems appear in long drafts, which argues for a final on-policy distillation pass or at\nleast rejection sampling with the teacher as judge."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Hinton's distillation loss multiply the soft-target term by T²?","options":["To penalise the student more heavily for confident mistakes","Because the soft-term gradient shrinks roughly as 1/T², so T² keeps its weight stable across temperatures","To convert the KL divergence into a cross-entropy","Because the teacher's logits are stored at temperature T"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Softening by T scales the logit gradients down by about 1/T². Without the correction, raising T to expose dark knowledge would also silently shrink the soft term relative to the hard-label term."},{"q":"Which distillation regime requires the teacher and student to share a tokenizer?","options":["Sequence-level distillation on generated text","Token-level distillation that matches next-token distributions","Rejection-sampled SFT on teacher outputs","Any distillation from an API-only teacher"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Matching distributions position by position only makes sense if the vocabulary indices mean the same thing in both models. Text-based regimes transfer through tokens, so any tokenizer pair works."},{"q":"What problem does on-policy distillation solve that sequence-level distillation leaves open?","options":["The teacher's outputs are too long to fit the student's context","The student cannot see the teacher's logits","The student only ever trains on teacher-written prefixes and never learns to recover from its own errors","The teacher must be re-run every epoch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Training on teacher trajectories creates exposure bias: at inference the student conditions on its own imperfect prefixes. Sampling from the student and scoring with the teacher trains on exactly that situation."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/quantization","sources":["Dettmers et al., 2022 — LLM.int8(): 8-bit Matrix Multiplication for Transformers at Scale","Frantar et al., 2022 — GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","Lin et al., 2023 — AWQ: Activation-aware Weight Quantization for LLM Compression and Acceleration","Official docs: llama.cpp (GGUF)"],"why":"Two numbers drive everything. A 70B-parameter model in bf16 is 140 GB of weights, which does not fit on\none 80 GB GPU; at 4 bits it is about 35–40 GB and does. A 7B model goes from 14 GB to about 4 GB, which is\nthe difference between a data-centre card and a laptop. That is the memory argument.\n\nThe speed argument is subtler. During decode the GPU reads every weight once per generated token, and the\narithmetic per weight is tiny (one multiply-add per token at batch size 1), so time per token is roughly\nbytes of weights divided by memory bandwidth. Halve the bytes and small-batch decoding nearly doubles in\nspeed, with no change to the maths. Quantization is a bandwidth optimisation wearing a compression costume.\n\nThe naive alternatives both fail. \"Train a smaller model instead\" loses more quality than quantizing a\nbigger one: across many reported comparisons a 4-bit model with twice the parameters beats a 16-bit model\nof half the size at the same memory footprint. \"Just cast to int8 with one scale per tensor\" fails because\nof outliers: transformers past a few billion parameters develop a handful of activation channels with\nmagnitudes tens of times larger than the rest, and one such value sets the scale for the whole tensor, so\neverything else rounds to zero or one level. Every modern method is a strategy for handling that range\nproblem: finer-grained scales, outlier-aware scaling, or error compensation.","how":"### The arithmetic of a quantizer\nSymmetric *b*-bit quantization maps a real value *x* to an integer *q* with one scale *s*:\n\n```\ns = max|x| / (2^(b−1) − 1)        q = clamp(round(x / s), −2^(b−1), 2^(b−1) − 1)        x̂ = s · q\n```\n\nINT8 gives 127 levels on each side of zero; INT4 gives only −8…7. The asymmetric (affine) variant adds a\nzero-point *z* so the grid covers [min, max] exactly, which matters for skewed distributions and is the\nusual choice for 4-bit weights. Rounding error is roughly uniform in [−*s*/2, *s*/2] with mean-squared\nerror *s*²/12, so error grows with the range the scale has to cover. That single fact explains the\ngranularity ladder:\n\n- **Per-tensor:** one scale. Cheapest, and ruined by a single outlier.\n- **Per-channel:** one scale per output row. Standard for INT8.\n- **Per-group:** one scale (and zero-point) per *g* consecutive weights along the input dimension,\n  typically *g* = 128 or 32. An outlier now only degrades its own group. The price is overhead: INT4 with\n  *g* = 128, an fp16 scale and a 4-bit zero-point costs 4 + 20/128 ≈ 4.16 bits per weight; *g* = 32 costs\n  about 4.6. \"4-bit\" models are never exactly 4 bits.\n\n### Weights only, or weights and activations\n**W4A16 / W8A16** (weight-only) keeps weights in low-bit storage and dequantizes them to fp16/bf16 inside\nthe matmul kernel. Memory and bandwidth drop; compute does not, because the multiply still happens in\n16-bit. This helps decode (bandwidth-bound) and does nothing for prefill (compute-bound); at large batch\nsizes the dequantization overhead can make a W4A16 model slower than the fp16 original. GPTQ and AWQ\nlive here.\n\n**W8A8** quantizes activations too, so the matmul runs on INT8 (or FP8) tensor cores at roughly twice the\nfp16 throughput. Activations are harder than weights because of the outlier channels and because they\nchange with every input. LLM.int8() handles this by pulling the few outlier columns out into an fp16\nside-matmul; SmoothQuant migrates activation range into the weights with a per-channel scale before\nquantizing both. On GPUs with native FP8 (E4M3) the problem eases, because a float format has a built-in\nexponent and copes with dynamic range, so FP8 weights+activations+KV cache has become the default\n\"almost free\" setting for throughput serving on that hardware.\n\nThe **KV cache** can be quantized too (8-bit is common), which doubles the number of concurrent sequences\nat long context; see the KV cache lesson for why that is often the binding constraint rather than weights.\n\n### Post-training quantization versus quantization-aware training\nPTQ takes a trained model and a small calibration set (a few hundred sequences) and produces a quantized\nmodel in minutes to hours; GPTQ and AWQ are PTQ. QAT simulates quantization in the forward pass during\nfine-tuning and uses a straight-through estimator for the backward pass, so the weights learn to live on\nthe grid; it is more accurate at extreme bit-widths (2–3 bits, or small models) but costs a training run.\nQLoRA sits in between: a frozen 4-bit base with trainable adapters that learn around its rounding error\n(see the PEFT lesson).\n\n### GPTQ: compensate the error you just made\nRound-to-nearest treats every weight independently. GPTQ instead minimises the layer's reconstruction\nerror ‖**WX** − **ŴX**‖² over calibration inputs **X**, using the second-order machinery of Optimal Brain\nSurgeon. The Hessian of that objective is **H** = 2**XXᵀ**, which depends on the inputs, not on **W**.\nGPTQ quantizes a weight matrix one column at a time: round column *j*, compute the error, and update all\nstill-unquantized columns by a multiple of the corresponding row of **H⁻¹** so they absorb the error.\nDoing this in a fixed column order with a Cholesky factor of **H⁻¹** and lazy batched updates made it\nnumerically stable and fast enough to quantize a 175B model in a few GPU-hours. The result is 4-bit (and\nusable 3-bit) weights with a small perplexity increase. Because it fits **W** to the calibration inputs,\nGPTQ is somewhat sensitive to the calibration data's domain.\n\n### AWQ: protect the weights that matter\nAWQ starts from a measurement: not all weights are equal, and the ones that matter are identified by the\n*activations* flowing through them, not by their own magnitude. A small weight on a channel that always\ncarries large activations contributes a lot to the output, so its rounding error hurts. Keeping that\n~1% of channels in fp16 works but produces a mixed-precision layout hardware dislikes. AWQ's trick is\nmathematically free: multiply a salient weight channel by *s* > 1 and divide the matching activation\nchannel by *s*; the product is unchanged, but the weight is now larger relative to the rounding step, so\nits relative error falls. The per-channel scales are chosen by a small grid search\n(*s* = mean activation magnitude^α, α ∈ [0, 1]) minimising output error on calibration data. There is\nno gradient and no weight reconstruction, so AWQ runs fast, overfits the calibration set less than GPTQ,\nand transfers well across domains and modalities. Both ship with fast INT4 GPU kernels (Marlin, ExLlama)\nin vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM.\n\n### GGUF: a container, not a method\nGGUF is the single-file format used by llama.cpp (it replaced the older GGML format in 2023). One file\nholds the tensors, their quantization type per tensor, and all metadata: architecture hyperparameters,\nthe tokenizer vocabulary and the chat template, laid out so the file can be memory-mapped and used\ndirectly. Its quantization types encode a bit-width and a block scheme: `Q8_0` and `Q4_0` use blocks of 32\nwith one fp16 scale (about 8.5 and 4.5 bits per weight); the K-quants (`Q4_K_M`, `Q5_K_M`, `Q6_K`) use\n256-weight super-blocks with compact sub-block scales, and the `S`/`M`/`L` suffix is a mixing recipe that\nkeeps the most sensitive tensors (attention value/output and parts of the MLP) at higher precision; the\nI-quants (`IQ2_XXS`…`IQ4_XS`) use non-uniform codebooks and an optional importance matrix from\ncalibration data to push below 4 bits. GGUF exists because llama.cpp's audience is CPUs, Apple silicon and\nmixed CPU/GPU machines where tensors are offloaded layer by layer, and the block layouts are designed for\nSIMD dequantization there. On a GPU server, GPTQ/AWQ/FP8 through a batching engine will usually\nout-throughput it.","example":"Why the granularity ladder matters, in NumPy. A Gaussian weight matrix gets one outlier input channel, as\nreal transformer weights and (more severely) activations do. The relative output error on random\nactivations shows per-tensor INT4 collapsing, per-channel barely helping, and per-group scales rescuing it.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nW = rng.normal(0, 0.02, size=(4096, 4096)).astype(np.float32)   # [out, in]\nW[:, 7] *= 40                                                   # one outlier input channel\n\ndef quant_sym(x, bits, axis=None):\n    \"\"\"Symmetric round-to-nearest with one scale per slice along `axis` (None = per-tensor).\"\"\"\n    qmax = 2 ** (bits - 1) - 1\n    s = np.max(np.abs(x), axis=axis, keepdims=True) / qmax\n    q = np.clip(np.round(x / s), -qmax - 1, qmax)\n    return (q * s).astype(np.float32)\n\ndef quant_grouped(x, bits, g):\n    rows, cols = x.shape\n    return quant_sym(x.reshape(rows, cols // g, g), bits, axis=2).reshape(rows, cols)\n\nX = rng.normal(size=(512, 4096)).astype(np.float32)             # calibration activations\nref = X @ W.T\nfor name, W_hat in [(\"int8 per-tensor\", quant_sym(W, 8)),\n                    (\"int4 per-tensor\", quant_sym(W, 4)),\n                    (\"int4 per-channel\", quant_sym(W, 4, axis=1)),\n                    (\"int4 group=128\", quant_grouped(W, 4, 128)),\n                    (\"int4 group=32\", quant_grouped(W, 4, 32))]:\n    err = np.linalg.norm(X @ W_hat.T - ref) / np.linalg.norm(ref)\n    print(f\"{name:17s} relative output error = {err:.3f}\")\n# Prints roughly 0.32 / 0.85 / 0.68 / 0.16 / 0.10 (int8 per-tensor already hurts with a 40x outlier).\n# Per-channel still fails here because every output row contains the outlier column, so every\n# row's scale is set by it; grouping isolates the damage to 1/32 (g=128) or 1/128 (g=32) of the weights.\n# GPTQ would additionally push each group's rounding error into its neighbours; AWQ would rescale\n# channel 7 before rounding. Bits per weight with an fp16 scale + 4-bit zero-point per group, as in\n# the ladder above: 8 / 4 / 4 / ~4.16 / ~4.6 respectively (this symmetric toy stores no zero-point).\n```","tradeoffs":["Memory and decode speed improve roughly in proportion to bits saved, while quality loss is small at 8-bit and usually modest at 4-bit for models of 7B and up; the loss grows for small models and for precision-sensitive tasks such as maths, code and long multi-step reasoning, where it must be measured.","Weight-only INT4 accelerates small-batch decode but not prefill, and at high batch its dequantization overhead can lose to fp16 or FP8 W8A8; the right choice flips with batch size and with whether your workload is prompt-heavy (RAG) or generation-heavy (chat).","Smaller groups cut error but add bits per weight and kernel complexity; *g* = 128 is the usual compromise, and *g* = 32 is worth it mainly below 4 bits or on small models.","PTQ is cheap and fast but depends on calibration data matching deployment; QAT is more robust at extreme bit-widths but costs a training run and an engineering pipeline most teams do not have.","GGUF runs on nearly anything with partial GPU offload and a single portable file, but a batching GPU engine with AWQ/GPTQ/FP8 kernels delivers far higher throughput per dollar for a multi-user service."],"pitfalls":["Evaluating only perplexity on a generic text set: perplexity can move by 0.1 while instruction following, maths or a minority language regress sharply; the symptom is a clean quantization report and a stream of user complaints. Run your task evals before and after.","Calibrating a code or multilingual model on English web text: the symptom is quality that looks fine on general prompts and degrades exactly in the deployment domain; GPTQ is more sensitive than AWQ here.","Expecting INT4 to cut time-to-first-token on long-prompt workloads: prefill is compute-bound and weight-only quantization does not reduce FLOPs; the symptom is unchanged or worse TTFT.","Sizing hardware at 4.0 bits per weight: scales and zero-points make it 4.2–5.5 depending on scheme, and the KV cache is extra; the symptom is an OOM on a card that \"should have fit\".","Re-quantizing an already quantized checkpoint (GPTQ → GGUF, or a merged QLoRA model) instead of starting from the highest-precision weights: errors compound; the symptom is a model noticeably worse than the same scheme applied to the bf16 original.","Losing or mangling tokenizer and chat-template metadata during GGUF conversion: the symptom is a model that rambles, never emits the end-of-turn token or ignores the system prompt despite correct weights."],"when":"On GPU servers, start with FP8 if the hardware supports it: near-zero quality cost, faster prefill and\ndecode. Go to INT4 AWQ or GPTQ when a model must fit a smaller or single GPU, or when decode latency at\nlow batch dominates, and verify on your evals. Choose GGUF when the target is a laptop, a CPU box, an\nedge device or a developer's machine, picking `Q4_K_M` or `Q5_K_M` as sensible defaults and moving up when\nquality evals demand it. Use QLoRA, not a serving format, when the goal is fine-tuning on a small GPU.\nMeasure three things every time: task evals against the unquantized model, tokens per second at your real\nbatch size, and total memory including the KV cache at your context length. In interviews, be able to\nderive bits per weight for a grouped scheme, explain why outliers break per-tensor scaling, and contrast\nhow GPTQ and AWQ decide what to protect.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the quantization scheme per deployment target, runs the calibration and the task evals, and checks the serving engine actually has a fast kernel for that format before shipping.","architect":"Owns the fit between model size, precision, GPU class and concurrency targets, and sets the rule that no quantized model ships without an eval delta against its full-precision parent.","fde":"Gets a customer's chosen model running on the hardware they already own, often a single 24–48 GB card or a CPU-only box, by choosing AWQ/GPTQ or a GGUF quant and proving quality on their data."},"check":{"q":"You must serve a 70B-class model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128) on a single 80 GB GPU with 8k\ncontext. Which precision do you pick, how much room is left for the KV cache, and what else do you check?","a":"bf16 needs 140 GB, so it is out; INT8 needs about 70 GB, leaving almost nothing for activations and\ncache; INT4 with group 128 is about 4.2 bits per weight, roughly 37 GB, leaving around 40 GB. The KV cache\nper token in fp16 is 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 ≈ 0.33 MB, so an 8k sequence costs about 2.6 GB and about 15\nconcurrent sequences fit, more with an 8-bit cache. I would use AWQ (or GPTQ with in-domain calibration),\nrun the task evals against the bf16 model on a second machine, check that the engine has a fast INT4 kernel\nfor this architecture, and confirm the workload is decode-heavy, because INT4 does nothing for prefill. If\nquality drops too far, the honest options are two GPUs with FP8 or a smaller model."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does weight-only INT4 quantization speed up decoding but not prefill?","options":["Prefill runs on the CPU in most engines","Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound, so reading fewer weight bytes helps; prefill is compute-bound and the matmul still runs in 16-bit","The KV cache is written in fp16 during prefill","INT4 kernels only support batch size 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Weight-only schemes dequantize to 16-bit inside the kernel, so FLOPs are unchanged. Decode reads all weights per token and benefits from fewer bytes; prefill is dominated by arithmetic over many tokens."},{"q":"How does AWQ decide which weights deserve protection?","options":["It keeps the largest-magnitude weights in fp16","It uses the Hessian of the reconstruction loss to rank weights","It looks at activation magnitudes per input channel and scales up the channels that carry large activations before rounding","It trains the weights with a straight-through estimator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"AWQ's observation is that saliency comes from the activations a weight multiplies, not the weight's own size. Rescaling those channels is output-preserving and shrinks their relative rounding error."},{"q":"What exactly is GGUF, and what does a GGUF file contain?","options":["A 4-bit quantization algorithm that replaces GPTQ","A single-file container used by llama.cpp holding per-tensor quantized weights plus tokenizer and architecture metadata","NVIDIA's FP8 format for Hopper GPUs","A calibration dataset for post-training quantization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GGUF is a file format: it bundles tensors in block-quantized types such as Q4_K_M with everything needed to run them. The quantization recipes are chosen when converting into it, not defined by it."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/pruning","sources":["Han et al., 2015 — Learning both Weights and Connections for Efficient Neural Networks","Frantar & Alistarh, 2023 — SparseGPT: Massive Language Models Can Be Accurately Pruned in One-Shot","Sun et al., 2023 — A Simple and Effective Pruning Approach for Large Language Models (Wanda)","Muralidharan et al., 2024 — Compact Language Models via Pruning and Knowledge Distillation"],"why":"Models are trained big because width and depth make optimisation easy and sample-efficient, not because\nevery parameter is needed at inference. The evidence of slack is old and consistent: many attention heads\ncan be removed after training with little loss, a sizeable fraction of middle transformer layers barely\nchange the residual stream they receive, and half the individual weights of a large model can be zeroed in\none shot with a modest perplexity increase. All that slack is paid for on every token you serve.\n\nThe naive route to a smaller model, training one from scratch at the target size, costs the full\npretraining budget, trillions of tokens, and gives you a model that was never as good as the big one.\nQuantization, the other obvious lever, shaves a constant factor on bytes but leaves parameter count and\nFLOPs unchanged, and it bottoms out around 4 bits. Pruning attacks the count itself: a pruned-and-repaired\nmodel starts from the parent's knowledge, so recovering most of its quality has been reported to need\ntens of times fewer tokens than training the small size fresh. It also composes with quantization, since\nfewer weights at fewer bits multiply.\n\nWhat makes pruning harder than it sounds is hardware. GPUs multiply dense matrices; a matrix that is 50%\nzeros in random places costs exactly the same to multiply as a full one unless the kernel knows the\npattern. So the central design question of pruning is not \"which weights are unimportant\" but \"which\n*shape* of removal can the hardware cash in\".","how":"### What you remove: the granularity ladder\n- **Unstructured** pruning zeroes individual weights anywhere. It preserves accuracy best at a given\n  sparsity because it has the most freedom, but the result is an irregular sparse matrix. Dense kernels\n  gain nothing, and storing it sparsely needs column indices whose overhead eats most of the saving at 50%;\n  general sparse kernels only beat dense ones at very high sparsity (around 90% and up). It is useful for\n  CPU runtimes with sparse support and for research, rarely for GPU serving.\n- **Semi-structured N:M** pruning, in practice **2:4**, zeroes exactly two of every four consecutive\n  weights. NVIDIA GPUs from Ampere onward have sparse tensor cores that execute this pattern at up to twice\n  the dense matmul rate, and store it as the two surviving values plus 2-bit position indices: per four\n  16-bit weights that is 2 × 16 + 2 × 2 = 36 bits instead of 64, about 56% of the dense size. The sparsity\n  is fixed at 50% and the constraint is local, so quality drops more than with free unstructured pruning\n  at the same ratio.\n- **Structured** pruning removes whole units: attention heads (the matching slices of W_Q, W_K, W_V and\n  W_O), MLP neurons (columns of the up/gate projections and the matching rows of the down projection),\n  hidden channels (hard, because the residual stream touches every layer), or entire layers (depth\n  pruning). The output is a smaller *dense* model, so every kernel on every device gets faster with no\n  special support. The price is the coarsest constraint and therefore the largest quality hit per\n  parameter removed, which is why structured pruning is almost always followed by recovery training.\n\n### How you decide: importance scores\n- **Magnitude** (|w|): the classic criterion, cheap and surprisingly good after training with weight\n  decay. On large LLMs it fails in one shot at 50% because of outlier features: a small weight sitting on\n  an input channel that always carries huge activations is important, and magnitude cannot see that.\n- **Activation-aware** (Wanda): score each weight by |W_ij| · ‖X_j‖₂, its magnitude times the norm of\n  the input feature it multiplies over a few calibration sequences, and prune the lowest scores *within\n  each output row* so every output keeps the same number of inputs. No gradients, no retraining, seconds\n  per layer, and it matches far more expensive methods at 50% on large models precisely because it fixes\n  the outlier blind spot.\n- **Second-order** (SparseGPT): the same Optimal Brain Surgeon machinery GPTQ uses. For each layer,\n  prune the weight whose removal least increases the reconstruction error ‖WX − ŴX‖², then update the\n  remaining weights in the row using the inverse Hessian **H⁻¹** = (XXᵀ)⁻¹ to compensate. Done\n  column-wise with shared Hessian updates, it prunes 100B+ models to 50–60% unstructured or 2:4 in a few\n  hours on one GPU, with better accuracy than Wanda at the cost of the Hessian work.\n- **Gradient / Taylor** scores (|w · ∂L/∂w| and group versions) estimate the loss change from removing a\n  unit and are the usual choice for heads and neurons. For layers, a cheap and effective proxy is how\n  little a block changes its input: compute the cosine similarity between a layer's input and output\n  hidden states on calibration data; blocks with similarity near 1 are nearly identity maps and can be\n  dropped first. The first and last few layers almost never qualify.\n\n### When you prune and how you recover\nOne-shot methods (Wanda, SparseGPT) prune a finished model and stop; they are the right tool for a quick\n50% result. Classic iterative pruning alternates prune → fine-tune → prune with a sparsity schedule that\nramps gradually, because the network needs time to reroute around each cut. For structured pruning of LLMs\nthe recipe that works is prune, then **distil from the unpruned parent**: the pruned model is an excellent\ninitialisation, and token-level distillation provides a dense signal that repairs it with a small\nfraction of a pretraining budget. Reported findings from that line of work: width pruning (heads, MLP,\nembedding channels) tends to preserve quality better than depth pruning at equal parameter count, while\ndepth pruning gives the bigger latency win because fewer sequential layers means fewer kernel launches\nper token. Mixture-of-experts can be read as learned, input-dependent structured sparsity, which is where\nmost of the industry's compute savings actually came from; see that lesson.","example":"Wanda-style pruning of one linear layer in PyTorch, compared with magnitude pruning, in both unstructured\n(per-row 50%) and 2:4 form. The calibration activations include a few outlier channels, which is exactly\nthe case where magnitude scores go wrong and activation-aware scores do not.\n\n```python\nimport torch\ntorch.manual_seed(0)\nd_in, d_out, n_tok = 1024, 1024, 4096\nW = torch.randn(d_out, d_in) * 0.02                # [out, in]\nX = torch.randn(n_tok, d_in)\nX[:, torch.randperm(d_in)[:8]] *= 30                # a few scattered outlier input channels, as in real LLMs\n\ndef prune_rows(W, score, sparsity=0.5):\n    \"\"\"Zero the lowest-scoring fraction of weights within each output row (Wanda's comparison group).\"\"\"\n    k = int(W.shape[1] * sparsity)\n    idx = score.argsort(dim=1)[:, :k]\n    mask = torch.ones_like(W, dtype=torch.bool).scatter_(1, idx, False)\n    return W * mask\n\ndef prune_2of4(W, score):\n    \"\"\"Keep the two highest-scoring weights in every block of four along the input dimension.\"\"\"\n    s = score.view(W.shape[0], -1, 4)\n    keep = torch.zeros_like(s, dtype=torch.bool).scatter_(2, s.topk(2, dim=2).indices, True)\n    return W * keep.view_as(W)\n\nref = X @ W.T\nmagnitude = W.abs()                                 # |W_ij|\nwanda = W.abs() * X.norm(dim=0)                     # |W_ij| * ||X_j||_2\nfor name, Wp in [(\"magnitude 50%\", prune_rows(W, magnitude)), (\"wanda 50%\", prune_rows(W, wanda)),\n                 (\"magnitude 2:4\", prune_2of4(W, magnitude)), (\"wanda 2:4\", prune_2of4(W, wanda))]:\n    density = (Wp != 0).float().mean().item()\n    err = ((X @ Wp.T - ref).norm() / ref.norm()).item()\n    print(f\"{name:14s} density={density:.2f} relative output error={err:.3f}\")\n# Expect wanda to beat magnitude clearly: magnitude happily deletes small weights on the outlier\n# channels, which carry most of the output. Same density, very different damage.\n# In a real model you would score every linear layer with a few hundred calibration sequences,\n# then measure perplexity and task evals; for structured cuts, follow with distillation from W.\n```","tradeoffs":["Unstructured pruning gives the best accuracy per removed weight but almost no GPU speedup; it only pays on sparse-aware CPU runtimes or at extreme sparsity, so for GPU serving it flips to being a reporting exercise rather than a cost saving.","2:4 sparsity can run matmuls at up to twice the dense rate on supporting GPUs (realised gains are smaller and kernel-dependent) and cuts weight bytes to about 56%, but it is a fixed 50%, usually needs recovery training, and only helps decode if the kernel reads the compressed form; on hardware without sparse tensor cores it is pure quality loss.","Structured pruning speeds up everything everywhere, but costs more quality per parameter and needs the most recovery compute; depth pruning is cheapest to implement and best for latency, width pruning preserves quality better at the same size.","Prune-then-distil needs a small fraction of the tokens that training a fresh small model would, but the result inherits the parent's architecture, tokenizer and biases, and its ceiling is the parent.","Stacking pruning with quantization multiplies savings, but the errors compound, so prune and recover first, quantize last, and evaluate at every stage."],"pitfalls":["Reporting sparsity as if it were speedup: a 50% unstructured-sparse model runs at identical tokens per second in a dense engine; the symptom is a proud \"50% smaller\" slide and an unchanged GPU bill.","Calibrating on data unlike the deployment traffic: scores are activation-dependent, so the symptom is fine perplexity on web text and broken behaviour on code or chat.","Removing layers by position rules of thumb without measuring block influence, including the first or last layers: the symptom is degenerate, repetitive output even at a small depth cut.","Skipping the recovery fine-tune because perplexity looked acceptable: perplexity and downstream ability diverge after pruning, so the symptom is intact next-token loss and collapsed instruction following or reasoning on your evals.","Width-pruning one side of a paired matrix (dropping MLP up-projection columns without the matching down-projection rows, or query heads without accounting for grouped KV heads): the symptom is a shape error if you are lucky and silent garbage if you are not.","Assuming pruning is behaviour-neutral: safety and refusal behaviour can be disproportionately fragile under pruning, so the symptom is a smaller model that complies with requests its parent refused. Re-run the safety suite, not just the capability suite."],"when":"Prune when you need a model size that does not exist in the family you have standardised on, when you have\na strong parent and some training budget for recovery, or when the hardware has 2:4 support and the\nworkload is matmul-heavy. Prefer quantization first; it is cheaper and usually reaches the memory target\nalone, and prune only when latency or parameter count, not bytes, is the constraint. Measure tokens per\nsecond and task evals at the deployment batch size, never sparsity percentages, and keep the KV cache in\nthe picture: pruning query heads does not shrink it, pruning KV heads or layers does. In day-to-day work\npruning shows up as a model-shrinking project with a distillation phase attached; in interviews, expect to\nexplain why unstructured sparsity does not speed up a GPU, how Wanda differs from magnitude pruning, and\nwhat 2:4 actually buys.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs one-shot pruning with calibration data from production prompts, wires the recovery distillation run, and benchmarks real tokens per second against the dense parent before claiming a win.","architect":"Decides whether the size gap is better closed by pruning a known-good parent or by adopting a different model, weighing recovery compute against the operational cost of a new family.","fde":"Rarely prunes in the field, but must recognise a vendor's \"50% sparse\" claim for what it is and ask which hardware and kernel path turns that into latency the customer will feel."},"check":{"q":"A product needs decode latency about 30% lower than its 8B model delivers on A100s, with no budget\nfor a different GPU. Which pruning options are realistic, and in what order would you try them?","a":"Decode is bandwidth-bound, so the target is fewer bytes read per token, and the first option is not\npruning at all: INT4 or INT8 weights cut bytes more than 30% with no training, so quantize first and\nmeasure. If latency is still short, depth pruning is the direct lever, since dropping a quarter of the\nlayers removes a quarter of the sequential work and the bytes; measure block influence by input/output\ncosine similarity, remove the least influential middle layers, and distil from the unpruned 8B to recover.\n2:4 sparsity is tempting on A100s, but its decode benefit depends on the engine reading the compressed\nformat and it needs recovery training too, so I would try it only after depth pruning. Throughout, I would\ntrack task evals and safety evals, not perplexity, and verify the KV cache budget is unchanged."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does 50% unstructured sparsity usually not make a GPU model faster?","options":["Zero weights still consume registers during attention","Dense kernels multiply the zeros anyway, and sparse formats need index overhead and specialised kernels that only pay off at much higher sparsity","The KV cache grows to compensate for the missing weights","Sparse models cannot be quantized"],"answer":1,"explanation":"GPUs execute dense matmuls regardless of zero values. Cashing in sparsity needs either hardware-known patterns such as 2:4 or structural removal that yields a smaller dense matrix."},{"q":"What does Wanda's importance score multiply together?","options":["Weight magnitude and the weight's gradient","Weight magnitude and the inverse Hessian diagonal","Weight magnitude and the L2 norm of the input activation feature it multiplies","Output activation variance and layer depth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wanda scores |W_ij| · ‖X_j‖₂ so that small weights on outlier input channels are protected, which is the case where pure magnitude pruning does the most damage in large language models."},{"q":"After structurally pruning a large model, what is the most token-efficient way to recover quality?","options":["Retrain the pruned architecture from random initialisation on the original corpus","Distil from the unpruned parent, using the pruned weights as the initialisation","Quantize it to 4-bit so the remaining weights are used more efficiently","Increase the learning rate and run one more epoch of SFT"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The pruned model already sits near a good solution, and token-level distillation gives a dense signal, so recovery reportedly needs a small fraction of the tokens a fresh small model would require."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/model-merging","sources":["Wortsman et al., 2022 — Model soups: averaging weights of multiple fine-tuned models improves accuracy without increasing inference time","Ilharco et al., 2023 — Editing Models with Task Arithmetic","Yadav et al., 2023 — TIES-Merging: Resolving Interference When Merging Models","Yu et al., 2023 — Language Models are Super Mario: Absorbing Abilities from Homologous Models as a Free Lunch"],"why":"Suppose you have three fine-tunes of the same 8B base: one for your support domain, one that follows tool\ncall formats, and a community instruct tune with far better conversational quality than yours. Serving\nall three means three copies of the weights, a router, and no way for one answer to use two skills at\nonce. Training one multi-task model instead means obtaining all three datasets (the community one is not\nyours to have), rebuilding the mix every time one dataset changes, and paying for a training run per\niteration. Ensembling costs three forward passes per token.\n\nMerging offers a fourth option: because every one of those models is `base + small delta`, you can add the\ndeltas and get a single model in minutes on a CPU. The reason this is not absurd is empirical and\nwell-replicated: models fine-tuned from the same pretrained weights stay in the same basin of the loss\nlandscape, so the straight line between them has no loss barrier (linear mode connectivity), and\naveraging two good solutions tends to land on a third good one that is often better than either. Model\nsoups showed that averaging fine-tunes with different hyperparameters improved accuracy and\nout-of-distribution robustness at zero inference cost. Task arithmetic showed the deltas behave like\nvectors you can add, scale and even negate to remove a behaviour.\n\nThe naive version, plain averaging, breaks as soon as the tasks disagree about a weight: one tune pushes it\nup, another pushes it down, and the average cancels both. The later methods are all answers to that\ninterference problem.","how":"### Task vectors\nFor a base θ_base and a fine-tune θ_i, the task vector is τ_i = θ_i − θ_base, computed tensor by tensor.\nTask arithmetic forms θ = θ_base + λ Σ τ_i for multi-task merging, θ_base − λ τ for forgetting (negating a\ntoxicity fine-tune reduces toxicity), and even analogies. λ is a single scalar (or one per task) tuned on\na validation set; because the deltas are additive approximations, λ around 0.3–1.0 in total is where\nthings usually work, and larger sums push activations out of the range the base was trained in.\n\n### Linear averaging and soups\nθ = Σ w_i θ_i with Σ w_i = 1. Uniform soups average everything; greedy soups add a model only if the\nvalidation metric improves. This is the right tool when the models learned the *same* thing in slightly\ndifferent ways (same data, different seeds or learning rates), and checkpoint averaging at the end of a\nlong training run is the same idea applied along one trajectory. It is the wrong tool when the models\nlearned different things.\n\n### SLERP\nSpherical linear interpolation treats each tensor (or the whole flattened model) as a vector and\ninterpolates along the arc between the two, with cos Ω = a·b / (‖a‖‖b‖) and\n\n```\nslerp(a, b, t) = sin((1 − t)Ω) / sin Ω · a  +  sin(tΩ) / sin Ω · b\n```\n\nThe point is norm preservation: a straight-line midpoint between two vectors at an angle is *shorter* than\neither, which shrinks activation scales layer by layer; SLERP keeps the magnitude. It only handles two\nmodels at a time and is the usual choice for blending two closely related instruct tunes.\n\n### TIES-Merging: trim, elect sign, disjoint merge\nTIES names two sources of interference. First, most entries of a task vector are tiny noise that still\nperturb other tasks when summed. Second, sign conflicts: when τ_A and τ_B disagree on direction, averaging\ncancels useful movement. The method does three things, in order, because each removes one failure mode:\n\n1. **Trim.** Keep only the top *k*% of each τ_i by magnitude (20% is a common setting); zero the rest.\n   Fine-tuning changes concentrate in a few parameters, so this removes noise without losing much.\n2. **Elect.** For each parameter, choose the sign carrying more total magnitude across tasks:\n   sign(Σ_i τ_i) after trimming. This decides which direction the merged model will move.\n3. **Disjoint merge.** Average only the task vectors that agree with the elected sign at that parameter,\n   ignoring the dissenters instead of letting them cancel the majority. Then θ = θ_base + λ τ_merged.\n\n### DARE: drop and rescale\nDARE starts from the observation that SFT deltas are extremely redundant: you can set a random 90% (or\nmore, on large models) of a task vector's entries to zero, multiply the survivors by 1/(1 − p), and the\nmodel's behaviour barely changes, because the rescaling keeps the expected delta identical and the\nredundancy covers the dropped entries. Sparsified deltas from different tasks collide far less often, so\nDARE is used as a preprocessing step before summing or before TIES (\"dare_ties\"). It works when deltas\nare small relative to the base; it fails on continued-pretraining deltas, which are large and not\nredundant.\n\n### Passthrough and frankenmerges\nStacking layers from different models, or repeating a block of layers to make a deeper model (depth\nup-scaling), is merging without arithmetic. The result is broken until it is healed with continued\ntraining, so this is a model-building technique rather than a free combination step.\n\n### Constraints that follow from the mechanism\nEverything above is elementwise arithmetic on tensors, so it presumes the same architecture, the same\ntensor shapes (hence the same tokenizer and vocabulary size) and the same base lineage. Two fine-tunes of\ndifferent base checkpoints have deltas measured from different origins; two models trained from different\nrandom initialisations are related by hidden permutations of their neurons and average into noise unless\naligned first. LoRA adapters are task vectors restricted to a low-rank subspace, so adapter merging (a\nweighted sum of B_iA_i products, with the same TIES/DARE options) is the same technique with the same\ninterference problems. And because nothing is trained, nothing is verified: every merge needs the full\nevaluation suite, including the chat template, end-of-turn behaviour and safety checks, before anyone\ncalls it a model.","example":"A compact TIES merge over Hugging Face state dicts in PyTorch. All checkpoints must share the base model\nand tensor shapes. It works tensor by tensor, so peak memory is one tensor times the number of models, and\nit falls back to copying non-float buffers unchanged.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef ties_merge(base, finetunes, k=0.2, lam=1.0):\n    \"\"\"base, finetunes: state dicts (name -> tensor) of the same architecture and base lineage.\n    Returns base + lam * TIES-merged task vector.\"\"\"\n    merged = {}\n    for name, w0 in base.items():\n        if not w0.is_floating_point():                       # token ids, buffers: pass through\n            merged[name] = w0.clone(); continue\n        taus = torch.stack([ft[name].float() - w0.float() for ft in finetunes])   # [n_tasks, ...]\n        # 1. Trim: keep the top-k fraction of each task vector by magnitude\n        flat = taus.abs().flatten(1)\n        n_keep = max(1, int(k * flat.shape[1]))\n        thresh = flat.kthvalue(flat.shape[1] - n_keep + 1, dim=1).values  # per-task cutoff\n        taus = taus * (taus.abs() >= thresh.view(-1, *([1] * (taus.dim() - 1))))\n        # 2. Elect: the sign with more total magnitude at each parameter wins\n        sign = torch.sign(taus.sum(0))\n        # 3. Disjoint merge: average only the entries that agree with the elected sign\n        agree = (torch.sign(taus) == sign) & (taus != 0)\n        tau = (taus * agree).sum(0) / agree.sum(0).clamp(min=1)\n        merged[name] = (w0.float() + lam * tau).to(w0.dtype)\n    return merged\n\n# usage sketch (every path must be a fine-tune of exactly the same base revision):\n# from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\n# base = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"org/base-8b\").state_dict()\n# fts = [AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(p).state_dict() for p in (\"org/support-sft\", \"org/tools-sft\")]\n# model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"org/base-8b\"); model.load_state_dict(ties_merge(base, fts))\n# Then: run the per-task evals, a general benchmark, the chat-template smoke test and the safety suite.\n# mergekit expresses the same recipe as a YAML config (merge_method: ties, density: 0.2, weight: ...).\n```","tradeoffs":["Zero training compute and no access to the original datasets, but also zero training signal: nothing guarantees the merged model works, so evaluation effort replaces training effort; when you do own the data and compute, multi-task SFT is the more reliable path.","One model for several skills saves GPUs and routing complexity, but interference grows with the number of tasks and the size of their deltas; past a handful of tasks, per-task quality usually slips below the specialists.","Plain averaging improves robustness when models are near-duplicates of each other, but it cancels conflicting updates; TIES and DARE fix that at the cost of hyperparameters (k, p, λ) that need a validation set to tune.","SLERP preserves norms and often beats linear interpolation for two models, but does not extend to many models; linear and TIES scale to N at the cost of norm drift.","Task-vector negation can remove unwanted behaviour cheaply, but the same mechanism can strip safety tuning, intentionally or by accident, which makes merged models a governance problem as much as a technical one."],"pitfalls":["Merging fine-tunes of different base revisions, or treating an instruct model as the base for deltas computed against the pretrained one: the symptom is incoherent or garbage output, because the deltas are measured from the wrong origin.","Vocabulary mismatch from added special tokens: embedding and output-head shapes differ, so the symptom is a shape error, or, if someone pads the tensors, wrong tokens at generation time.","Weights that sum well above one, or λ too high: the symptom is repetitive, degenerate text and logits that blow up, because activations leave the range the base model expects.","Each parent learned its own chat template: the merged model gets a mixture, so the symptom is leaked role tags, never emitting end-of-turn, or ignoring the system prompt.","Trusting leaderboard scores for merges: benchmark contamination accumulates across parents, so the symptom is a model that tops a public board and underperforms its parents on your tasks.","Skipping the safety regression: merging can quietly undo refusal training; the symptom is a model that complies with requests every parent refused, discovered by a user rather than by a test."],"when":"Merge when you have several fine-tunes of the same base and need one artifact, when you want to borrow a\ncommunity tune's conversational quality for your domain model without its data, when you are averaging\ncheckpoints or seeds from your own run, or when you want a fast experiment before committing to a\nmulti-task training job. Do not merge as a substitute for training when you own the data, and never ship a\nmerge that has not been through the per-task evals, a general benchmark, a template smoke test and the\nsafety suite. Measure each parent's task metric before and after, and treat any drop as the cost of the\nmerge, not noise. In interviews, be ready to explain task vectors, why TIES elects a sign, why DARE's\nrescaling preserves behaviour, and why two models from different initialisations cannot simply be averaged.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes task vectors, tunes density and λ on a validation set, and runs the full eval and safety suite on the merged checkpoint before registering it like any trained model.","architect":"Decides whether a merged generalist or routed specialists better fit the serving budget and risk posture, and sets provenance rules for which parent checkpoints may be merged into production.","fde":"Combines a customer's domain fine-tune with a stronger instruct tune when retraining is not an option on site, and proves the result against the customer's own regression suite."},"check":{"q":"A team has a support-domain SFT of an 8B base and wants the conversational quality of a newer\ncommunity instruct tune built on the same base. What are their options, and how would you merge safely?","a":"Options are routing between the two models, retraining on the instruct data (unavailable), or\nmerging. For merging, first confirm both checkpoints derive from the exact same base revision and share\nthe tokenizer and vocabulary size. Compute both task vectors against the base, then try TIES with density\naround 0.2 and λ near 1, and SLERP between the two as a second candidate. Tune on a validation set holding\nsupport tickets and general chat prompts. Evaluate per-task metrics against each parent, a general\nbenchmark for collateral damage, a chat-template smoke test for end-of-turn behaviour, and the safety\nsuite, because merging can erode refusals. Ship only if support quality holds within tolerance and the\nconversational gain is measurable; otherwise fall back to routing."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does TIES-Merging elect a sign per parameter before averaging?","options":["To make the merged weights sparse enough for fast kernels","Because task vectors that disagree in direction cancel under plain averaging, so only the majority direction is kept","Because negative deltas are usually noise","To normalise each task vector to unit length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sign conflicts are a main source of interference: averaging +0.3 and −0.3 yields zero and loses both tasks' progress. Electing the dominant sign and averaging only agreeing entries preserves movement."},{"q":"DARE drops 90% of a task vector's entries and rescales the rest by 10×. Why does the model still work?","options":["Because 90% of fine-tuned weights are exactly zero already","Because the base model ignores small deltas","Because SFT deltas are highly redundant and rescaling keeps the expected delta unchanged","Because dropped entries are recovered during the first inference pass"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Random dropping with 1/(1 − p) rescaling is unbiased, and small fine-tuning deltas carry redundant information, so the behaviour survives; the method fails on large, non-redundant continued-pretraining deltas."},{"q":"Which pair can be safely merged by weight averaging?","options":["Two 7B models of the same architecture trained from different random initialisations","Two fine-tunes that started from the identical pretrained checkpoint","A 7B and a 13B model from the same family","Any two models that share a tokenizer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Linear mode connectivity holds for models sharing an initialisation. Independently trained models have permuted hidden units and different basins, and different sizes do not even have matching tensor shapes."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/data-curation","sources":["Lee et al., 2022 — Deduplicating Training Data Makes Language Models Better","Zhou et al., 2023 — LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment","Rae et al., 2021 — Scaling Language Models: Methods, Analysis & Insights from Training Gopher","Penedo et al., 2024 — The FineWeb Datasets: Decanting the Web for the Finest Text Data at Scale"],"why":"A language model is a compressed copy of its training distribution. That sentence sounds harmless until\nyou look at what a raw crawl or a raw log export contains: navigation menus repeated across ten thousand\npages, the same licence header in every file, cookie banners, SEO spam, half-translated product listings,\ntest questions from the benchmarks you plan to report, customer emails with phone numbers, and support\ntranscripts where the agent's \"resolution\" was wrong. Train on it and the model learns all of it with\nequal diligence. Duplicates are the quietest problem: a passage repeated a thousand times is not \"more\nevidence\", it is a thousand gradient steps toward memorising that passage, and the reported effect of\ndeduplicating a web corpus was both better perplexity and an order-of-magnitude drop in verbatim\nregurgitation.\n\nThe naive response, \"get more data\", makes it worse: more of the same distribution, more compute burned\non repeats, and no fix for contamination. The second naive response, \"the model is big enough to figure\nout what is good\", is contradicted by every published ablation: the same architecture trained on a\nfiltered subset of a corpus beats the one trained on the whole corpus on knowledge and reasoning\nbenchmarks, by margins that would otherwise take a substantially larger model or far more tokens to buy.\n\nFor fine-tuning the lesson is even sharper. LIMA fine-tuned a 65B model on about a thousand curated\nprompt–response pairs and was competitive with models trained on orders of magnitude more, the argument\nbeing that alignment mostly teaches format and style, and that a small set with diverse prompts and\nuniformly excellent responses teaches it cleanly, while a huge noisy set teaches the average of its\nnoise. Curation is the lever that decides what the model becomes; architecture decides how well it can\nbecome it.","how":"A curation pipeline is a sequence of filters, each cheap enough to run on everything that survives the\nprevious one, with the expensive judgement calls last. The order matters because every stage is also a\nplace where good data is lost, so you measure drop rates per stage and sample the rejects by hand.\n\n### 1. Provenance and permission\nRecord source, licence, collection date and a content hash for every document before anything else,\nbecause you will need to delete by source (a takedown, a customer leaving), reweight by source, and\nexplain where a behaviour came from. Strip or pseudonymise PII at this stage with pattern rules plus a\nnamed-entity model; a model trained on real emails will emit real emails.\n\n### 2. Extraction and format normalisation\nPull main text out of HTML, fix encodings, identify language with a fast classifier and keep the ones you\nintend to serve. For fine-tuning data, parse every example into one fixed structure (system, user,\nassistant turns) and one template, and drop anything that does not parse, because the model learns the\ntemplate as rigidly as the content.\n\n### 3. Heuristic quality filters\nCheap rules that remove boilerplate with high precision: document length bounds, mean word length in a\nsane range, a cap on the symbol-to-word ratio, a cap on the fraction of lines that are bullets or that\nend in an ellipsis, a requirement that common function words appear, a requirement that lines end in\nterminal punctuation. These are the Gopher and C4 style rules, and they work because machine-generated\njunk and navigation chrome fail them while prose passes. They are deliberately blunt; some rules\n(bad-word lists, punctuation requirements) over-remove dialects and informal text, which is a bias you\nmust know you are introducing.\n\n### 4. Deduplication\nExact duplicates fall to a hash of normalised text. Near-duplicates need MinHash: represent each document\nas its set of word 5-gram shingles, apply *k* hash functions (128 is typical) and keep each function's\nminimum, which gives a signature whose per-position match probability equals the Jaccard similarity of\nthe shingle sets. Comparing all pairs is O(n²), impossible at billions of documents, so locality-sensitive\nhashing splits the signature into *b* bands of *r* rows and only compares documents that collide in at\nleast one band. The probability two documents with similarity *J* become candidates is\n1 − (1 − Jʳ)ᵇ: with *r* = 8, *b* = 16 that is about 0.95 at *J* = 0.8 and 0.06 at *J* = 0.5, an S-curve\nwith its threshold near (1/*b*)^(1/*r*) ≈ 0.71. Within-document repeated substrings (boilerplate pasted\ninto otherwise unique pages) need a suffix-array pass. For fine-tuning sets, deduplicate *prompts*, not\njust whole examples, and add an embedding-based pass to catch paraphrases.\n\n### 5. Decontamination\nIndex every evaluation set you will ever report and remove training documents that share long n-grams\nwith any of them; 13-gram overlap was the GPT-3 rule and 8–13 remains common. Do this for your internal\ngolden sets as well as public benchmarks, because the goal is a valid measurement, not a clean public\nrecord. N-gram overlap misses paraphrases and translations, so a similarity search against eval\nembeddings is a worthwhile second pass.\n\n### 6. Model-based quality scoring\nHeuristics cannot judge content. Train a small classifier on \"good\" versus \"random\" (early LLM corpora used\nreference text as the positive class), or have a strong LLM score a sample for educational value and\ndistil that into a fast classifier run over everything, as FineWeb-Edu did; keeping only high scores gave\nlarge gains on knowledge benchmarks. The cost is the labeller's taste imported at scale: a classifier\ntuned for textbook prose will quietly down-weight code, forums and dialects unless you score those\ndomains separately.\n\n### 7. Mixing and curriculum\nDecide weights per source (web, code, books, maths, your domain). Small high-quality sources are\nup-sampled, with the caveat that repeating data beyond a few epochs returns little; learned weighting\nwith a proxy model exists but most teams tune mixes empirically on a small run. Pretraining runs commonly\nanneal onto the highest-quality slice at the end. For fine-tuning, balance task types and response lengths\nto match what you want the model to do in production, because the model learns the length distribution\nas surely as the content.\n\n### 8. Fine-tuning specifics\nVerify responses where you can (execute code, check answers), remove hedging or refusals you do not want,\nenforce one style, check inter-annotator agreement on a sample, and look at the data: a few hundred\nexamples read by a human expose systematic problems no metric reports. Then version the dataset, pin it to\nthe model it produced, and feed error analysis from evals back into the next version.","example":"A compact curation pass over raw documents with Gopher-style heuristics, exact deduplication, MinHash/LSH\nnear-deduplication via `datasketch`, and 13-gram decontamination against evaluation sets. Each stage\nreturns early so you can count what it dropped.\n\n```python\nimport re, hashlib\nfrom datasketch import MinHash, MinHashLSH\n\ndef shingles(text, n=5):\n    toks = re.findall(r\"\\w+\", text.lower())\n    return {\" \".join(toks[i:i + n]) for i in range(max(1, len(toks) - n + 1))}\n\ndef passes_heuristics(text):\n    w = re.findall(r\"\\w+\", text)\n    if not 50 <= len(w) <= 100_000: return False                   # length bounds\n    if not 3 <= sum(map(len, w)) / len(w) <= 10: return False      # mean word length\n    lines = [l for l in text.splitlines() if l.strip()]\n    if sum(l.lstrip().startswith((\"-\", \"*\", \"•\")) for l in lines) > 0.9 * len(lines): return False\n    if sum(l.rstrip().endswith(\"...\") for l in lines) > 0.3 * len(lines): return False\n    return True\n\ndef curate(docs, eval_texts, num_perm=128, threshold=0.7):\n    eval_grams = set().union(*(shingles(t, 13) for t in eval_texts))   # decontamination index\n    seen, lsh, kept, dropped = set(), MinHashLSH(threshold=threshold, num_perm=num_perm), [], {}\n    for i, text in enumerate(docs):\n        if not passes_heuristics(text): dropped[\"heuristic\"] = dropped.get(\"heuristic\", 0) + 1; continue\n        h = hashlib.sha1(\" \".join(text.lower().split()).encode()).hexdigest()\n        if h in seen: dropped[\"exact\"] = dropped.get(\"exact\", 0) + 1; continue\n        seen.add(h)\n        if shingles(text, 13) & eval_grams: dropped[\"contaminated\"] = dropped.get(\"contaminated\", 0) + 1; continue\n        m = MinHash(num_perm=num_perm)\n        for g in shingles(text): m.update(g.encode())\n        if lsh.query(m): dropped[\"near_dup\"] = dropped.get(\"near_dup\", 0) + 1; continue\n        lsh.insert(str(i), m)\n        kept.append(text)\n    return kept, dropped\n\n# kept, dropped = curate(raw_docs, eval_texts); print(len(kept), dropped)\n# Then: read 100 random rejects per stage and 100 random keeps, run a quality classifier on the keeps,\n# and only afterwards split train/validation, so near-duplicates cannot straddle the split.\n```","tradeoffs":["Aggressive filtering raises average quality but shrinks volume and narrows diversity (dialects, code, niche domains); when you are data-constrained, a cleaner smaller set repeated a few times beats a larger noisy one, but beyond a few repeats more fresh data wins again.","A low near-duplicate threshold removes legitimate templated text such as contracts and forms, which you may want the model to learn; a high threshold keeps near-copies that drive memorisation. Tune per source.","Model-based scoring judges content far better than heuristics but imports the labeller's preferences at scale, costs a classifier pass over everything, and can over-select benchmark-like prose.","Decontamination protects eval validity, but it also deletes genuinely useful documents that happen to be quoted in benchmarks, and it cannot catch paraphrased leaks without an embedding pass.","A small curated SFT set is cheap and effective for teaching format, tone and task structure, but it cannot inject much new knowledge; for that, volume and pretraining-style data come back into play."],"pitfalls":["Deduplicating only exact matches: near-duplicate boilerplate survives, so the symptom is a model that regurgitates licence headers, cookie notices and canned openings, and inflated validation scores.","Splitting train/validation before deduplicating: near-duplicates straddle the split, so validation loss looks excellent and production performance does not match it.","Selecting examples by length or by surface polish: the model learns to be long and ornate, so the symptom is responses that grew after fine-tuning without any gain on task metrics.","Mixing templates (some examples with system prompts, some without; two different turn formats): the symptom is unpredictable adherence to instructions depending on the prompt's shape.","Decontaminating against public benchmarks only: the internal golden set leaks in, so your own numbers are the ones you cannot trust.","Training on logs without PII scrubbing: the symptom is the model emitting real names, emails or account numbers, found by a customer or an auditor.","Discarding rejected examples without the reason: you cannot audit why a behaviour is missing, tune a threshold, or explain to a reviewer what was excluded."],"when":"Curation is not optional; what varies is the effort. A one-off fine-tune on a few thousand examples\ndeserves a human read of all of them, a dedup pass and template enforcement. A production pipeline that\nregenerates training sets from logs deserves the full staged pipeline with per-stage drop metrics,\ncontamination checks against every eval set, PII scrubbing and dataset versioning. Measure drop rate per\nstage, duplicate rate, contamination hits, inter-annotator agreement on labels, and the downstream eval\ndelta between dataset versions, which is the only number that matters in the end. Day to day this is\nscripts over JSONL, a dashboard of stage counts and a habit of reading samples; in interviews, expect to\nexplain MinHash and LSH, why deduplication reduces memorisation, what LIMA showed, and how you would\ndecontaminate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the filter, dedup and decontamination code, versions each dataset with its stage counts, and reads samples of what every stage rejected before trusting the pipeline.","architect":"Sets the data policy: licensing and PII rules, which eval sets are protected by decontamination, retention and deletion paths, and the versioning contract between datasets and models.","fde":"Turns a customer's messy export (tickets, documents, logs) into a clean, deduplicated, de-identified training set inside their environment and documents exactly what was removed and why."},"check":{"q":"You inherit 200,000 customer support conversations to fine-tune a drafting model. What do you do\nbefore you train, and what do you measure to know the data is ready?","a":"First provenance and PII: confirm the data may be used, then scrub names, emails, phone and account\nnumbers. Parse every conversation into one turn template and drop the unparseable. Filter by heuristics\n(empty or one-line replies, macros, auto-responses) and by outcome where available, keeping conversations\nwith good customer ratings or verified resolutions. Deduplicate exactly, then near-deduplicate with\nMinHash on both whole conversations and customer prompts, since macros produce thousands of near-copies.\nDecontaminate against the evaluation set built with the support leads. Read a few hundred examples with\nan SME and label a sample for quality to check agreement. Balance ticket categories and response lengths\nto the production distribution, then version the set. I would measure per-stage drop counts, duplicate\nrate, contamination hits, agreement on the quality sample, and finally the eval delta versus the base\nmodel, which decides whether the data is ready."},"quiz":[{"q":"What problem do MinHash and locality-sensitive hashing solve together?","options":["Removing PII from documents at scale","Finding near-duplicate documents without comparing every pair","Scoring documents for educational quality","Measuring n-gram overlap with evaluation sets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MinHash signatures estimate Jaccard similarity, and LSH banding only compares documents that collide in a band, turning an O(n²) pairwise search into a near-linear candidate-generation step."},{"q":"Why can 13-gram overlap decontamination still miss contamination?","options":["Because 13 is too short to match any real sentence","Because paraphrased or translated copies of a test item share no long exact n-gram with it","Because evaluation sets are never public","Because n-gram checks only run on the validation split"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Exact n-gram matching catches verbatim leaks. A benchmark question rewritten in different words, or translated, carries the same answer with no shared 13-gram, so an embedding-similarity pass is needed."},{"q":"What did LIMA demonstrate about fine-tuning data?","options":["That more instruction data always improves alignment","That synthetic data outperforms human-written data","That around a thousand diverse, uniformly high-quality examples can teach format and style competitively","That pretraining data should be deduplicated before fine-tuning"],"answer":2,"explanation":"LIMA's claim is that alignment mostly teaches format and style, so a small set with diverse prompts and excellent responses suffices; large noisy sets teach the average of their noise."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/synthetic-data-generation","sources":["Wang et al., 2022 — Self-Instruct: Aligning Language Models with Self-Generated Instructions","Zelikman et al., 2022 — STaR: Bootstrapping Reasoning With Reasoning","Xu et al., 2023 — WizardLM: Empowering Large Language Models to Follow Complex Instructions","Shumailov et al., 2024 — AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data"],"why":"Real labelled data has three problems that no budget fully fixes. It is scarce where you need it most:\nthe long tail of odd inputs that break a product appears once a month in logs and never in a scraped\ncorpus. It is private: a customer's internal forms, ticket formats and tool outputs exist nowhere public,\nand often cannot leave their environment. And it rarely contains what you want the model to *do*: humans\ndo not write out step-by-step reasoning, preference comparisons between two drafts, or tool-call\nsequences, so the behaviours modern post-training needs have no natural corpus.\n\nGeneration is cheap; a model can produce a hundred thousand examples overnight. The naive version,\n\"prompt the big model to write a dataset\", fails in three predictable ways. The outputs collapse onto the\ngenerator's favourite phrasings, names and structures, so the fine-tuned model learns a narrow template\nand fails on real inputs. The generator's mistakes become the student's ground truth. And nothing stops\nthe generated set from containing your evaluation questions, which makes your scores meaningless.\n\nEvery effective synthetic-data recipe is built around the scarce resource, which is not generation but\n*verification*. Self-Instruct bootstrapped instruction data from 175 human-written seed tasks and filtered for\nnovelty; STaR kept only reasoning traces that reached the correct answer; code pipelines keep only\nprograms that pass tests; reasoning-model distillates keep only traces a checker accepts. Where\nverification is strong, synthetic data lets a model exceed its own data; where it is absent, synthetic data\nis a slow copy of the generator with the tails cut off.","how":"### The loop\nSeed → specify → generate → verify → deduplicate and balance → train → evaluate on *real* held-out data →\ninspect failures → adjust the specification. The last three steps are the ones teams skip, and they are\nthe reason the loop converges rather than drifts.\n\n### Sources of diversity\nMode collapse is the default behaviour of a sampled generator, so diversity has to be engineered in:\n\n- **Seeds and in-context examples.** Self-Instruct sampled a handful of tasks from a seed pool (human\n  written at first, then including accepted generations) for every prompt and rejected candidates whose\n  ROUGE-L overlap with existing tasks was too high, so each round pushed outward.\n- **Evolution.** Evol-Instruct rewrites existing instructions along explicit axes (add a constraint,\n  deepen, concretise, require more reasoning, or mutate to a different topic) so the difficulty\n  distribution grows rather than regressing to easy prompts.\n- **Personas.** Conditioning the generator on who is asking (a billion-persona pool was built for exactly\n  this) spreads vocabulary, intent and style far more than temperature does.\n- **Grounding in real artefacts.** Generate questions *from* real documents, records or code rather\n  than from nothing. The inputs then follow the real distribution, and in many cases the label comes for\n  free.\n- **Sampling.** Multiple samples per prompt at moderate temperature, then deduplicate by n-gram overlap\n  and by embedding similarity, because duplicates in synthetic sets are far denser than in scraped ones.\n\n### Verification, strongest to weakest\n1. **Execution.** Code against unit tests, SQL against a database, maths against a known answer or a\n   symbolic solver. Exact, cheap, and the reason code and maths are where synthetic data has done best.\n2. **Construction.** Decide the label first, then generate the input: sample a structured record and ask\n   the model to write the email that contains it; sample a tool-call sequence and generate the user\n   request it answers. The label is correct by construction. Add a round-trip check (an independent pass\n   must recover the label from the input) to catch generations that quietly dropped a fact.\n3. **Consistency.** Majority vote over several samples (self-consistency), or agreement between two\n   different generators, filters out one-off errors; STaR's trick of regenerating a rationale with the\n   answer as a hint (\"rationalisation\") recovers training signal from failures.\n4. **LLM-as-judge.** A rubric-driven judge scales to anything, but carries biases (length, position,\n   self-preference) that must be calibrated against human labels before you trust its filter rate.\n5. **Humans.** Domain experts review a stratified sample; they are the gold standard and the bottleneck,\n   so spend them on calibrating the other verifiers, not on everything.\n\n### Where it is used\nInstruction tuning (prompt–response pairs), preference data for DPO and RLHF (AI feedback over pairs of\ncandidates, with a judge or a rubric), pretraining-scale corpora (synthetic textbooks and rephrased web\ntext that proved unusually token-efficient for small models), evaluation and red-team sets (with the\ncaveat that a model must not be judged on data its own generator produced), and privacy-preserving\nstand-ins for customer data, where a programmatic generator with synthetic identities lets work proceed\noutside a restricted environment. Distilling a large model's behaviour into a small one is the same\nmachinery with a specific aim; see the knowledge distillation lesson.\n\n### Model collapse and how to avoid it\nTraining a model on its own (or its predecessor's) unfiltered output, generation after generation,\nshrinks the tails first and then the whole distribution: rare phrasings vanish, variance falls, errors\ncompound, and each generation is a blurrier copy. The defences follow from the mechanism. Keep real data\nin the mix, accumulating rather than replacing it. Verify, so errors do not compound. Track diversity\ndirectly (distinct n-gram ratios, embedding dispersion, length and topic distributions) across dataset\nversions. Prefer a different or stronger generator to the model being trained. And decontaminate the\nsynthetic set against every eval, because a generator that has seen the benchmark will reproduce it.\n\n### Provenance and permission\nRecord the generator model and version, the prompt template, the seed, every verifier's verdict and the\nsampling settings for each example, because you will need to trace a bad behaviour back to the batch that\ntaught it. Check the generator's terms: some API providers prohibit using outputs to train models that\ncompete with theirs, and some open-weight licences attach conditions to derived models.","example":"Construction-first generation for an extraction task, with a round-trip check. The record is sampled by a\nprogram, so the label is correct before any model is involved; the model only writes the realistic email.\n`llm` is a stub for whichever provider you use.\n\n```python\nimport json, random\n\ndef llm(prompt: str, temperature: float = 0.8) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Call your model provider and return text. Use temperature ~0.8 to write, 0 to check.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n\nVENDORS = [\"Acme Tooling\", \"Northwind Traders\", \"Blue Fern Studio\", \"Kappa Logistics\"]\nPERSONAS = [\"terse accounts-payable clerk\", \"chatty small-business owner\", \"automated billing system\"]\n\ndef sample_record(rng):                          # the LABEL comes first, so it is correct by construction\n    return {\"vendor\": rng.choice(VENDORS), \"invoice_no\": f\"INV-{rng.randint(1000, 99999)}\",\n            \"total\": round(rng.uniform(20, 5000), 2), \"currency\": rng.choice([\"USD\", \"EUR\"]),\n            \"due_date\": f\"2026-{rng.randint(1, 12):02d}-{rng.randint(1, 28):02d}\"}\n\ndef make_example(rng):\n    rec = sample_record(rng)\n    email = llm(f\"You are a {rng.choice(PERSONAS)}. Write a realistic email of 60-150 words that mentions \"\n                f\"exactly these facts in your own words and order, plus one unrelated detail: \"\n                f\"{json.dumps(rec)}. Do not output JSON.\")\n    # round-trip verification: an independent extraction pass must recover the record exactly\n    back = llm(\"Extract vendor, invoice_no, total (number), currency and due_date (YYYY-MM-DD) as JSON \"\n               f\"from this email:\\n{email}\", temperature=0)\n    try:\n        ok = json.loads(back) == rec\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        ok = False\n    return {\"input\": email, \"output\": rec, \"verified\": ok,\n            \"provenance\": {\"generator\": \"<model id>\", \"prompt\": \"invoice-v3\", \"persona_pool\": \"v1\"}}\n\nrng = random.Random(7)\nexamples = [make_example(rng) for _ in range(1000)]\nkept = [e for e in examples if e[\"verified\"]]\nprint(f\"kept {len(kept)}/{len(examples)}\")\n# Next: near-deduplicate the emails, balance vendors and currencies, hold out a slice for evaluation,\n# and have a human read 50 kept and 50 rejected examples before trusting the pass rate.\n```","tradeoffs":["Cheap, fast and controllable coverage of rare cases, against a distribution that drifts from real usage; once you have real traffic, curated real data should anchor the set and synthetic data fill the gaps, not the reverse.","The student is bounded by the generator on the same task, unless verification adds information from outside the generator (test results, ground truth), in which case self-improvement loops become possible; without such a verifier, expect a slightly worse copy.","Executable verifiers are exact and cheap but exist only for some domains; LLM judges cover everything with known biases; humans are accurate and slow, so the practical design calibrates the fast verifiers against a human-labelled sample.","Diversity techniques (personas, evolution) widen coverage but produce some unrealistic examples, so they trade a realism check for the breadth they buy.","Synthetic stand-ins unblock work on private data, but a generator fine-tuned on that private data can leak it, while a purely programmatic generator is safe and less realistic."],"pitfalls":["No verification step: the student learns the generator's errors as truth, so the symptom is a fine-tuned model that is confidently wrong in exactly the same places as the generator.","Low diversity: the same four names, the same opening sentence, the same structure; the symptom is a low distinct-n-gram ratio in the dataset and a model that handles the template and fails real inputs.","Evaluating on data from the same generator and prompt as the training set: the symptom is an eval score that looks excellent and a production rollout that does not.","Replacing real data entirely across successive versions: the symptom is shrinking vocabulary and variance from one dataset version to the next, which is collapse in progress.","Inheriting the generator's style: overly polite, markdown-heavy, verbose; the symptom is a customer saying the outputs do not sound like their house style even though accuracy is fine.","No per-example provenance: when a bad behaviour appears, nobody can say which batch, prompt or generator version taught it, so the fix becomes \"regenerate everything\".","Ignoring the generator's terms of service or licence until launch review: the symptom is a legal block on a model that already works."],"when":"Reach for synthetic data when real labelled data is scarce, private or missing the rare cases, when you\nneed behaviours nobody writes down (reasoning traces, preference pairs, tool-call sequences), when you are\nbootstrapping before there is traffic, and above all when you have a verifier that can tell good examples\nfrom bad. Lean on real curated data when it is abundant, and use synthetic data to fill specific gaps\nfound by error analysis. Measure verification pass rate per batch, diversity metrics across versions,\nthe eval delta on *real* held-out data, and agreement between your fast verifiers and a human-labelled\nsample. In day-to-day work this is a generation pipeline with provenance on every row; in interviews,\nexpect to be asked how you would prevent collapse and why you would generate the label before the input.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the generate–verify–deduplicate pipeline with per-row provenance, calibrates the judge against human labels, and gates every batch on verification pass rate and diversity metrics.","architect":"Decides which generators are permitted (licence and terms), how synthetic and real data are mixed and versioned, and which evaluation sets are walled off from any generator.","fde":"Bootstraps a customer's model from thirty labelled examples by construction-first generation over their real document types, with SMEs reviewing samples and verifying the round-trip check."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a model that extracts fields from forty internal form types, but can supply only\nthirty labelled examples. How would you build a training set, and how would you know it is trustworthy?","a":"I would not ask the model to invent forms. I would take the forty real templates and the field\nschema, write a program that samples valid records for each type, and use a model to render each record\ninto realistic filled forms in several personas and styles, varying layout noise. The label is the sampled\nrecord, so it is correct by construction; a separate extraction pass must recover it exactly, and only\nround-trip successes survive. I would near-deduplicate, balance across form types, and keep the thirty\nhuman-labelled examples strictly as evaluation, never training. Trust comes from three measurements: the\nround-trip pass rate per form type, SME review of a stratified sample of kept and rejected items, and the\neval score on the thirty real examples, with any gap between synthetic and real scores treated as the\npipeline's error bar, not noise."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most determines the quality of a synthetic training set?","options":["The size of the generator model","The sampling temperature","The strength of the verification step that decides which generated examples survive","The number of examples generated"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Generation is cheap and errors are the norm; a verifier that catches wrong or duplicated examples is what separates a dataset that improves a model from one that teaches the generator's mistakes."},{"q":"Why generate the label first and the input second in a construction-first pipeline?","options":["Because inputs are longer and more expensive to generate","Because the label is then correct by construction, and a round-trip check can verify the input faithfully contains it","Because models cannot produce structured output reliably","Because it avoids needing any deduplication"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sampling the structured answer programmatically removes the generator from the label entirely; the model only writes the surface text, and an independent extraction pass confirms nothing was dropped or changed."},{"q":"Which practice best protects against model collapse across successive training rounds?","options":["Raising the sampling temperature each round","Using a larger generator each round","Keeping real data in the mix, verifying outputs, and tracking diversity across dataset versions","Training for fewer epochs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Collapse comes from recursively copying a narrowing distribution with compounding errors. Anchoring on real data, filtering errors and measuring diversity attack each part of that mechanism directly."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/data-labeling","sources":["Cohen, 1960 — A Coefficient of Agreement for Nominal Scales","Landis & Koch, 1977 — The Measurement of Observer Agreement for Categorical Data","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback"],"why":"Supervised fine-tuning, reward models, and the small classifiers that route requests or enforce\nguardrails all need targets. The model does not learn \"the task\"; it learns to reproduce the mapping\npresent in the labeled set, noise included. If 15% of labels are wrong in a consistent direction, the\nmodel learns that direction. If they are wrong at random, the achievable accuracy drops and more data is\nneeded to reach any given level. Pretraining averages any single document away across billions of\ntokens; a fine-tuning set of 2,000 examples has no such averaging, so one annotator's habit of filing\nevery ambiguous ticket under \"billing\" becomes model behaviour.\n\nThe naive approach, a one-line instruction handed to whoever is free, fails for three reasons. Most real\ntasks are underspecified: \"is this answer helpful?\" means different things to different people, so the\nlabels encode the annotator rather than the task. With one labeler per item you cannot measure the noise\nrate, so when the model plateaus at 80% validation accuracy nobody can say whether the model or the\nlabels are the limit. And the items you most need labeled well, the rare and borderline ones, are exactly\nwhere untrained labelers disagree most.\n\nLabeling as a discipline makes the target defined (guidelines with worked edge cases), measurable\n(agreement on overlapping items) and improvable (adjudicated disagreements feed back into the\nguidelines).","how":"### What a label is in LLM work\n- **Categorical:** intent, topic, toxicity, routing class. Cheapest per item; feeds classifiers and\n  guardrails.\n- **Span or structured:** entities, citations, JSON fields. Needs a schema and tooling that enforces it.\n- **Demonstrations:** a complete target response for a prompt, written or edited by an expert. This is\n  SFT data and the most expensive kind, because the annotator produces rather than judges.\n- **Preferences:** a prompt plus two candidate responses; which is better, optionally why. This feeds\n  reward models, RLHF and DPO. Comparing is easier and more consistent than writing, and a comparison\n  stays informative even when both candidates are flawed, which is why InstructGPT-style pipelines\n  collect far more comparisons than demonstrations.\n- **Rubric scores:** 1–5 on named criteria. Convenient, and the hardest to keep consistent across people\n  unless every score level has anchor examples.\n\n### The loop\n1. **Define the task on paper.** Label schema, decision rules, and 20–30 worked examples that include\n   the boundary cases and an explicit \"if unsure, do X\" rule. Ambiguity you leave here gets resolved\n   differently by every annotator later.\n2. **Pilot.** 100–200 items, each labeled independently by two or three people.\n3. **Measure agreement.** For two raters on categorical labels use Cohen's kappa,\n   κ = (p_o − p_e) / (1 − p_e), where p_o is the observed fraction of items they agree on and p_e is the\n   agreement expected by chance from each rater's label frequencies. The correction matters because two\n   raters who both say \"not spam\" 95% of the time agree about 90% of the time by luck alone. Fleiss'\n   kappa extends this to many raters; Krippendorff's alpha also handles missing labels and ordinal\n   scales. The Landis–Koch reading is the common one: below 0.4 poor, 0.6–0.8 substantial, above 0.8\n   near-perfect, with the bar set by the stakes of the task.\n4. **Adjudicate and rewrite.** Every disagreement is a guideline gap or an annotator error. Fix the\n   guideline, re-brief, re-pilot until agreement is acceptable.\n5. **Produce with QA.** Single-label most items, but keep 5–10% overlap to track agreement, seed gold\n   items with known answers to catch drift and inattention, and route anything flagged to adjudication.\n6. **Version and freeze.** Store guideline version, annotator id and timestamp on every record. A\n   guideline change mid-project silently splits the dataset into two tasks.\n\n### Who labels, and how much a model should help\nDomain experts are the only option for specialised demonstrations; crowd or vendor workforces scale\ncategorical tasks once guidelines are solid. Model-assisted labeling, where an LLM proposes a label or\ndrafts a response and a human verifies, cuts cost sharply but introduces anchoring: people accept\nplausible suggestions, so errors the model makes confidently pass through, and a model later trained on\nthat data is partly trained on its own output. Keep the overlap and gold subsets blind to the suggestion\nso you can measure the acceptance bias. Active learning chooses which items to label next, those where\nthe current model is uncertain or annotators disagreed, because labels on items the model already\nhandles add little.\n\n### Why agreement rather than accuracy\nThere is no external truth to score the labels against; the labels are the truth you are constructing.\nAgreement is the observable proxy for whether the task is defined well enough to be learnable, and it\nsets a ceiling: a model cannot be meaningfully evaluated above the level at which humans agree with\neach other.","example":"A pilot round on support tickets: two annotators, kappa computed by hand, disagreements listed for\nadjudication, and the adjudicated result written as SFT records with provenance. On this data it prints\nkappa ≈ 0.48, \"moderate\" on the Landis–Koch scale, which means the guidelines are not finished.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport json\n\npilot = [  # (item_id, text, label_from_annotator_a, label_from_annotator_b)\n    (\"t1\", \"Card charged twice this month\", \"billing\", \"billing\"),\n    (\"t2\", \"App crashes when I open invoices\", \"bug\", \"billing\"),\n    (\"t3\", \"How do I export my data?\", \"howto\", \"howto\"),\n    (\"t4\", \"Refund still not received after 10 days\", \"billing\", \"billing\"),\n    (\"t5\", \"Login page blank on Safari\", \"bug\", \"bug\"),\n    (\"t6\", \"Can I pay yearly instead of monthly?\", \"billing\", \"howto\"),\n]\n\ndef cohen_kappa(a, b):\n    n = len(a)\n    p_o = sum(x == y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / n\n    ca, cb = Counter(a), Counter(b)\n    p_e = sum((ca[k] / n) * (cb[k] / n) for k in set(ca) | set(cb))\n    return (p_o - p_e) / (1 - p_e)\n\na = [r[2] for r in pilot]; b = [r[3] for r in pilot]\nprint(f\"kappa = {cohen_kappa(a, b):.2f}\")          # 0.48 on this pilot\nfor item_id, text, la, lb in pilot:\n    if la != lb:\n        print(f\"ADJUDICATE {item_id}: {text!r}  A={la}  B={lb}\")\n\n# After adjudication the agreed label is the target. Keep provenance on every record.\ngold = {\"t2\": \"bug\", \"t6\": \"billing\"}\nwith open(\"tickets_sft.jsonl\", \"w\") as f:\n    for item_id, text, la, lb in pilot:\n        label = gold.get(item_id, la)\n        record = {\n            \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Classify this support ticket: {text}\"},\n                         {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": label}],\n            \"meta\": {\"guideline\": \"v3\", \"annotators\": [\"a\", \"b\"], \"adjudicated\": item_id in gold},\n        }\n        f.write(json.dumps(record) + \"\\n\")\n```","tradeoffs":["Domain experts give the highest label quality and are mandatory for specialised demonstrations, but cost and throughput push most categorical work to trained crowds once agreement is stable.","Overlapping annotators multiplies cost, but it is the only way to measure label noise; reduce overlap to 5–10% only after the pilot shows agreement has stabilised.","Model-assisted labeling cuts cost and time sharply, but it anchors humans to the model's errors; the saving is real only if a blind subset shows acceptance is not inflating agreement.","Detailed guidelines raise consistency but freeze a definition; when production inputs drift, agreement on new items falls and the guideline must be reopened rather than defended.","Preference labels are cheaper per item than demonstrations and tolerate imperfect candidates, but each carries less information, so you need many more of them and a reward model or DPO to use them."],"pitfalls":["One annotator per item with no overlap; the symptom is a validation plateau that nobody can attribute to the model or to the labels.","Labeling the evaluation set with the same model-assisted pipeline as the training set; the symptom is inflated scores that collapse on a human-only audit.","Changing guidelines mid-project without relabeling earlier items; the symptom is agreement with gold items drifting over time and a model that learns a blend of two definitions.","Sampling items uniformly from production so rare classes get a handful of examples; the symptom is high overall accuracy with near-zero recall on the class you built the model for.","Rubric scores without anchor examples per level; the symptom is one annotator's \"4\" being another's \"2\", hidden because most items cluster in the middle.","Sending raw customer text to a vendor without PII redaction and a data-processing agreement; the symptom is a compliance incident rather than a model problem."],"when":"Label when the behaviour you need is not already in the base model or cannot be prompted reliably, and\nwhen you will measure the result: a routing classifier, a reward model for your domain, SFT\ndemonstrations in a house style, or a golden evaluation set. Before labeling thousands of items, check\nwhether a few hundred high-quality examples plus a strong prompt gets you there; in LLM fine-tuning,\nquality and coverage beat volume, and a smaller set of clean, diverse demonstrations routinely\noutperforms a much larger noisy one. Measure kappa on the pilot, the gold-item pass rate during\nproduction, and the model's accuracy on a human-only held-out set. In interviews, the expected depth is\nto explain why chance-corrected agreement matters, how adjudication improves guidelines, and what\nmodel-assisted labeling does to the data it produces.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the labeling export and import, computes agreement in the pipeline, stores guideline version and annotator ids on each record, and keeps the gold subset blind to model suggestions.","architect":"Decides what gets labeled and by whom, where labeled data lives and how it is versioned, and which tasks justify expert annotators versus crowd or model-assisted labeling.","fde":"Runs the pilot with the customer's own domain experts, turns their disagreements into guidelines, and sets the expectation that labeling quality, not model choice, is usually the bottleneck."},"check":{"q":"A vendor offers to label 20,000 historical support tickets into eight routing classes in a week,\none annotator per ticket. How would you structure the engagement so that the result is trustworthy?","a":"Start with a written guideline and a pilot of about 200 tickets labeled by three annotators\nindependently, compute Fleiss' kappa or Krippendorff's alpha, and adjudicate every disagreement into\nguideline revisions before releasing the full batch. Insist on 5–10% ongoing overlap and a seeded gold\nset so drift and inattention are measurable during the week. Stratify the sample so the rare classes get\nenough items, redact PII before export, and label a separate human-only evaluation set that never sees\nany model suggestion. Finally, record guideline version and annotator id on every record so later\nanalysis can isolate a bad batch instead of discarding the whole dataset."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why report Cohen's kappa instead of the raw percentage of items two annotators agree on?","options":["Kappa is easier to compute for more than two annotators","Raw agreement is inflated when labels are skewed, because raters agree by chance on the common class; kappa subtracts that chance agreement","Kappa measures accuracy against the ground truth, which raw agreement cannot","Raw agreement cannot be computed for categorical labels"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With a 95% \"not spam\" base rate, two raters who ignore the text would still agree about 90% of the time. Kappa removes the expected-by-chance share, so it reflects agreement on the decisions that actually required judgement. Neither metric measures accuracy, since there is no external truth."},{"q":"What is the main risk of having an LLM pre-fill labels for human review?","options":["It makes the labels incompatible with the fine-tuning format","Human annotators become slower because they must read two things","Annotators tend to accept plausible suggestions, so the model's confident errors pass through and the next model is partly trained on its own output","The model's labels are always worse than human labels, so pre-filling never helps"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pre-filling genuinely cuts cost, which is why it is common. The problem is anchoring: agreement looks high because humans agree with the suggestion, not with each other. Keeping a blind subset lets you measure how much acceptance bias the suggestions introduce."},{"q":"Why do RLHF-style pipelines collect many more preference comparisons than written demonstrations?","options":["Preference labels are the only kind a transformer can learn from","Demonstrations cannot be stored in the chat format used for training","Comparing two responses is faster and more consistent across annotators than writing a good response, and it still gives signal when both candidates are imperfect","Preference comparisons carry more information per item than a demonstration"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each comparison carries less information than a full demonstration, which is why many are needed. They win on cost and consistency: judging is easier than producing, and a reward model can aggregate thousands of noisy comparisons into a usable signal."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/train-val-test-split","sources":["Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, 2009 — The Elements of Statistical Learning","Kohavi, 1995 — A Study of Cross-Validation and Bootstrap for Accuracy Estimation and Model Selection","Official docs: scikit-learn model_selection"],"why":"Training minimises loss on the examples it sees, so training loss says nothing about new data; a model\nthat memorised every row would score perfectly. Held-out data is the only instrument that measures\ngeneralisation. But one held-out set stops being enough the moment you start making decisions with it.\nSuppose you try twenty learning-rate and epoch combinations and keep the one with the best validation\nscore. Each score is true quality plus noise, and picking the maximum of twenty noisy numbers picks the\nluckiest noise along with the best model, so the winning score overstates what production will see.\nStatisticians call this selection bias; practitioners call it overfitting the validation set. The fix is\nthe same either way: a second held-out set that no decision ever touched, read once.\n\nThe naive recipe, \"shuffle the rows and cut 80/20\", fails in a second, quieter way: rows are rarely\nindependent. Four turns of one support conversation, ten chunks of one PDF, fifty events from one user,\nlast month's and this month's tickets. If the model trains on some of them and is tested on the rest, it\nis rewarded for recognising the conversation, document, user or period, and the number you get has no\nrelationship to the number on a genuinely new one. A split is only meaningful when it is made at the level\non which you expect the model to generalise.","how":"### Three roles, not three ratios\n- **Train** receives gradient steps. Nothing else is ever fit to it.\n- **Validation** (dev) drives every choice made by looking at a number: hyperparameters, early stopping,\n  checkpoint selection, decision thresholds, prompt and template variants. You may read it as often as you\n  like, and that is exactly why its final score is biased upward.\n- **Test** gives the one-shot estimate. If you read it, change something and read it again, it has become\n  a second validation set; disciplined teams then carve a fresh one out of newly arrived data.\n\n### How big each set should be\nRatios such as 80/10/10 are inherited from the small-data era. What matters is the absolute size of the\nheld-out sets, because the standard error of a measured accuracy *p* on *n* examples is about\n`sqrt(p·(1−p)/n)`. At p = 0.9 that is ±3 points for n = 100, ±0.95 points for n = 1,000 and ±0.3 points\nfor n = 10,000. So a 1,000-example test set cannot separate two models one point apart, however large the\ntraining set is. With ten million rows a 1% test set is 100,000 rows, far more than needed; with 2,000\nfine-tuning examples a 10% slice is 200 rows and barely resolves a two-point difference. Choose held-out\nsizes from the effect you need to detect, then give everything else to training.\n\n### Ways to cut\n1. **Random.** Only valid when rows are independent and identically distributed, which is rarer than it\n   looks.\n2. **Stratified.** Preserve class or category proportions in every split. Essential when a class is rare:\n   a random 10% slice of 50 positives can easily contain two of them, and the metric on that class becomes\n   a coin flip.\n3. **Grouped.** Assign whole groups (conversation, user, document, source repository) to a single split.\n   Hashing the group id into a bucket makes the assignment deterministic and stable as new data arrives.\n4. **Temporal.** Train on the past, validate on the recent past, test on the newest period. Mandatory when\n   the deployed model will face the future, which it always does; it also exposes drift early.\n5. **Cross-validation.** *k* folds rotate the validation role, giving *k* estimates at *k* times the\n   training cost. Worth it when data is small and training is cheap (classical models, small heads),\n   rarely for LLM fine-tunes.\n\n### LLM-specific rules\n- Deduplicate before splitting, including near-duplicates such as one template with different fill-ins;\n  otherwise validation is partly a copy of training and overfitting becomes invisible.\n- Check held-out sets against any public benchmark you report, and against data your base-model vendor may\n  have pretrained on; a contaminated test set measures memory, not skill.\n- Split before fitting anything on data: tokenizer training, normalisation statistics, target encodings,\n  embedding-based filters. Fitting on the union leaks the held-out distribution into the pipeline.\n- Validation loss on the dev set and the task metric on the test set are different instruments. Token-level\n  loss is cheap and continuous; the task metric is what the customer sees. Keep both.","example":"A deterministic, grouped 80/10/10 split for a multi-turn support dataset. Hashing the conversation id\nmeans every turn of a conversation lands in the same split, the assignment never changes when rows are\nadded, and the result is identical on every machine.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef bucket(key: str, buckets: int = 100) -> int:\n    # Stable across processes and machines; Python's built-in hash() is salted per run.\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % buckets\n\ndef assign(example: dict) -> str:\n    # Split on the unit of generalisation: all turns of one conversation travel together.\n    b = bucket(example[\"conversation_id\"])\n    return \"test\" if b < 10 else \"val\" if b < 20 else \"train\"   # 10 / 10 / 80\n\nrows = [{\"conversation_id\": f\"conv-{i // 4}\", \"turn\": i % 4,\n         \"label\": \"billing\" if i % 7 == 0 else \"other\"} for i in range(4000)]\nsplits = {s: [r for r in rows if assign(r) == s] for s in (\"train\", \"val\", \"test\")}\n\nfor name, rs in splits.items():\n    convs = {r[\"conversation_id\"] for r in rs}\n    print(name, len(rs), \"rows\", len(convs), \"conversations\", Counter(r[\"label\"] for r in rs))\n\n# Leakage check: no conversation may appear in two splits.\nids = {s: {r[\"conversation_id\"] for r in rs} for s, rs in splits.items()}\nassert not (ids[\"train\"] & ids[\"val\"]) and not (ids[\"train\"] & ids[\"test\"]) and not (ids[\"val\"] & ids[\"test\"])\n\n# A row that arrives next month keeps the split its conversation already has.\nassert assign({\"conversation_id\": \"conv-17\"}) == assign({\"conversation_id\": \"conv-17\"})\n```\n\nThe `Counter` line is the stratification check: if the `billing` share differs noticeably between splits,\neither enlarge the held-out sets or stratify within hash buckets. When there are no groups, scikit-learn's\n`train_test_split(..., stratify=y)` does the stratified cut directly.","tradeoffs":["Larger validation and test sets give tighter estimates but take rows from training; the balance flips toward training when the dataset is a small fine-tuning set and a ±2-point error bar is acceptable.","Grouped and temporal splits report lower numbers than a random split, and the lower number is the honest one; the random number was high precisely because it leaked.","Cross-validation multiplies training cost by *k*; it pays only when the variance of a single split is large relative to the differences you need to detect.","A hash-based split is reproducible and stable as data grows, but cannot be re-stratified after the fact; rare classes have to be checked or stratified within buckets.","Reading the test set once gives an unbiased estimate but teaches you nothing for the next iteration; rotating in fresh test sets costs labelling effort but keeps honesty over a long project."],"pitfalls":["Splitting by row when rows share a source; the symptom is validation accuracy far above production and a sharp drop the first time a new customer, document or user arrives.","Fitting preprocessing (scalers, tokenizers, vocabularies, filters) on all data before splitting; the symptom is a small, persistent optimistic bias that vanishes when the pipeline is rebuilt on train only.","Reusing the test set across iterations; the symptom is a test score that keeps climbing while production does not move, because the test set has quietly become a validation set.","Random splits with a rare class; the symptom is the minority-class metric jumping between 0% and 100% across seeds.","Re-splitting with a new seed on every run; the symptom is incomparable experiments and \"improvements\" that are nothing but split noise.","Temporal splits with features computed over the whole time window (aggregates, embeddings fit on all periods); the symptom is a time-split test score that is still too good to be true.","Drawing the validation set from the same synthetic template as training; the symptom is near-perfect validation alongside poor human evaluation on real prompts."],"when":"Every supervised project needs the three roles; the decision is how to cut. Reach for a grouped split the\nmoment any two rows share an origin, and a temporal split whenever data arrives over time and the model\nwill serve the future. Size held-out sets from the standard-error formula against the smallest difference\nyou need to detect. Use cross-validation for small classical models, not for LLM fine-tunes where one run\nis expensive. Day to day this is the first thing to audit when a model looks too good: who made the split,\non what key, and was anything fit before it. In interviews, \"how would you split this data\" is really\n\"what is the unit of generalisation and how would you stop leakage\", and a strong answer names the key,\nthe sizes and the one-shot discipline for the test set.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the deterministic split function, the leakage assertion and the stratification check into the data pipeline so every run uses the same held-out sets.","architect":"Owns the policy that evaluation sets are versioned, group- or time-keyed, sized to the decision they support and refreshed when the test set has been consumed by iteration.","fde":"Builds the held-out set with the customer's SMEs from their most recent data and defends a lower, honest number against the inflated one produced by a row-level random split."},"check":{"q":"You fine-tune a ticket classifier on 40,000 tickets from 3,000 customers with a random 80/10/10\nsplit and get 94% validation accuracy, but the first new customer onboarded sees 78%. What happened, how\nwould you re-split, and how large should the held-out sets be?","a":"The random split put tickets from the same customer on both sides, so the model learned\ncustomer-specific signals (product names, signatures, phrasing) and the 94% measured recognition, not\nclassification. Re-split by hashing the customer id so each customer is entirely in one set, and\nconsider a temporal cut as well if ticket mix drifts. The honest number will fall toward 78%, which is\nthe number that matters. Sizing: 4,000 held-out tickets gives a standard error near 0.4 points at 90%\naccuracy, but the effective sample is closer to 300 customers, so expect wider error bars and stratify by\ncategory so rare ticket types appear in every split. Finally, keep the test set untouched until the final\ncomparison."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why keep a separate test set when a validation set already measures held-out performance?","options":["The validation set is too small to be statistically meaningful","Choosing the best of many configurations on validation biases its score upward, so an untouched set is needed for an unbiased estimate","Validation data is used for gradient updates after the first epoch","Test sets are required to compute the loss function correctly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every selection made on validation picks the configuration whose noise was luckiest as well as the best model, so the winning validation score overstates generalisation. Validation data is never used for gradient updates, and size is a separate concern."},{"q":"You are predicting next month's churn from per-user event logs. Which split is appropriate?","options":["A random 80/10/10 split over events","A stratified split over events by churn label","A temporal split with whole users assigned to one side","Ten-fold cross-validation over events"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model will face future months and new users, so the split must respect time and keep each user's events together; any event-level split leaks user identity and future information into training, and stratification alone does not fix either."},{"q":"Roughly how precisely does a 1,000-example test set measure accuracy near 90%?","options":["About ±0.1 percentage points","About ±1 percentage point","About ±5 percentage points","About ±10 percentage points"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard error is sqrt(0.9 × 0.1 / 1000) ≈ 0.0095, so differences smaller than about one point are inside the noise; a tenfold larger set is needed for ±0.3, which is why absolute size matters more than the split ratio."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/epochs","sources":["Muennighoff et al., 2023 — Scaling Data-Constrained Language Models","Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, 2016 — Deep Learning","Official docs: Hugging Face Trainer (num_train_epochs, max_steps)"],"why":"Gradient descent updates on minibatches of *B* examples, so after `ceil(N / B)` steps every one of the\n*N* training examples has contributed to the parameters once. With a small learning rate each example\nnudges the weights only slightly, and for a dataset of a few thousand rows one pass leaves the model far\nfrom the best fit those rows can support. Repeating passes is how you extract more gradient signal from\ndata you cannot get more of. But every repetition shows the model the same specific examples again, and\nafter the shared pattern has been learned the only thing left to fit is what is particular to those rows:\nlabel noise, phrasing, the order of fields. The epoch count is therefore a knob on how much to trust these\nexact examples versus the prior the model already has.\n\nThe naive stopping rules both fail. \"Train until training loss stops falling\" never stops, because loss on\nrepeated data keeps falling all the way to memorisation. \"Always one epoch\" leaves small sets underfit;\n\"always ten\" overfits most of them. The honest control is the validation curve: train while held-out loss\nor the task metric improves, stop when it turns. Pretraining is the opposite regime: the corpus is so large\nrelative to compute that the run ends before one pass completes, and unique tokens are worth more than\nrepeated ones, although a data-constrained scaling study found that repeating data for up to roughly four\npasses costs little compared with fresh data.","how":"### Accounting\nWith *N* examples, micro-batch size *B* and gradient accumulation over *A* micro-batches:\n\n```\nmicro_batches_per_epoch = ceil(N / B)\noptimizer_steps_per_epoch = micro_batches_per_epoch / A\ntotal_optimizer_steps = epochs × optimizer_steps_per_epoch\n```\n\nLearning-rate schedulers are defined over optimizer steps, so changing the epoch count silently changes\nthe decay horizon: a cosine schedule over 375 steps reaches a given fraction of peak LR at a different\npoint than one over 1,250 steps. The Hugging Face `Trainer` derives `max_steps` from\n`num_train_epochs` (default 3) unless `max_steps` is set explicitly, in which case it wins. Two runs with\ndifferent epoch counts are therefore not \"the same run for longer\"; they are different schedules.\n\n### Reshuffling every epoch\nThe order of examples within an epoch determines which examples share a batch. A fixed order makes the\ngradient noise identical in every pass and means the same examples are always freshest at the end of the\nepoch when you evaluate. Reseeding the sampler per epoch (`seed + epoch`) gives different batch\ncompositions each pass while keeping the run reproducible and resumable: a checkpoint that records the\nepoch and the number of batches consumed can regenerate the exact remaining order.\n\n### Where evaluation and checkpoints go\nThe epoch boundary is the conventional moment to evaluate and save, because every example has been seen\nan equal number of times. That is fine when an epoch is 125 steps. When an epoch is hours long, evaluate\nevery *n* steps instead: the model can overfit or diverge in the middle of an epoch and an end-of-epoch\ncheck would miss the best checkpoint. Conversely, with 200 fine-tuning examples an epoch is a handful of\nsteps, and per-epoch evaluation is the only granularity that is not noise.\n\n### Why the number matters so much in fine-tuning\nA pretrained model is a strong prior; each pass over a small instruction set moves it further from that\nprior and toward the specifics of those rows. Illustrative arithmetic: 1,000 examples at batch 8 give 125\nsteps per epoch; three epochs are 375 steps and a 3% warmup is eleven steps. Common practice for\nsupervised fine-tuning is a small number of epochs, with the count rising only for tiny sets where each\nepoch is a few steps, but the actual control is total steps × learning rate judged against the\nvalidation curve, not a magic integer.\n\n### Pretraining counts tokens\nA corpus of trillions of tokens with a compute budget that covers a fraction of it has no meaningful\nepoch; the schedule, the warmup and the evaluation cadence are all defined in tokens or steps. Partial\nreuse of data is a choice made under data constraints, not a default.\n\n### Which unit to compare on\nCompare runs on the same dataset by epochs; compare runs across dataset sizes by optimizer steps or tokens\nseen. \"Two epochs on 10,000 examples\" and \"two epochs on 1,000\" differ by 10× in updates.","example":"A minimal training loop that makes the accounting explicit: epochs, micro-batches, accumulation, a\nper-epoch reseeded sampler, and the linear schedule defined over optimizer steps. Run it and the printed\nstep counts show why the schedule length depends on the epoch count.\n\n```python\nimport math, torch\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader, TensorDataset\n\nN, B, EPOCHS, ACCUM = 1000, 8, 3, 4\nx = torch.randn(N, 16); y = (x.sum(1) > 0).float()\nxv = torch.randn(200, 16); yv = (xv.sum(1) > 0).float()            # held-out\nmodel = torch.nn.Sequential(torch.nn.Linear(16, 32), torch.nn.ReLU(), torch.nn.Linear(32, 1))\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\nloss_fn = torch.nn.functional.binary_cross_entropy_with_logits\n\nmicro_per_epoch = math.ceil(N / B)                                 # 125\ntotal_steps = math.ceil(EPOCHS * micro_per_epoch / ACCUM)          # 94 optimizer steps, incl. final flush\nsched = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.LambdaLR(opt, lambda s: max(0.0, 1 - s / total_steps))\n\nmicro, step = 0, 0\nfor epoch in range(EPOCHS):\n    g = torch.Generator().manual_seed(1234 + epoch)                # new order every epoch, reproducible\n    loader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(x, y), batch_size=B, shuffle=True, generator=g)\n    model.train()\n    for xb, yb in loader:\n        (loss_fn(model(xb).squeeze(1), yb) / ACCUM).backward()\n        micro += 1\n        if micro % ACCUM == 0:                                     # one update per ACCUM micro-batches\n            opt.step(); sched.step(); opt.zero_grad(); step += 1\n    model.eval()\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        val = loss_fn(model(xv).squeeze(1), yv).item()\n    print(f\"epoch {epoch+1}: micro-batches={micro} optimizer steps={step} \"\n          f\"lr={sched.get_last_lr()[0]:.2e} val_loss={val:.3f}\")\n\n# 375 micro-batches is not divisible by 4: three gradients are still pending. Flush them.\nif micro % ACCUM:\n    opt.step(); opt.zero_grad(); step += 1\n```\n\nThe leftover micro-batches at the end are the kind of accounting detail epochs hide: without the final\nflush, three batches of data contribute nothing; if the schedule is sized to 93 steps instead of 94, the\nflush runs at a learning rate of zero and still contributes nothing; and with accumulation that does not\ndivide an epoch, gradients from the last batches of one epoch land in the first update of the next.","tradeoffs":["More epochs lower training loss and improve the fit on small sets, but each extra pass fits more of the rows' particulars; the balance flips at the point where the validation curve turns upward.","Repeating data is free while new data costs money; unique tokens are worth more per token, so repetition is a fallback for data-constrained regimes, not a substitute for collection.","Per-epoch evaluation is cheap and conventional but coarse for long epochs; step-based evaluation finds the best checkpoint at the cost of extra evaluation compute.","Dropping the last partial batch keeps tensor shapes uniform but leaves a few examples unseen each pass; irrelevant at scale, measurable with a few hundred examples."],"pitfalls":["Copying a fixed epoch count from a tutorial without looking at the validation curve; the symptom is held-out loss rising through the second half of the run while training loss keeps falling.","Changing epochs and not noticing the learning-rate schedule changed with it; the symptom is runs that are \"longer\" but incomparable, with different LR at the same step.","Gradient accumulation that does not divide the epoch; the symptom is an optimizer step count that does not match the expected arithmetic and gradients leaking across epoch boundaries.","Comparing runs by epoch when dataset sizes differ; the symptom is a \"two-epoch\" run on ten times the data being credited to the data rather than to ten times the updates.","Resuming from a checkpoint without the sampler position; the symptom is some examples seen twice and others never in that epoch, and a resumed run that no longer matches an uninterrupted one.","Evaluating only at epoch boundaries on a large dataset; the symptom is the best checkpoint sitting between two evaluations and never being saved."],"when":"Think in epochs when the dataset is fixed and small enough that the model will see it several times:\nsupervised fine-tuning, reward-model training, classifier heads. Start low, evaluate at least once per\nepoch, and let the validation curve, not a convention, decide when to stop; save a checkpoint at each\nevaluation so the best one can be recovered. Think in steps or tokens when data streams or is far larger\nthan the budget: pretraining and continued pretraining. When reading someone else's training config,\nconvert epochs into optimizer steps before judging it, and check whether the schedule length was tied to\nthat number. In interviews, being able to derive steps from examples, batch size and accumulation, and to\nexplain why pretraining uses tokens while fine-tuning uses epochs, is the expected baseline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets `num_train_epochs` or `max_steps`, wires per-epoch reseeding and evaluation, and checks that accumulation divides the epoch so step counts match the plan.","architect":"Defines the training budget in steps or tokens and the evaluation cadence, so epoch counts across teams and datasets translate into comparable compute and comparable schedules.","fde":"Explains to a customer with 800 labelled examples why the fine-tune is run for a few epochs with validation checks rather than \"until the loss is zero\", and what the curve they are shown means."},"check":{"q":"A colleague reports that the fine-tune run with five epochs reached a lower loss than the run with\ntwo, so five epochs is better. What would you ask to see before agreeing?","a":"First, which loss: training loss on repeated data always falls with more epochs, so only the\nheld-out loss or the task metric can support the claim. Second, the validation curve across the whole run,\nto see whether held-out performance peaked earlier and the five-epoch endpoint is already past the turn.\nThird, the schedule: with step-based decay the five-epoch run spent its learning rate differently, so the\ncomparison mixes epochs with schedule shape; comparing the best checkpoint of each run is fairer than\ncomparing endpoints. Finally, a check on the task metric and on general capability, because a lower token\nloss after many passes often comes with memorised phrasing and brittleness on paraphrased inputs."},"quiz":[{"q":"4,000 examples, micro-batch 16, gradient accumulation 2, three epochs. How many optimizer steps?","options":["750","375","250","1,500"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each epoch has 4000 / 16 = 250 micro-batches, and accumulation over two makes 125 optimizer steps per epoch; three epochs give 375. The tempting 750 forgets accumulation, which halves the update count."},{"q":"Why is LLM pretraining usually described in tokens rather than epochs?","options":["Tokenisation makes examples variable-length so epochs cannot be counted","The corpus is larger than the compute budget can cover, so the run ends before one full pass and unique data is preferred over repeats","Optimizers require a token budget to compute the learning rate","Epochs only apply to classification tasks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A pass over trillions of tokens rarely completes, so the schedule and evaluation cadence are set in tokens or steps. Variable-length examples are easily counted; the issue is scale, not tokenisation."},{"q":"What goes wrong if the training set is presented in the same order every epoch?","options":["The learning rate schedule resets at every epoch boundary","Gradient accumulation stops working across epochs","Batch compositions and gradient noise repeat exactly, and the same examples are always freshest at evaluation time","The validation loss can no longer be computed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reseeding the sampler each epoch gives different batch groupings and decorrelated noise between passes while staying reproducible; a fixed order biases what the model has just seen when it is evaluated."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/learning-rate","sources":["Smith, 2017 — Cyclical Learning Rates for Training Neural Networks","Goyal et al., 2017 — Accurate, Large Minibatch SGD: Training ImageNet in 1 Hour","Loshchilov & Hutter, 2019 — Decoupled Weight Decay Regularization","Touvron et al., 2023 — Llama 2: Open Foundation and Fine-Tuned Chat Models"],"why":"Gradient descent is `θ ← θ − η·∇L(θ)`. The gradient is a first-order, local statement: it says which\ndirection is downhill at this point, not how far downhill continues. How far is governed by curvature.\nFor a one-dimensional quadratic `L = ½λθ²` the update multiplies θ by `(1 − ηλ)`, so the iteration\nconverges only when `η < 2/λ`; above that, each step overshoots the minimum by more than it approached\nit and the loss grows without bound. A real loss surface has many directions with different curvatures,\nand a single η must be small enough for the sharpest of them while still making progress along the flat\nones. That compromise is why the learning rate must be tuned and why adaptive optimisers, which rescale\neach coordinate, exist at all; even with them, the global η still sets how far every weight can move.\n\nBoth naive policies fail. \"Set it small to be safe\" wastes the budget: distance travelled is roughly steps\n× step size, so a rate ten times too small spends the whole run in the early part of the trajectory and\nends with the loss of a much shorter run; it also tends to generalise worse, an empirical regularity whose\nexplanation is still debated. \"Set it large to go fast\" produces loss spikes, NaNs and a model whose\npretrained features were destroyed in the first hundred steps. The learning rate is therefore not a\nconstant but a trajectory, with a warmup, a peak and a decay, each answering a different failure.","how":"### What η means under SGD versus Adam\nUnder SGD, `Δθ = −η·g`: the step scales with the gradient's magnitude, which differs across layers by\norders of magnitude, so a good SGD rate is specific to the architecture and even the initialisation.\nUnder Adam and AdamW, `Δθ ≈ −η·m̂ / (√v̂ + ε)`, where m̂ and v̂ are running estimates of the gradient's\nmean and squared magnitude. The division normalises scale away: for a consistently signed gradient the\nstep is about η per parameter regardless of how large the raw gradient was, and smaller when the gradient\nflips sign. That is why transformer learning rates cluster in a narrow band whatever the layer: the Llama 2\nreport, for instance, trained its 7B and 13B models with a peak of 3e-4 and the 34B and 70B with 1.5e-4,\nwith cosine decay to 10% of peak. AdamW's weight decay is also multiplied by η (`θ ← θ·(1 − η·λ)`), so\nraising the learning rate strengthens regularisation at the same time.\n\n### Warmup: why the first steps are special\nRuns start with η near zero and ramp linearly to the peak, over thousands of steps in pretraining or a few\npercent of the steps in fine-tuning. Two reasons. Adam's second-moment estimate is built from very few\nsamples at the start, so normalisation is unreliable and a full-size step can land anywhere. And the\nearliest gradients are the largest and least informative: a freshly attached head or a zero-initialised\nadapter produces gradients that would tear through pretrained features if applied at full rate. Warmup\nlets the statistics and the representation settle before committing to large moves. Depth on the\nmechanism lives in `ml-fundamentals/warmup`.\n\n### Decay: why the last steps are special\nMinibatch gradients are noisy, so at a fixed rate the parameters jitter inside a region whose size grows\nwith η; the loss plateaus at the noise floor rather than at the minimum. Shrinking η late in the run\n(cosine or linear decay to a fraction of peak) lets the parameters settle into the basin. Some recent\nrecipes hold the rate constant and decay only in a final phase so a run can be extended without replaying\nthe schedule. Schedule shapes are covered in `ml-fundamentals/learning-rate-schedulers`.\n\n### Scaling with batch size\nA larger batch averages more samples, reduces gradient noise and tolerates larger steps. For SGD the\nlinear scaling rule (multiply η by *k* when the batch grows by *k*, with warmup) is well established; for\nAdam the right exponent is contested and square-root scaling is often recommended. Either way, a rate\ncopied from a run with a different batch size is a different hyperparameter.\n\n### Fine-tuning rates\nFull fine-tuning of an LLM typically uses peaks in the low 1e-5 range; LoRA and other adapters typically\nuse rates around ten times higher. Treat these as common starting points, not laws. The full-model rate is\nlow because every weight moves and large moves erase pretrained knowledge (see catastrophic forgetting);\nadapter rates are higher because the trainable set is small, starts at zero and must leave zero quickly.\n\n### Finding the rate\nThe range test increases η exponentially over a short run and plots loss: the usable region ends just\nbefore the loss explodes, and the best peak sits somewhat below that edge. A coarse grid at ×3 spacing\n(1e-5, 3e-5, 1e-4) resolves most fine-tuning questions. Diagnostics: spikes or NaN mean too high;\na slow, monotone descent that ends above the baseline means too low; gradient clipping firing on most\nsteps means the rate is leaning on the clip rather than being right.","example":"The stability bound is easiest to see on the quadratic, and Adam's normalisation is easiest to see by\nfeeding it two gradients that differ by 1000× and watching the parameter move by the same amount.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef gd(lr, curvature=4.0, theta=1.0, steps=10):\n    # Loss L = 0.5 * curvature * theta**2, gradient = curvature * theta\n    for _ in range(steps):\n        theta = theta - lr * curvature * theta          # = theta * (1 - lr * curvature)\n    return theta\n\nfor lr in (0.05, 0.1, 0.45, 0.5, 0.6):\n    print(f\"lr={lr:<5} factor={1 - lr * 4:+.2f}  theta after 10 steps={gd(lr):+.4f}\")\n# lr=0.05  factor=+0.80  0.1074   slow, monotone\n# lr=0.1   factor=+0.60  0.0060   fast, monotone\n# lr=0.45  factor=-0.80  0.1074   oscillates, still converges\n# lr=0.5   factor=-1.00  1.0000   bounces forever: exactly the 2/curvature limit\n# lr=0.6   factor=-1.40  28.925   diverges\n\n# Adam normalises the gradient scale away, so the first step is ~lr whatever the gradient size.\nw = torch.nn.Parameter(torch.tensor([1.0]))\nopt = torch.optim.Adam([w], lr=1e-3)\nfor scale in (1.0, 1000.0):\n    w.data.fill_(1.0); opt.state.clear()\n    w.grad = torch.tensor([scale])\n    opt.step()\n    print(f\"gradient={scale:>6}: moved by {1.0 - w.item():.6f}\")   # both ~0.001\n```\n\nIn a Hugging Face fine-tune the same ideas appear as `learning_rate=2e-5`, `warmup_ratio=0.03` and\n`lr_scheduler_type=\"cosine\"`; the peak, the ramp and the decay are three settings of one trajectory.","tradeoffs":["A higher peak rate reaches a lower loss within a fixed budget and often generalises better, but instability grows with it; the balance flips as soon as loss spikes appear, which happens more readily at larger model scale.","Longer warmup protects against early divergence and feature damage but delays learning; with a short fine-tune of a few hundred steps, a long warmup can consume a large share of the run.","Decaying to near zero gives the best final weights but a finished run cannot be extended; a constant phase with a late decay keeps that option at the cost of a slightly less settled endpoint.","A low fine-tuning rate preserves pretrained knowledge and reduces forgetting but may underfit the new task; the balance shifts toward a higher rate when the task is far from the pretraining distribution."],"pitfalls":["Copying a rate across optimisers, batch sizes or parameterisations (SGD versus AdamW, full fine-tune versus LoRA); the symptom is a run that diverges immediately or one that barely moves in the budget.","Changing the number of steps (epochs, dataset size) while the schedule is defined in steps; the symptom is two \"identical\" configs with different effective rates at the same step and incomparable results.","Letting gradient clipping hide an excessive rate; the symptom is the clip triggering on almost every step while the loss creeps rather than drops.","Skipping or truncating warmup with Adam; the symptom is divergence or a permanent loss plateau in the first few hundred steps that no later schedule repairs.","Raising the learning rate without noticing that AdamW's decay scales with it; the symptom is weights shrinking faster than intended and underfitting that looks like a data problem.","Reporting \"learning rate 2e-5\" with no warmup, schedule or batch size; the symptom is that nobody, including you, can reproduce the run."],"when":"Every training run sets a learning rate; the decision is how to choose it and its trajectory. For a new\nfine-tuning task, start from a common rate for the parameterisation you are using, run a short range test\nor a ×3 grid, and read the loss curve for spikes (too high) or a flat, early plateau (too low). Pair the\npeak with a warmup of a few percent of the steps and a decay that fits the run length, and record all\nthree with the batch size. Watch the gradient norm alongside the loss; a healthy run has a stable norm,\nnot one rescued by clipping. In interviews, derive the 2/λ bound, explain why Adam makes rates transferable\nacross layers, and say why fine-tuning rates are lower than pretraining rates; that chain of reasoning is\nwhat distinguishes understanding from a remembered number.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the peak rate, warmup ratio and schedule in the training config, runs the range test or grid, and reads loss and gradient-norm curves to diagnose a rate that is too high or too low.","architect":"Sets the policy that learning rate, warmup, schedule and batch size are recorded together for every run so results are reproducible and comparable across teams and model sizes.","fde":"When a customer's fine-tune on their data diverges or regresses on general ability, the learning rate and its warmup are the first settings to inspect and the easiest to explain."},"check":{"q":"Your full fine-tune at a peak rate of 2e-5 learns the task but regresses on general instruction\nfollowing; your LoRA run at the same 2e-5 learns almost nothing. Explain both outcomes and what you would\nchange.","a":"The full fine-tune moves every weight by about the learning rate per step under AdamW, so even a\nmodest rate accumulated over hundreds of steps shifts the whole network away from the pretrained solution;\nthe regression is forgetting, and the fixes are a lower rate, fewer steps, early stopping on a general\nevaluation, or mixing general data into the set. The LoRA adapters start at zero and are a tiny fraction\nof the parameters, so at 2e-5 they barely leave their initialisation within the run; adapter training\ncommonly uses rates around ten times higher. The change is to tune the rate per parameterisation, run a\nquick grid at ×3 spacing for each, and watch both the task metric and the general evaluation rather than\ntraining loss alone."},"quiz":[{"q":"For the loss L = ½λθ² with λ = 4, which learning rate makes plain gradient descent diverge?","options":["0.1","0.25","0.45","0.6"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The update multiplies θ by (1 − ηλ), which must stay inside (−1, 1), so η must be below 2/λ = 0.5. At 0.45 the iterate oscillates but shrinks; at 0.6 the factor is −1.4 and the loss grows every step."},{"q":"Why do Adam learning rates transfer across layers and architectures better than SGD rates?","options":["Adam ignores the gradient and moves every weight by exactly η","Adam divides by a running estimate of gradient magnitude, so the step is roughly η per parameter regardless of raw gradient scale","Adam automatically performs a learning-rate range test during warmup","Adam applies weight decay, which cancels differences in gradient scale"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Normalisation by the second-moment estimate removes the layer-to-layer scale differences that make SGD rates architecture-specific. Adam does not ignore gradients; sign changes and noise still shrink its steps."},{"q":"A bf16 fine-tune shows loss spikes and then NaN within the first 50 steps. Which learning-rate issue is most likely?","options":["The rate is too low, so the loss cannot leave its plateau","The peak rate is too high or the warmup too short, so early unreliable Adam steps are taken at full size","The decay schedule ended too early","Weight decay was set to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Early steps combine the largest gradients with the least reliable second-moment estimates; a full-size step there overshoots sharply curved directions. Decay is irrelevant that early, and a too-low rate produces slowness, not spikes."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/overfitting","sources":["Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, 2009 — The Elements of Statistical Learning","Srivastava et al., 2014 — Dropout: A Simple Way to Prevent Neural Networks from Overfitting","Nakkiran et al., 2019 — Deep Double Descent: Where Bigger Models and More Data Hurt","Belkin et al., 2019 — Reconciling Modern Machine-Learning Practice and the Classical Bias-Variance Trade-off"],"why":"A training objective asks only one thing: lower the loss on these examples. A model with enough capacity\ncan do that by learning the rule, or by learning the examples. Both routes reach low training loss; only\nthe first reaches low loss on examples it has not seen. Memorising is often the easier route, because\nevery dataset carries signals that correlate with the label inside the set and nowhere else: an annotator\nwho filed ambiguous tickets under \"billing\", a template whose phrasing differs by class, an id or timestamp\nthat happens to sort with the answer. Gradient descent has no reason to prefer the rule over these\nshortcuts, and once the rule is learned the shortcuts and the noise are the only loss left to remove.\n\nThe naive answer, \"train until the loss stops falling\", is precisely the recipe for overfitting, because\non repeated data the training loss keeps falling to zero. The other naive answer, \"use a smaller model\",\nthrows away the capacity that makes the model good at the rule in the first place. What you want is a\nmodel with enough capacity to learn the pattern and a training procedure that stops, or is held back,\nbefore it learns the noise. Measuring that requires data the model has never seen, which is why\noverfitting and the train/validation/test split are inseparable ideas.","how":"### The generalisation gap\nCall the loss on training data *L_train* and on fresh data from the same distribution *L_val*. Early in\ntraining both fall together because the model is learning structure that is shared. At some point\n*L_train* keeps falling while *L_val* flattens; the difference between them is the generalisation gap,\nand it grows with every step that fits something specific to the training rows. The gap is driven by the\nratio of effective capacity to effective data: more trainable parameters, more steps, higher learning\nrate and less diverse data all widen it. Noise in the labels sets a floor on achievable *L_val*; a model\nthat goes below the noise floor on training data has memorised the noise.\n\n### How it looks in LLM fine-tuning\n- Held-out loss turns up after one or two passes while training loss keeps dropping.\n- The model reproduces training completions verbatim, including names, numbers and boilerplate, and can\n  be prompted to regurgitate training records, which is a privacy incident as well as a modelling failure.\n- Outputs converge on one style or length regardless of the prompt, because the fine-tuning set had one.\n- Accuracy is high on prompts phrased like the training data and collapses on paraphrases.\n- Token-level evaluation loss and the task metric can disagree: in practice it is common for held-out loss\n  to rise while the task metric is still flat or improving, because loss penalises every token including\n  stylistic ones. Pick checkpoints on the metric you ship, with loss as the early-warning signal.\n\n### Levers, and why each one works\n1. **Data.** More examples make memorisation harder than learning the rule; deduplication removes rows\n   the model would otherwise see several times per epoch; cleaner labels lower the noise floor. This is the\n   only lever that raises the ceiling as well as closing the gap.\n2. **Steps.** Fewer epochs, a lower learning rate, and early stopping limit how far the model travels from\n   the pretrained prior, so it stops after the shared structure and before the particulars.\n3. **Capacity.** Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (low-rank adapters, a few trainable layers) constrains the\n   update to a small subspace; a smaller model does the same more bluntly.\n4. **Regularisation.** Weight decay penalises large weights, dropout forces redundancy, label smoothing\n   stops the model from driving probabilities to exactly one, and mixing general-purpose data into the\n   fine-tuning set dilutes the shortcuts specific to your rows. Details in\n   `ml-fundamentals/dropout-regularization`.\n\n### What overfitting is not\n- **Underfitting:** both losses high and close together; the model lacks capacity or steps.\n- **Distribution shift:** the gap between validation and production, not between training and validation;\n  more training on the same data will not help.\n- **Contamination or leakage:** held-out data that duplicates training data makes overfitting invisible\n  rather than curing it.\n- **Catastrophic forgetting:** losing capabilities the fine-tuning set never measured; it often\n  accompanies overfitting but is diagnosed on a different evaluation.\n\n### A caveat on the classic picture\nThe textbook U-shaped curve (error falls, then rises with capacity) is not the whole story for modern\nnetworks. Double descent and related results show that heavily over-parameterised models can fit the\ntraining data exactly and still generalise well, and that test error can fall again past the\ninterpolation point. The practical consequence is not to rely on a rule of thumb about model size; rely\non the held-out curve for the model and data in front of you.","example":"A worked scenario with illustrative numbers. A team fine-tunes a 7B instruct model on 2,000 support\nreplies for ten epochs at a modest learning rate and evaluates after each epoch:\n\n| epoch | train loss | val loss | task metric (human-rated usefulness, %) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1.42 | 1.38 | 71 |\n| 2 | 1.10 | 1.21 | 78 |\n| 3 | 0.95 | 1.18 | 80 |\n| 4 | 0.82 | 1.19 | 80 |\n| 5 | 0.70 | 1.25 | 78 |\n| 8 | 0.41 | 1.52 | 69 |\n| 10 | 0.28 | 1.71 | 63 |\n\nTraining loss falls monotonically all the way; validation loss bottoms at epoch 3 and the task metric\nplateaus across epochs 3 and 4 before falling. By epoch 10 the model answers training-set questions\nword for word and gives a boilerplate reply to anything else. The right checkpoint is epoch 3 or 4, and\nthe run should have stopped around epoch 6. The monitor below encodes that rule: stop after three\nevaluations without improvement and reload the best checkpoint.\n\n```python\nclass EarlyStopping:\n    \"\"\"Stop when the validation metric has not improved by min_delta for `patience` evaluations.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, patience=3, min_delta=0.005, mode=\"min\"):\n        self.patience, self.min_delta = patience, min_delta\n        self.sign = 1 if mode == \"min\" else -1\n        self.best, self.best_step, self.bad_evals = None, None, 0\n\n    def update(self, value, step) -> bool:\n        improved = self.best is None or self.sign * (self.best - value) > self.min_delta\n        if improved:\n            self.best, self.best_step, self.bad_evals = value, step, 0\n        else:\n            self.bad_evals += 1\n        return self.bad_evals >= self.patience      # True -> stop and reload best_step's checkpoint\n\nstop = EarlyStopping(patience=3)\nfor epoch, val_loss in enumerate([1.38, 1.21, 1.18, 1.19, 1.25, 1.33, 1.44, 1.52, 1.61, 1.71], start=1):\n    if stop.update(val_loss, step=epoch):\n        print(f\"stop at epoch {epoch}; best was epoch {stop.best_step} (val {stop.best})\")\n        break\n# -> stop at epoch 6; best was epoch 3 (val 1.18)\n```","tradeoffs":["Regularisation and fewer steps reduce variance but add bias; pushed too far they produce underfitting, so the balance flips when both training and validation loss sit high together.","Early stopping is nearly free but only as good as the validation set; with a leaky or tiny validation set it stops at the wrong place, and the real cost moves to building that set properly.","Parameter-efficient fine-tuning limits how much the model can memorise, but it equally limits how much of a genuinely new task it can learn; for tasks far from pretraining, full fine-tuning with more data wins.","More data is the only lever that improves the achievable ceiling, but it costs labelling time, and synthetic data that shares one template can add rows while adding little diversity."],"pitfalls":["Judging a run by training loss; the symptom is a proud \"loss went to 0.1\" followed by a model that cannot handle a rephrased question.","A validation set that near-duplicates training rows; the symptom is validation loss tracking training loss downward forever, with overfitting surfacing only in production.","Stopping on token loss when the task metric is what matters, or the reverse; the symptom is a checkpoint chosen one or two epochs away from the best one on the metric customers see.","Mistaking distribution shift for overfitting and adding regularisation; the symptom is validation numbers that stay fine while production keeps degrading.","Tuning dozens of hyperparameters on the same validation set; the symptom is a validation score that improves with every experiment and a test score that does not, because you have overfit the validation set.","Fine-tuning on records that contain personal data for many epochs; the symptom is the model regurgitating names, addresses or account numbers verbatim when prompted with a prefix."],"when":"Overfitting is not something you choose; it is what every sufficiently long run does, so the decision is\nhow to detect and bound it. Always train with a held-out set that respects the unit of generalisation,\nevaluate at least once per epoch, save a checkpoint at each evaluation and keep the best one. Start with\nfew epochs and a low learning rate and lengthen only if validation is still improving. Reach for more or\ncleaner data before reaching for regularisation knobs, and for a smaller trainable footprint when data is\nscarce. Day to day, the gap between training and validation curves is the first chart to inspect in any\nrun review. In interviews, be ready to explain the gap, the levers, and why the classic U-curve is only\na guide for modern networks.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires held-out evaluation, early stopping and best-checkpoint loading into the training job and reads the train/validation gap before believing any loss number.","architect":"Sets the policy that every fine-tuned model ships with a validation curve, a held-out task metric and a data-deduplication step, so overfit models are caught before deployment.","fde":"Explains to a customer why a model trained longer on their 500 examples got worse, and why the cure is more diverse examples and an honest evaluation set rather than more epochs."},"check":{"q":"A fine-tuned classifier shows 99% training accuracy, 97% validation accuracy, and 81% accuracy\nin production two weeks after launch. Is this overfitting, and what would you check before changing the\ntraining recipe?","a":"The small train-validation gap argues against classic overfitting; the big validation-production\ngap points elsewhere. Check first whether the validation set shares sources with training (same users,\ndocuments or templates), since leakage would hide overfitting and make 97% meaningless; a grouped\nre-split answers that. Then compare the production input distribution with the training data: new\ncustomers, new product names or a changed upstream pipeline mean distribution shift, which more\nregularisation will not fix and which calls for fresh data and a temporal validation set. Only if a\nleak-free validation set also drops would I treat it as overfitting and reduce epochs, add data or\nconstrain the trainable parameters."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which pattern in the training curves most clearly indicates overfitting?","options":["Training and validation loss both high and close together","Training loss falling steadily while validation loss has turned upward","Validation loss falling faster than training loss","Both losses falling together throughout the run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A widening gap with validation loss rising means the model is fitting particulars of the training rows that do not transfer. Both losses high and close is underfitting, and both falling together is healthy."},{"q":"A 2,000-example fine-tune overfits after two epochs. Which single change most directly raises the achievable quality rather than just closing the gap?","options":["Training for more epochs with a lower learning rate","Switching to a larger base model","Adding more deduplicated, diverse training examples","Increasing weight decay"],"answer":2,"explanation":"More diverse data makes the rule cheaper to learn than the rows and lowers the noise floor, improving the ceiling; the other options trade bias for variance or add capacity without adding information."},{"q":"The validation set contains near-duplicates of training examples. What happens to overfitting detection?","options":["Overfitting is detected earlier because duplicates are easier to fit","Nothing changes as long as the test set is clean","Validation loss tracks training loss downward, so overfitting becomes invisible until production","The model cannot overfit because duplicates act as regularisation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memorised rows score well on their near-copies, so the gap that reveals overfitting never appears. A clean test set would show the truth, but only once, at the end, after every decision was already made."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/catastrophic-forgetting","sources":["McCloskey & Cohen, 1989 — Catastrophic Interference in Connectionist Networks: The Sequential Learning Problem","Kirkpatrick et al., 2017 — Overcoming Catastrophic Forgetting in Neural Networks","Ouyang et al., 2022 — Training Language Models to Follow Instructions with Human Feedback","Qi et al., 2023 — Fine-tuning Aligned Language Models Compromises Safety, Even When Users Do Not Intend To!"],"why":"A neural network stores everything in one shared set of weights. There is no region for \"French\", one for\n\"SQL\" and one for \"refusing dangerous requests\"; the same matrices participate in all of them. Training on\na new dataset computes the gradient of the new loss alone, and that gradient has no term for the old\ntasks. Any weight that the new task wants to move will move, however important it was to something the\nloss cannot see. The phenomenon was named in the connectionist literature in the late 1980s as the\nsequential learning problem, and nothing about scale removes it; larger models have more capacity and\nmore redundant features, which slows forgetting per step, but a small dataset repeated for several epochs\nat a healthy learning rate drags the whole network toward those rows.\n\nFor LLMs the problem has a sharper edge because the capabilities you are forgetting are the product.\nThe InstructGPT work reported an \"alignment tax\": models fine-tuned with RLHF regressed on public NLP\nbenchmarks, and the authors mixed pretraining gradients back in to reduce it. Later work showed that\nfine-tuning an aligned chat model on ordinary, benign task data can erode its refusal behaviour, even\nwhen nobody intended that. The naive alternative, \"my task evaluation went up, ship it\", is exactly how\nthese regressions reach production: the metric that would have caught them was never run.","how":"### The mechanism in one line\nLet θ* be the weights after pretraining and alignment. Fine-tuning minimises *L_new(θ)*. Its gradient\nmoves θ along directions that reduce *L_new*; where those directions also raise *L_old* the old loss\nrises, and nothing in the update notices. A useful picture is curvature: directions in which *L_old* is\nsharply curved are the ones that matter to the old tasks (the Fisher information measures this), and a\nstep of size η along such a direction costs roughly `½·F·η²` of old loss. Forgetting is therefore a\nfunction of three things: how far you move (steps × learning rate), in which directions (what the data\nasks for), and how sharp the old loss is there.\n\n### Mitigation families\n1. **Replay (rehearsal).** Mix data that represents the old behaviour into the fine-tuning set so the\n   loss has a term for it. The PPO-ptx variant in InstructGPT is this idea with pretraining data; in\n   practice teams mix a share of general instruction data, chat-formatted examples and safety examples\n   into a task fine-tune, with the proportion tuned on the regression suite. The limit: you need data that\n   represents what you want to keep, and for a closed base model you only have proxies.\n2. **Constrain the move.** A lower learning rate, fewer epochs and early stopping judged on the general\n   suite, not only on the task, keep the model near θ*. Explicit versions add a penalty on the distance:\n   elastic weight consolidation weights each parameter's displacement by its Fisher importance,\n   `λ/2 · Σ_i F_i (θ_i − θ*_i)²`; L2-SP uses a plain quadratic. RLHF and DPO carry a KL term to a\n   reference policy that plays the same role at the level of outputs. At LLM scale the learning-rate and\n   epoch controls plus the KL term are what is actually used; Fisher-weighted penalties are rare.\n3. **Isolate the update.** LoRA and other adapters freeze the base weights and train a small set of new\n   parameters. The base is untouched by construction, the adapter can be removed or swapped per task,\n   and the low rank limits how far outputs can move. But the model's behaviour still changes wherever the\n   adapter is active, so adapters reduce forgetting rather than preventing it, and a high-rank adapter\n   trained hard forgets too.\n4. **Average back.** Interpolating the fine-tuned weights with θ* (weight-space ensembling, model merging)\n   recovers much of the old behaviour while keeping most of the task gain, with the mixing coefficient\n   chosen on the two evaluations; see `training-adaptation/model-merging`.\n5. **Measure it.** None of the above is a guarantee. Run a fixed general-capability suite (instruction\n   following, a reasoning subset, format and chat-template adherence, refusal behaviour on a harmful\n   prompt set) before and after every fine-tune and block the release on regression beyond a tolerance.\n\n### What usually gets forgotten first\nBehaviours that are rare in the fine-tuning data and sharply defined in the original: the chat template\nand system-prompt handling when training on raw completions, refusals when training on compliant\nanswers, multilingual ability when training in one language, and tool-call formatting when training on\nprose. Each is invisible to a task-only evaluation and obvious to a user.\n\n### Sequential fine-tunes compound\nFine-tuning v2 from v1 on the second batch of data, then v3 from v2, forgets a little of each earlier\nbatch at every hop. The safer pattern is to fine-tune from the base model on the union of all data each\ntime, which also makes the run reproducible.","example":"A team fine-tunes an instruct model on 5,000 legal-clause extraction examples. The first attempt (full\nfine-tune, three epochs, task data only) lifts the extraction metric but the general evaluation shows\nlower instruction following and a drop in refusal rate on harmful prompts. The second attempt mixes a\nquarter of general instruction data into every batch, trains adapters at a low rate for two epochs, and\ngates on the regression suite. The helpers (`load_jsonl`, `run_suite`, `finetune`) stand in for the\nteam's pipeline functions.\n\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(0)\n\ntask = load_jsonl(\"legal_clauses.jsonl\")            # 5,000 new-task examples\ngeneral = load_jsonl(\"general_chat_sample.jsonl\")   # open instruction/chat data: the replay buffer\n\nREPLAY = 0.25                                       # general data as a share of the mixed set\nn_replay = int(len(task) * REPLAY / (1 - REPLAY))   # 1,666 rows so general = 25% of the total\nmixed = task + random.sample(general, n_replay)\nrandom.shuffle(mixed)                               # interleave; never train the sources in blocks\n\nSUITE = [\"ifeval_subset\", \"gsm8k_subset\", \"chat_template_adherence\", \"refusal_rate_harmful\"]\n\ndef gate(before: dict, after: dict, max_drop: float = 0.02):\n    regressions = {k: (before[k], after[k]) for k in before if after[k] < before[k] - max_drop}\n    assert not regressions, f\"general capability regressed: {regressions}\"\n\nbefore = run_suite(base_model, SUITE)\nmodel = finetune(base_model, mixed, lr=1e-4, epochs=2, lora_r=16, eval_every_steps=100)\nafter = run_suite(model, SUITE)\ngate(before, after)                                 # fails the release if any metric drops > 2 points\n```\n\nThe replay share, the rank and the two-point tolerance are starting points to be tuned on the suite,\nnot constants. The important design choice is that the suite runs before training too: a drop can only\nbe recognised against the base model's own number.","tradeoffs":["Replay costs training tokens and needs data that represents the old behaviour; for a closed base model you use open instruction sets as a proxy, which protects general skills but not the vendor's specifics.","A lower learning rate and fewer steps reduce forgetting but may underfit the task; the balance tips toward more movement when the task is far from pretraining and the old capabilities matter less.","Adapters bound the damage and keep the base swappable, but a task that needs deep changes may need full fine-tuning with heavier replay instead.","A KL penalty or weight-space pull toward the reference keeps behaviour close but caps the reward or task gain; raising the penalty trades task quality for retention.","Fisher-weighted penalties are principled but need an extra pass to estimate importance and are rarely worth it at LLM scale when replay and early stopping achieve the same retention."],"pitfalls":["Evaluating only on the task; the symptom is a model that aces the fine-tuning benchmark and fails users on anything adjacent, discovered after release.","Training on raw completions without the chat template; the symptom is the model ignoring system prompts or producing malformed turns after the fine-tune.","Fine-tuning on compliant answers only; the symptom is a measurable rise in compliance with harmful requests even though the data was benign.","Chaining fine-tunes v1 → v2 → v3; the symptom is capabilities from the first batch quietly eroding with each release.","Treating LoRA as a guarantee against forgetting; the symptom is a high-rank adapter trained for many epochs showing the same regressions as a full fine-tune.","Diagnosing forgetting as overfitting and adding regularisation; the symptom is the task metric dropping while the general metric does not recover, because the old data is still absent from the loss."],"when":"Treat forgetting as a risk to manage on every fine-tune, including small adapter runs. Before training,\nfix the general suite and record the base model's numbers; during training, evaluate on both task and\nsuite at the same cadence; after training, gate on the suite. Reach for replay first because it attacks\nthe cause, then for a lower learning rate, fewer epochs and adapters, then for weight averaging when a\ngood task checkpoint regressed and you want to recover most of it cheaply. Skip the heavier machinery\nunless the suite shows it is needed. Day to day, this is why \"what did we lose\" is as routine a question\nas \"what did we gain\" in a fine-tune review. In interviews, explaining the mechanism (no loss term for\nthe old tasks) and the ordered set of mitigations is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the data mix with a replay share, runs the general suite before and after, and implements the regression gate that blocks a fine-tune from being promoted.","architect":"Owns the standing general-capability and safety suite every fine-tuned model must pass, and the rule that fine-tunes start from the base on the union of data rather than chaining.","fde":"Shows a customer the before/after suite results alongside the task gain, so the decision to deploy weighs what the model lost in their environment and not only what it learned."},"check":{"q":"A customer fine-tuned a chat model on 8,000 of their internal Q&A pairs and reports it now answers\ntheir questions well but \"feels dumber\", occasionally ignores the system prompt and answers questions it\npreviously refused. Explain what happened and lay out a recovery plan.","a":"The fine-tune optimised only the Q&A loss, so weights supporting general instruction following,\nsystem-prompt handling and refusal behaviour were overwritten; the internal data likely lacked system\nprompts and refusals entirely, so those behaviours had no gradient keeping them in place. Recovery:\nre-run from the base model, not from the damaged checkpoint; format the data with the chat template and\nsystem prompts; mix in a share of general instruction data plus a safety set so the loss has a term for\nthe behaviours to keep; train adapters at a lower rate for fewer epochs with evaluation on a general suite\nat every checkpoint; and gate deployment on that suite. If a strong task checkpoint already exists,\ninterpolating it toward the base can recover much of the lost behaviour quickly while the proper run is\nprepared."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the root cause of catastrophic forgetting during fine-tuning?","options":["The model's parameter budget is full, so new knowledge must evict old knowledge","The fine-tuning loss has no term for the old tasks, so gradients freely move weights those tasks depended on","Adam's momentum carries the model past the optimum of the new task","The tokenizer vocabulary is changed by the new data"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shared weights are updated to reduce only the new loss; capacity is rarely the constraint, and neither momentum nor tokenisation explains why old capabilities degrade while new ones improve."},{"q":"Which mitigation requires access to data representative of the capabilities you want to keep?","options":["Lowering the learning rate","Training a low-rank adapter instead of the full model","Replay, mixing general data into the fine-tuning set","Early stopping on the task metric"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replay works by giving the loss a term for the old behaviour, which needs examples of it; the other options constrain the update without needing old data, and early stopping on the task metric alone does not detect forgetting at all."},{"q":"A team ships monthly fine-tunes, each trained from the previous month's model on that month's new data. What should change?","options":["Increase the learning rate so each month's data is learned faster","Train each release from the base model on the union of all months' data, with a general regression suite","Drop the evaluation suite because the task data is growing","Use a larger rank for the adapters each month"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chained fine-tunes forget a little of every earlier batch per hop; starting from the base on all data removes the compounding, makes runs reproducible, and the suite catches whatever is still lost."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/checkpointing","sources":["Mohan et al., 2021 — CheckFreq: Frequent, Fine-Grained DNN Checkpointing","Rajbhandari et al., 2020 — ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","Vaswani et al., 2017 — Attention Is All You Need","Official docs: PyTorch torch.save / Distributed Checkpoint; Hugging Face Trainer"],"why":"Training is a long, stateful computation on hardware that fails. A single-GPU fine-tune runs for hours on\na spot instance that can be reclaimed at any minute; a pretraining run occupies thousands of accelerators\nfor weeks, and with that many parts, hardware faults are routine rather than exceptional. The Llama 3\nreport, for example, counts hundreds of job interruptions during a 54-day pretraining window, most of\nthem unexpected. Without checkpoints every failure restarts from zero, and the expected time to finish\na run longer than the mean time between failures diverges: you never finish. With checkpoints every *T*\nminutes, a failure costs on average *T/2* minutes of recomputation plus the restart time.\n\nResumability is the forcing reason; two more follow from having the artefacts. Model selection: the best\nmodel is usually not the last one, because validation performance peaks and then overfitting sets in, so\nyou need the weights from the peak step, which only exist if you saved them. Rollback and reproducibility:\na deployed model that misbehaves needs a known-good predecessor, and a result nobody can reconstruct is\nnot a result. The naive alternative, saving only the weights at the end or at epoch boundaries, fails on\nall three counts: it cannot resume correctly (the optimizer state is gone), misses the peak, and leaves\nnothing to roll back to.","how":"### What a resumable checkpoint contains\nResuming means continuing the exact same trajectory, so everything that influences the next step must be\nsaved:\n- **Model weights**, in the training precision plus fp32 master weights under mixed precision.\n- **Optimizer state.** AdamW keeps two fp32 moments per parameter. Using the ZeRO accounting of about 16\n  bytes per parameter for weights, gradients and Adam state in mixed precision, a 7B model carries\n  roughly 100 GB of training state, most of it optimizer moments. Dropping them and resuming with fresh\n  moments produces a loss spike, because Adam's first steps after a reset are badly scaled.\n- **Scheduler state and step counter**, so the learning rate continues from where it was instead of\n  restarting warmup.\n- **Mixed-precision loss-scaler state**, where used.\n- **RNG states** (Python, NumPy, PyTorch CPU and CUDA) and the **data-loader position** (epoch, batches\n  consumed, sampler seed), so dropout masks and example order after resume match an uninterrupted run and\n  no example is skipped or seen twice.\n- **Config and tokenizer**, so the artefact can be loaded without the training code.\n\n### Frequency\nThe expected loss from a failure is half the interval, and each save costs write time during which the\nGPUs may idle. Saving a 100 GB state over a 1 GB/s link is about 100 seconds; saving every 30 minutes\ncosts about 5% of throughput in exchange for losing at most 30 minutes. Two techniques shrink the cost:\nasynchronous checkpointing copies the state to host memory in milliseconds and writes to storage in the\nbackground while training continues, and incremental schemes save only what changed. Pick the interval\nfrom failure rate, save cost and the value of lost minutes, and save more often near the end of a run.\n\n### Distributed layout\nUnder data parallelism every rank holds the same state and one rank writes it. Under ZeRO or FSDP the\noptimizer state and weights are sharded, so each rank writes its own shard in parallel, which is fast but\nties the checkpoint to that world size and sharding scheme. Resuming on a different number of GPUs\nrequires a resharding step; libraries such as PyTorch Distributed Checkpoint and DeepSpeed provide\nconsolidation utilities. Keep a consolidated, weights-only export alongside the sharded training state for\nevaluation and serving.\n\n### Atomicity and retention\nWrite to a temporary path and rename on completion, so a crash mid-write never leaves a truncated file\nmasquerading as the latest checkpoint; a manifest with a hash catches silent corruption. Retention policy:\nkeep the last *k* for resume, the best on validation for selection, and periodic ones (every *n* steps or\nevery epoch) for analysis, and delete the rest, because 100 GB per save fills any disk quickly.\n\n### Selection, averaging and formats\nRecord the validation metric with each checkpoint so \"best\" is a lookup, not a re-evaluation. Averaging\nthe weights of the last several checkpoints is a cheap, old trick that often improves the final model;\nthe original Transformer paper averaged the last 5 (base) or 20 (big) checkpoints. For export, prefer\n`safetensors` over pickled `torch.save` files: a pickle can execute arbitrary code on load, which is why\n`torch.load` now defaults to `weights_only=True` and why weights from untrusted sources should never be\nunpickled. With adapters, the checkpoint is the adapter weights plus an exact reference (name and hash)\nto the base model they were trained on; without that reference the adapter is unusable.","example":"A compact save/load pair for a single-process PyTorch loop: full state, atomic rename, keep-last-*k*\nretention, and RNG restoration. The Hugging Face `Trainer` does the same through `save_steps`,\n`save_total_limit`, `load_best_model_at_end`, `metric_for_best_model` and `resume_from_checkpoint`.\n\n```python\nimport os, random, numpy as np, torch\n\ndef save_checkpoint(dirpath, step, model, opt, sched, scaler, sampler_state, keep_last=3):\n    state = {\"step\": step, \"model\": model.state_dict(), \"optimizer\": opt.state_dict(),\n             \"scheduler\": sched.state_dict(), \"scaler\": scaler.state_dict() if scaler else None,\n             \"sampler\": sampler_state,                                   # epoch, batches consumed, seed\n             \"rng\": {\"torch\": torch.get_rng_state(), \"cuda\": torch.cuda.get_rng_state_all(),\n                     \"python\": random.getstate(), \"numpy\": np.random.get_state()}}\n    tmp, final = f\"{dirpath}/ckpt-{step}.pt.tmp\", f\"{dirpath}/ckpt-{step}.pt\"\n    torch.save(state, tmp)\n    os.replace(tmp, final)             # atomic rename: a crash mid-write never leaves a half file\n    saved = sorted((int(f.split(\"-\")[1].split(\".\")[0]), f) for f in os.listdir(dirpath) if f.endswith(\".pt\"))\n    for _, old in saved[:-keep_last]:  # retention: keep the newest k; keep \"best\" elsewhere\n        os.remove(f\"{dirpath}/{old}\")\n\ndef load_checkpoint(path, model, opt, sched, scaler):\n    state = torch.load(path, map_location=\"cpu\", weights_only=False)  # trusted file only: it is a pickle\n    model.load_state_dict(state[\"model\"]); opt.load_state_dict(state[\"optimizer\"])\n    sched.load_state_dict(state[\"scheduler\"])\n    if scaler and state[\"scaler\"]:\n        scaler.load_state_dict(state[\"scaler\"])\n    torch.set_rng_state(state[\"rng\"][\"torch\"]); torch.cuda.set_rng_state_all(state[\"rng\"][\"cuda\"])\n    random.setstate(state[\"rng\"][\"python\"]); np.random.set_state(state[\"rng\"][\"numpy\"])\n    return state[\"step\"], state[\"sampler\"]          # caller fast-forwards the data loader from these\n\n# In the loop:  if step % SAVE_EVERY == 0: save_checkpoint(CKPT_DIR, step, model, opt, sched, scaler,\n#                   {\"epoch\": epoch, \"consumed\": batches_this_epoch, \"seed\": 1234 + epoch})\n# On startup:   step, sampler = load_checkpoint(latest, model, opt, sched, scaler); skip `consumed` batches.\n```\n\nThe caller restores the data order by reseeding the sampler with the saved epoch seed and skipping the\nconsumed batches, which is the part most hand-written loops forget.","tradeoffs":["Frequent saves bound the work lost to a failure but cost write time and storage; the balance moves toward more frequent saves as failure rates rise and toward fewer as the save itself becomes slow relative to the interval.","Sharded checkpoints write in parallel and avoid gathering 100 GB onto one rank, but tie the artefact to a world size and need a resharding step to resume elsewhere.","Weights-only exports are small and serve directly but cannot resume training; full state resumes exactly but is several times larger, so most teams keep both.","Keeping many checkpoints enables rollback, averaging and post-hoc analysis, but at 100 GB each the storage bill becomes the constraint, which is what retention policies trade against.","Asynchronous checkpointing hides the write latency but needs host-memory headroom for a full copy of the state and adds a failure mode in which the background write itself fails silently."],"pitfalls":["Saving only weights and resuming with a fresh optimizer; the symptom is a loss spike immediately after resume and a resumed run that never rejoins the original curve.","Not saving the data-loader position; the symptom is examples seen twice or never in the resumed epoch, and a loss curve after resume that differs from an uninterrupted run.","Writing checkpoints in place without a temporary file and rename; the symptom is a truncated latest checkpoint after a crash and no resumable state at all.","Letting the checkpoint directory fill the disk; the symptom is the run dying during a save, often taking the latest good checkpoint with it.","A retention rule that deletes the best checkpoint because it is old; the symptom is a final model worse than one that existed an hour earlier. The Hugging Face `Trainer` retains the best checkpoint when `load_best_model_at_end` is set; hand-written loops must do the same explicitly.","Saving adapters without recording the base model's exact version; the symptom is an adapter that loads without error onto a different base and produces garbage.","Loading pickled checkpoints from untrusted sources; the symptom is arbitrary code running on load."],"when":"Every training run longer than a few minutes should checkpoint, and every run whose output will be\ndeployed should keep the best checkpoint by validation metric. Decide the interval from failure rate and\nsave cost, decide the format from how the run is parallelised and who consumes the artefact, and decide\nretention from storage and the need for rollback. Measure save time as a share of step time and the\nrecovery time after a simulated preemption. In day-to-day work this is a configuration task in the\ntraining framework; in a platform role it becomes storage layout, model registry integration and\npromotion rules. In interviews, expect to list what a resumable checkpoint must contain and to explain\nwhy weights alone are insufficient.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures save interval, retention and best-checkpoint selection in the trainer, tests a resume after a kill, and exports weights-only `safetensors` for evaluation and serving.","architect":"Owns checkpoint storage layout, sharding strategy, the registry that records which checkpoint became which deployed model, and the rollback path to a previous one.","fde":"On a customer's cluster, verifies resume works under their preemption policy and that checkpoints and adapter references are stored where the customer's governance requires."},"check":{"q":"A fine-tuning job on spot GPUs was preempted at step 3,200 and resumed from a checkpoint that\ncontained only `model.state_dict()`. The loss jumped from 0.9 to 1.6 on resume and never recovered the\noriginal curve. What went wrong and what should the checkpoint have contained?","a":"The resumed run restarted AdamW with empty moment estimates, so its first steps were badly scaled\nand large, and the learning-rate scheduler restarted from step zero, re-running warmup and resetting the\ndecay horizon, so the trajectory diverged from the original. The sampler also restarted, so the epoch's\ndata order and position were lost. A resumable checkpoint needs the model weights, the optimizer state\nincluding both Adam moments, the scheduler state and global step, the mixed-precision scaler state if\nused, the RNG states, and the data-loader position (epoch, batches consumed, seed), written atomically\nvia rename and retained under a policy that keeps the latest few and the best by validation metric."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does resuming from a weights-only checkpoint typically produce a loss spike?","options":["The weights are saved in lower precision and lose information","The optimizer's moment estimates and the scheduler step are reset, so the first updates are badly scaled and warmup restarts","The data loader starts from the first example of the dataset again","Gradient checkpointing must be re-enabled after a resume"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adam's steps depend on accumulated moments and the schedule position; fresh moments and a restarted warmup change the step size abruptly. Data order matters for reproducibility but does not by itself cause a spike."},{"q":"Which practice prevents a crash during a save from destroying the latest usable checkpoint?","options":["Saving checkpoints more frequently","Using sharded checkpoints across ranks","Writing to a temporary file and atomically renaming it on completion","Compressing the checkpoint before writing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A crash mid-write leaves a truncated file; writing to a temporary path and renaming means the final name only ever points at a complete file. Frequency and sharding change cost, not atomicity."},{"q":"How is checkpointing related to gradient checkpointing?","options":["Gradient checkpointing saves gradients to disk between steps","They are the same feature enabled by one flag in most trainers","They are unrelated: checkpointing persists training state, gradient checkpointing recomputes activations to save memory","Gradient checkpointing is the sharded form of checkpointing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The shared name is historical. One is about durability and resume; the other trades compute for activation memory during backward and writes nothing to storage."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/gradient-checkpointing","sources":["Chen et al., 2016 — Training Deep Nets with Sublinear Memory Cost","Korthikanti et al., 2022 — Reducing Activation Recomputation in Large Transformer Models","Official docs: PyTorch torch.utils.checkpoint"],"why":"Training memory has four parts: weights, gradients, optimizer state and activations. The first three are\na fixed number of bytes per parameter (about 16 with AdamW in mixed precision) and PEFT and quantization\nattack them. Activations are different: they scale with batch × sequence length × hidden size × layers,\nand they exist only because backprop needs them. For a linear layer `y = xW`, the weight gradient is\n`xᵀ · dL/dy`, so `x` must be kept; GELU needs its input; softmax needs its output. The autograd engine\ntherefore holds every intermediate tensor from the forward pass until the backward pass consumes it, and\nbackward runs last-layer-first, so at the end of the forward pass *all* of them are live at once.\n\nPut numbers on it. Korthikanti et al. estimate the activations of one transformer layer in bf16, without\nFlashAttention, at roughly `s·b·h·(34 + 5·a·s/h)` bytes (s = sequence, b = batch, h = hidden, a = heads).\nFor a 7B-class model (h = 4096, a = 32, 32 layers) at s = 4096, b = 1: the linear term 34·s·b·h is about\n570 MB per layer and the attention-score term 5·a·s²·b is about 2.7 GB per layer, so ~105 GB in total.\nFlashAttention removes the s² term by never materialising the score matrix, which leaves ~18 GB, and that\nfigure doubles at 8k. A QLoRA run whose weights plus adapter plus optimizer state fit in 6 GB still\ncannot fit on a 24 GB card at 8k context because of activations alone (all figures illustrative, from\nthe formula above).\n\nThe naive alternatives all fail on this shape of problem. Gradient accumulation shrinks the micro-batch,\nbut you are already at b = 1. Shortening the sequence changes the task you are training for. A bigger GPU\nis a procurement ladder, not an engineering answer. Gradient checkpointing is the lever that trades the\none resource GPUs have in abundance, compute, for the one they lack.","how":"### The observation\nA block's internal activations are a deterministic function of its input (dropout aside, which is handled\nby replaying the RNG state). If you still have the input, you can rebuild the internals whenever you need\nthem. So you do not have to keep them across the whole forward pass; you only need them during that\nblock's own backward step.\n\n### The algorithm\n1. Partition the network into segments. In transformers the common choice is one decoder block per\n   segment, because block boundaries are where the activation tensor is smallest (just `s·b·h`).\n2. In the forward pass, run each segment without recording the autograd graph. Keep only the segment's\n   input tensor; let its internal activations be freed.\n3. In the backward pass, when the gradient reaches segment *k*'s output, re-run segment *k*'s forward\n   from its stored input with graph recording on, backpropagate through that local graph, free it, and\n   move on to segment *k − 1*.\n\nPeak activation memory becomes (all segment inputs) + (one segment's full internals). With n layers and\nsegments of √n layers each, both terms are O(√n), which is the \"sublinear memory\" result of Chen et al.\nPer-block checkpointing in a transformer stores n block inputs, each `s·b·h·2` bytes, plus one block's\ninternals. In the 7B example at 4k: 32 × 33.5 MB ≈ 1.1 GB of inputs plus ~570 MB for the live block,\ndown from ~18 GB.\n\nThe cost is that each segment's forward runs twice. Forward is roughly a third of a training step's FLOPs\n(backward is about twice forward), so the worst case is about a third more compute; the commonly quoted\nwall-clock slowdown is in the 20–30% range (illustrative; it depends on how much of the step is\ncommunication or data loading rather than math).\n\n### Selective recomputation\nNot every activation costs the same to store or to recompute. Korthikanti et al. point out that the\nattention scores and their softmax/dropout outputs are the entire s² term yet are cheap to recompute, so\nrecomputing *only* those gives most of the memory benefit for a fraction of the overhead. FlashAttention\ndoes exactly this implicitly: it saves the per-row log-sum-exp and rebuilds score tiles in backward. The\ntwo techniques compose: per-block checkpointing cuts *both* terms from \"n layers' worth\" to \"one live\nlayer's worth\", and FlashAttention then removes the s² term from that one live layer as well. The second\nstep matters more than it sounds: the live block's eager-attention score tensors are `5·a·s²·b` bytes,\nabout 2.7 GB for one 7B layer at 4k and ~10.7 GB at 8k, so without FlashAttention that single block sets\na memory floor that no amount of checkpointing lowers.\n\n### Framework mechanics and the reentrant trap\nPyTorch exposes `torch.utils.checkpoint.checkpoint(fn, *args, use_reentrant=False)`. There are two\nimplementations. The older *reentrant* one runs the segment under `no_grad` and re-enters autograd in\nbackward; its sharp edge is that if **no input to the segment requires grad**, autograd never records\nthe segment at all, so parameters inside it silently receive no gradient. That is the LoRA case: the\nembedding output does not require grad because the embeddings are frozen. PEFT recipes work around it\nwith `model.enable_input_require_grads()`. The *non-reentrant* implementation uses saved-tensor hooks,\nhandles inputs without grad correctly, and is the recommended mode. In Transformers you enable it with\n`model.gradient_checkpointing_enable(gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={\"use_reentrant\": False})` or\n`TrainingArguments(gradient_checkpointing=True)`. Transformers also forces `use_cache=False` when\ncheckpointing is on, because the KV cache that generation builds is pointless during training and\nconflicts with recomputation.\n\n### Where it sits among the other levers\nGradient accumulation cuts the b in `s·b·h`; sequence or context parallelism cuts the s by splitting it\nacross GPUs; FlashAttention cuts the s² term; CPU offloading of activations moves them across PCIe, which\nis usually slower than simply recomputing. With FSDP, checkpoint per block *inside* each FSDP unit so\nthat recomputation does not trigger extra parameter all-gathers.","example":"A residual MLP stack with and without checkpointing. Weights and gradients are identical in both runs\n(about 6 GB in fp32 for this configuration), so the entire difference in peak memory is activations: the\ncheckpointed run keeps only each block's `[tokens, d]` input and rebuilds the `4·d`-wide hidden and GELU\ntensors during backward.\n\n```python\nimport torch, torch.nn as nn\nfrom torch.utils.checkpoint import checkpoint\n\nclass Block(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.ff = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(d, 4 * d), nn.GELU(), nn.Linear(4 * d, d))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return x + self.ff(x)\n\nclass Net(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, d, n, ckpt):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.blocks = nn.ModuleList(Block(d) for _ in range(n))\n        self.ckpt = ckpt\n    def forward(self, x):\n        for b in self.blocks:\n            x = checkpoint(b, x, use_reentrant=False) if self.ckpt else b(x)\n        return x\n\ndef peak_gb(ckpt, d=2048, n=24, tokens=4096):\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache(); torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\n    net = Net(d, n, ckpt).cuda()\n    x = torch.randn(tokens, d, device=\"cuda\", requires_grad=True)\n    net(x).sum().backward()\n    return torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated() / 2**30\n\nfor flag in (False, True):\n    print(f\"checkpointing={flag}: peak {peak_gb(flag):.1f} GB\")\n# Expect the checkpointed peak to be roughly half; raise `tokens` and the gap widens, because\n# stored activations grow with tokens while the weight/gradient share stays fixed.\n# Hugging Face equivalent: model.gradient_checkpointing_enable(\n#     gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={\"use_reentrant\": False})\n```","tradeoffs":["You pay roughly one extra forward pass per step (20–30% wall-clock, illustrative) for a large cut in activation memory; if you are compute-bound with memory to spare, it is pure loss and should be off.","The freed memory usually lets you raise the micro-batch, and a GPU at micro-batch 1 is badly under-utilised, so tokens/second often goes *up* overall despite the recompute; this flips once the batch already saturates the GPU.","Per-block granularity is simple and matches FSDP wrapping; coarser segments store fewer boundary inputs but hold a larger live segment during recompute (total recompute is one extra forward either way), and the √n split that balances the two is rarely worth the engineering over per-block.","Non-reentrant mode handles frozen inputs and nested checkpoints correctly; reentrant mode can have slightly lower overhead in some stacks but fails silently in the LoRA case, so the default should be non-reentrant."],"pitfalls":["Confusing it with model checkpointing (saving state to disk); the symptom is someone enabling \"checkpointing\" for resumability, losing a multi-day run, and discovering nothing was ever written.","Reentrant mode with frozen embeddings in a LoRA run; the symptom is a flat loss curve and adapter gradients that are `None`, fixed by `enable_input_require_grads()` or `use_reentrant=False`.","Enabling checkpointing and still hitting OOM at long context; the symptom is a peak that fell a lot but stalls at a floor of several GB per live block, because eager attention materialises that block's s² score tensors (~10 GB for one 7B layer at 8k) during recompute and only FlashAttention/SDPA removes them.","Measuring \"steps got slower\" and switching it off globally instead of raising the micro-batch to recover utilisation; the right metric is tokens per second, not seconds per step.","Side effects inside a checkpointed function (counters, logging, in-place updates, custom RNG not covered by `preserve_rng_state`) run twice; the symptom is doubled metrics or a gradient mismatch versus the uncheckpointed run."],"when":"Reach for it when activation memory is the thing that does not fit, which you diagnose by computing the\n`s·b·h·34` estimate or by comparing `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()` with and without the flag. In\npractice it is on by default for any fine-tune at 4k+ context on a single GPU, and it is what makes\nQLoRA on a 24 GB card at 8k realistic. Leave it off for small models and short sequences where the GPU is\ncompute-bound and memory is plentiful. Always pair it with FlashAttention or SDPA, re-tune the micro-batch\nafter enabling it, and judge the result by tokens/second. In interviews, the expected depth is to separate\nthe per-parameter memory (weights, gradients, optimizer) from activation memory, state what checkpointing\ntrades, explain the √n argument, and name the reentrant trap.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Flips the flag in `TrainingArguments`, Axolotl or Unsloth, pairs it with FlashAttention, raises the micro-batch until memory is full again, and checks tokens/second against the baseline.","architect":"Sets the sequence-length-versus-GPU-tier policy knowing that checkpointing lets a 24 GB card handle an 8k QLoRA, so the capacity plan is not \"buy 80 GB cards\" by default.","fde":"Fits a customer's long-document fine-tune onto the fixed GPU quota in their VPC instead of waiting on a procurement cycle, and explains the 20–30% step-time cost up front."},"check":{"q":"A team runs QLoRA on a 7B model at 8k context on a 24 GB GPU. The 4-bit weights are ~4 GB and the\nadapter plus optimizer state is tiny, yet they OOM at micro-batch 1. Explain what is using the memory and\nwhat you would change, with rough numbers.","a":"The rest is activations. With h = 4096 and 32 layers, the linear term 34·s·b·h at s = 8192 is about\n1.1 GB per layer, roughly 36 GB across the model even with FlashAttention; without it the attention-score\nterm 5·a·s²·b adds about 10 GB per layer. So first turn on FlashAttention or SDPA to delete the s² term,\nthen enable gradient checkpointing in non-reentrant mode (or call `enable_input_require_grads()`), which\ncuts stored activations to one block input per layer (~67 MB each, ~2 GB total) plus a single live block\n(~1.1 GB). That fits with room to raise the micro-batch, at roughly a 20–30% per-step cost. Verify with\n`max_memory_allocated()` before and after, and judge the change by tokens per second."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does gradient checkpointing trade, and for what?","options":["Disk space for GPU memory, by paging activations to NVMe","Extra forward compute for activation memory, by recomputing block internals during backward","Numerical precision for memory, by storing activations in 8-bit","Communication bandwidth for memory, by sharding activations across GPUs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Checkpointing keeps only block inputs and re-runs each block's forward during the backward pass. Nothing leaves the GPU and nothing changes precision; the price is roughly one additional forward pass of compute."},{"q":"A LoRA run with reentrant checkpointing shows a flat loss and `None` adapter gradients. Why?","options":["LoRA adapters cannot be placed inside a checkpointed segment","4-bit base weights cannot be recomputed during the backward pass","No segment input requires grad because embeddings are frozen, so autograd never records the segment","The RNG state is not restored, so dropout masks differ between the two forward passes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In reentrant mode the segment is only attached to the graph through inputs that require grad. Frozen embeddings produce inputs that do not, so the adapters inside get no gradient. `enable_input_require_grads()` or `use_reentrant=False` fixes it."},{"q":"A 7B QLoRA run at 8k context enables per-block checkpointing; peak memory drops sharply but the run still OOMs on a 24 GB card during backward. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Optimizer state dominates and checkpointing does not touch it","The one live block being recomputed still materialises its s² attention-score tensors because FlashAttention/SDPA is off","Too many gradient-accumulation steps are keeping old activations alive","The checkpoint files on disk are too large for the page cache"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Per-block checkpointing shrinks stored activations to the block inputs plus one live block, but inside that live block eager attention still builds the full `5·a·s²·b` score tensors, about 10 GB for a single 7B layer at 8k, so the floor is set by one block and only a kernel that never materialises the matrix removes it. Optimizer state in QLoRA is tiny, and accumulation steps do not keep activations alive between micro-batches."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/fine-tune-vs-rag-vs-prompt-decision","sources":["Lewis et al., 2020 — Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks","Ovadia et al., 2023 — Fine-Tuning or Retrieval? Comparing Knowledge Injection in LLMs","Zhang et al., 2024 — RAFT: Adapting Language Model to Domain Specific RAG"],"why":"\"Make the model know our stuff\", \"make it sound like us\", \"make it stop doing X\" arrive every week, and\nthe reflex answer is \"fine-tune it\", because training feels like the real engineering. That reflex is\nexpensive and usually wrong for the first request. Fine-tuning on a pile of documents does not reliably\nmake a model recall their contents: the loss is next-token prediction over a handful of phrasings, which\nis nothing like the massive repetition and paraphrase that got facts into the model during pretraining.\nOvadia et al. compared knowledge injection methods and found retrieval consistently beat unsupervised\nfine-tuning on new facts. Even when fine-tuned knowledge does stick, it is frozen at training time,\ncannot be scoped per tenant or deleted, carries no citation, and has to be redone when the base model is\nupgraded.\n\nThe opposite reflex, \"just prompt harder\", has its own ceilings. Prompts grow to thousands of tokens of\ninstructions and examples, which costs money and latency on every call; few-shot examples cannot pin\ndown a precise output distribution; and some behaviours (strict schema compliance in a small model,\na domain's tone, a tool-calling idiom) never stabilise by instruction alone.\n\nRAG sits between them and is often the answer, but only for one kind of gap: it helps when the model is\n*missing* information that exists in retrievable text. It does nothing when the model has the\ninformation and still does the wrong thing with it, and it introduces retrieval quality as a new failure\nmode.\n\nSo the decision needs a framework that maps the symptom to the lever, and a measurement loop that proves\nthe cheaper lever insufficient before paying for the next one. Without it, teams spend two weeks on a\nLoRA to fix what a 200-token system prompt edit would have fixed, or build a retrieval stack to fix a\nformatting problem.","how":"### Three levers, three different objects\n| Lever | What it changes | Good at | Weak at | Cost shape |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Prompt (system prompt, few-shot, structured output) | The input of this request | Instructions, format, small behaviour shifts, per-deployment config | Precise distributions, very long rule sets, small-model compliance | Tokens × requests, partly offset by prompt caching |\n| RAG | The input, with retrieved evidence | Facts, freshness, per-user/tenant scoping, citations, deletion | Behaviour the model already gets wrong with the facts in hand | Index, embeddings, retrieval quality, ops |\n| Fine-tune (SFT/DPO, usually LoRA) | The weights | Default style, format, task skill, tool idiom, compressing a long prompt, distilling into a smaller model | Facts that change, facts needing attribution, anything still being redesigned | Data, GPU time, evals, re-doing on base-model upgrades, forgetting risk |\n\n### The decision procedure\n1. **Name the gap with failing examples.** Collect 50–200 real failures and sort them: the model lacked\n   information (knowledge gap); it had the information and still answered wrongly, in the wrong form or\n   tone (behaviour gap); it was right but too slow or expensive (economics gap). Most requests contain\n   two gaps tangled together, and the lever is different for each.\n2. **Knowledge gap → RAG.** Or long context, if the corpus is small, static and the token cost is\n   acceptable. Do not fine-tune to inject the content. If the model handles retrieved passages badly\n   (ignores them, cannot say \"not in the documents\"), that is a behaviour gap: fine-tune it to *use*\n   retrieved context (the RAFT idea), still keeping the facts in the index.\n3. **Behaviour gap → prompt first.** System prompt, a few curated examples, structured-output mode. Build\n   the eval set from the failures and score it. If the score plateaus below target, or the prompt needed\n   to reach it is so long that it dominates per-request cost, move to a LoRA SFT on the same reviewed\n   examples; use DPO when the failures are preference-shaped (two answers, one better) rather than\n   right/wrong.\n4. **Economics gap → fine-tune.** Distil the big model's outputs into a smaller model, or train the\n   few-shot block into the weights so the prompt shrinks; verify on the same eval set that quality held.\n5. **Combine.** The usual end state of a production domain assistant is RAG for facts, a fine-tuned or\n   well-prompted model for behaviour, and a prompt for per-deployment configuration. The levers are not\n   exclusive; the mistake is using the wrong one for a given gap.\n\n### Why fine-tuning is weak at facts\nSFT shifts probability mass toward the sequences it sees. A fact seen a few times in one phrasing does\nnot become reliably retrievable under other phrasings, which is what question answering requires. RAG\nputs the fact in the context window, where attention can copy it directly, so the model needs no\nmemorisation at all. This is also why fine-tuned \"knowledge\" fails silently: the model produces\nplausible details in the style of the training data rather than refusing.\n\n### Reversibility and the upgrade tax\nA prompt change is one deploy with instant rollback and transfers largely unchanged to a newer base\nmodel. A RAG index is updated incrementally and supports deletion, and it transfers to any model.\nA fine-tune is a new artifact that must be re-evaluated, rolled back by swapping adapters, and\nre-trained for every base-model release; with proprietary APIs it may also lock you to one vendor's\nfine-tuning product. That tax is why \"prompt until proven insufficient\" is the default ordering, not\na statement that fine-tuning is bad.","example":"A support assistant for a SaaS product (all numbers illustrative). Three complaints: it does not know\nthe product docs, which change weekly; it should answer in brand voice as a fixed JSON object; and it\nruns 2 million requests a month with a 5,000-token prompt of instructions and twelve examples.\n\nSort the gaps. The docs are a knowledge gap: build RAG over the docs with weekly re-indexing, so new\npages are answerable the day they ship and retired pages stop being cited. Voice and JSON are behaviour\ngaps: tighten the system prompt, enable structured-output mode, and measure on 200 reviewed cases.\nSuppose that reaches 97% valid JSON and a tone score of 4.1/5 on a rubric, against targets of 99.5% and\n4.5. The prompt has plateaued, so fine-tune: a LoRA SFT on 1,500 reviewed answers reaches 99.7% and 4.6,\nand the twelve examples can leave the prompt, which shrinks to 800 tokens.\n\nNow the economics. Input tokens drop from 5,000 × 2M = 10 billion to 800 × 2M = 1.6 billion per month,\nabout 6× fewer; with prompt caching the cached share is discounted, so the realised saving is smaller\nbut still material. The fine-tune costs a few GPU-hours per iteration plus the eval harness, repeated at\neach base-model upgrade. Knowledge stayed in the index; the weights only learned behaviour; the system\nprompt still carries per-customer configuration.","tradeoffs":["Prompting costs nothing to try and rolls back instantly, but every request pays for the prompt and quality plateaus; it flips to fine-tuning when a multi-thousand-token prompt runs at high volume or evals stall below target.","RAG gives freshness, citations, access control and deletion, but you now own a retrieval system whose recall becomes the dominant failure mode; it flips to plain long context when the corpus is small and rarely changes, and it never flips to fine-tuning for facts.","Fine-tuning gives behaviour you cannot prompt into existence and a cheaper per-request path, but costs data, evals, forgetting risk and re-training at every base-model release; it flips back to prompting when the task is still changing or the model is upgraded often.","Combining RAG with a fine-tuned model is the strongest configuration, but doubles the moving parts; it only pays off when the team can run the same eval set across both layers after every change."],"pitfalls":["Fine-tuning to inject facts; the symptom is confident, plausible details that match the training data's style but not its content, and no way to answer questions about last week's changes.","Skipping the eval set; the symptom is \"the fine-tuned model feels better\" with no number attached, and regressions on other tasks discovered by users rather than by the team.","Building RAG for a behaviour gap; the symptom is retrieval returning the right passage while the model still answers in the wrong format or ignores the passage.","Fine-tuning before the prompt has been seriously tried; the symptom is a two-week training project that a short system-prompt edit would have resolved.","Ignoring the upgrade tax; the symptom is a fine-tuned model blocking a migration to a stronger base model for months because nobody budgeted to re-train and re-evaluate.","Treating long context as free RAG; the symptom is per-request cost blowing up and lost-in-the-middle errors on the documents that matter most."],"when":"Use the procedure every time someone asks for a model to \"know\", \"sound like\" or \"stop doing\" something.\nStart by classifying failures, then prompt, then RAG for knowledge, then LoRA for behaviour or cost, and\nmeasure the same eval set at every rung. Reach for fine-tuning when the behaviour is stable and the prompt\nhas plateaued or become the dominant per-request cost, when a smaller model must match a larger one on a\nnarrow task, or when the output distribution must be precise. Avoid it while the task is still being\ndesigned, when facts must be fresh or attributable, or when you cannot afford to re-train at the next\nbase-model release. In interviews, the expected answer is never \"fine-tune\" or \"RAG\" alone; it is the\ngap classification, the ordering, and the measurement loop that justifies each step.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the eval set from real failures first, ships the prompt, then the retrieval layer, then a LoRA, scoring the same set at every rung and owning the prompt-token budget.","architect":"Owns the lever policy: which knowledge must stay in retrieval for freshness, access control or compliance, which behaviours may move into weights, and the plan for base-model upgrades.","fde":"Translates the customer's \"it doesn't know our business\" into the knowledge/behaviour split in week one, and declines the request to fine-tune on the company wiki with the reasoning to back it."},"check":{"q":"A customer says: \"The model doesn't know our product catalogue and keeps answering in the wrong\ntone, so let's fine-tune it on our docs.\" How do you respond, and what plan do you propose?","a":"Separate the two gaps. The catalogue is knowledge that changes, must be scoped and must be\nattributable, so it belongs in retrieval: fine-tuning on the docs will not make SKUs reliably recallable,\ncannot remove discontinued items, and gives no citations. Tone is behaviour: tighten the system prompt\nwith a few curated examples first and score it on a rubric over 100–200 real conversations. If that\nplateaus below target, train a LoRA on reviewed answers for tone only, keeping facts in the index. Later,\nif the model mishandles retrieved passages, fine-tune it to use context rather than to contain it.\nPropose the measurement plan up front so each step is justified by a number, not a feeling."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which situation is RAG the right lever for?","options":["The model has the facts but formats answers inconsistently","The model lacks information that exists in documents and changes over time","The model is accurate but too slow and expensive at current volume","The model refuses too many legitimate requests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"RAG supplies missing, changing, attributable information at request time. Formatting and refusal are behaviour gaps for prompting or fine-tuning; cost is an economics gap for distillation or prompt compression."},{"q":"Why is supervised fine-tuning a poor way to inject facts?","options":["Fine-tuning cannot modify attention-layer weights, only the output head","Next-token training on a few phrasings does not make facts reliably retrievable, and it offers no freshness or citation","LoRA's low rank is too small to store any factual content","Fine-tuning only changes the tokenizer vocabulary, not the model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SFT shifts probabilities toward seen sequences; it does not build the robust, paraphrase-invariant recall that pretraining's massive repetition produces. Retrieval puts the fact in context, where attention can copy it."},{"q":"When does fine-tuning beat prompting on cost?","options":["Always, because fine-tuned models are inherently smaller","When the task definition changes every week","When a long few-shot prompt at high volume dominates per-request cost and the behaviour is stable","When the base model is upgraded every month"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Training the examples into the weights removes thousands of tokens from every request, which pays off at volume; frequent task or base-model changes mean re-training repeatedly, which erases that saving."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/tooling","sources":["Official docs: TRL (Hugging Face)","Official docs: Axolotl","Official docs: Unsloth","Hu et al., 2021 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models"],"why":"Before these tools every fine-tune was a bespoke script, and the hard part was never the training loop;\nit was the dozen details around it. Chat data has to be rendered through the exact template the model\nexpects. The loss has to be masked so the model learns the assistant turns, not the user's questions.\nSequences have to be packed without letting examples attend to each other. LoRA has to be injected into\nthe right projection matrices. Mixed precision, gradient checkpointing, a 4-bit base with paged\noptimizers, checkpoint and resume, logging: each is a few lines, and each is easy to get subtly wrong.\nPreference training adds a second layer, because DPO needs log-probabilities from a reference model on\nexactly the right tokens, and GRPO needs a generation loop, reward functions and group-relative\nadvantages.\n\nThe damaging property of these mistakes is that they do not crash. Loss on the prompt tokens gives a\nmodel that parrots questions. A template mismatch between training and serving gives a model that\nlooked fine in the notebook and misbehaves in vLLM. A padded-token bug in DPO gives a model that is\nslightly worse for reasons nobody can diagnose. Teams that write the loop from scratch reproduce these\nbugs one at a time, over weeks.\n\nThe tools encode the known-good recipe so the only variables left are the ones that should vary: data,\nhyperparameters and evaluation. A hand-written loop is still the right way to *learn* what happens, and\nthe right escape hatch for a genuinely new loss, but not the way to ship a fine-tune.","how":"### TRL: trainers as the primitive\nTRL sits on top of `transformers` and PEFT. Unsloth hands its patched model to TRL trainers, and Axolotl\nuses TRL for its preference and RL trainers (its SFT path subclasses the Transformers `Trainer` directly,\nwith its own packing and loss masking). `SFTTrainer` with\n`SFTConfig` accepts a dataset of `messages` (conversational) or plain `text`, renders it with the\ntokenizer's chat template, optionally packs sequences, applies LoRA through a `peft_config`, and runs\nthe standard `Trainer` loop. `DPOTrainer` takes `prompt`/`chosen`/`rejected` rows, computes the\nlog-probability ratio of each answer against a reference model, and minimises\n`−log σ(β · (Δ_chosen − Δ_rejected))`; with a LoRA policy the reference is the base model with the\nadapter disabled, so no second copy is held in memory. `GRPOTrainer` samples several completions per\nprompt (optionally through vLLM), scores them with plain Python reward functions, and normalises\nrewards within each group to form advantages. `RewardTrainer` and `PPOTrainer` cover the classic RLHF\npath. You write Python, every piece is overridable, and that is the point: TRL is the layer for people\nwho need control or a custom loss.\n\n### Axolotl: the run as a config file\nAxolotl turns a run into a YAML document: base model, adapter type (full, LoRA, QLoRA), LoRA rank and\ntargets, datasets with their format type (`alpaca`, `sharegpt`, `chat_template`, and others), sequence\nlength, sample packing, micro-batch and accumulation, learning rate and scheduler, precision,\nFlashAttention, gradient checkpointing, DeepSpeed or FSDP settings, and experiment tracking. It\ntokenises and packs the data once and caches it, launches through `accelerate` so a one-GPU test and an\neight-GPU run use the same file, and can merge the adapter afterwards. It exists because the dataset\nformat matrix is a genuine pain and because a config file in git is a reproducible experiment; a Python\nscript with twenty keyword arguments is not.\n\n```yaml\nbase_model: Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct\nadapter: qlora\nload_in_4bit: true\nlora_r: 16\nlora_alpha: 32\nlora_target_linear: true\ndatasets:\n  - path: data/support_chats.jsonl\n    type: chat_template\nsequence_len: 4096\nsample_packing: true\nmicro_batch_size: 2\ngradient_accumulation_steps: 8\nnum_epochs: 2\nlearning_rate: 2e-4\nlr_scheduler: cosine\nbf16: auto\nflash_attention: true\ngradient_checkpointing: true\noutput_dir: ./out/support-qlora\n```\n\nRun it with the Axolotl CLI (`axolotl train config.yml`; older releases use\n`accelerate launch -m axolotl.cli.train config.yml`).\n\n### Unsloth: the kernels\nUnsloth replaces the attention, MLP and LoRA forward and backward passes of supported model families\nwith hand-written Triton kernels and manual autograd. Fewer intermediate tensors are materialised, more\noperations are fused, and the cross-entropy over the vocabulary is computed in chunks instead of as one\nhuge logits tensor; it also ships its own gradient-checkpointing variant that offloads block inputs to\nCPU asynchronously (`use_gradient_checkpointing=\"unsloth\"`). The API is a two-call wrapper:\n`FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained(name, max_seq_length=..., load_in_4bit=True)` then\n`FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model(model, r=16, target_modules=[...], lora_alpha=16, ...)`, after which\nyou hand the model to a TRL trainer as usual. The project's own figures are roughly 2× faster training\nand substantially lower VRAM than stock Transformers plus PEFT on the same GPU; treat those as vendor\nclaims and benchmark on your model. The sharp edges: only patched architectures are accelerated, and the\nopen-source release has historically been single-GPU focused, so check the current docs before planning\na multi-GPU run around it. It exports merged 16-bit weights, bare adapters, or GGUF for llama.cpp.\n\n### How they compose\nTRL is the base; Axolotl configures Transformers/TRL/PEFT/`accelerate` from YAML; Unsloth swaps in faster\nkernels underneath a TRL trainer. Similar config-driven frameworks exist (LLaMA-Factory, torchtune) and the\nsame mental model applies.","example":"A LoRA SFT on reviewed support conversations with TRL. The dataset rows carry a `messages` list, so the\ntrainer renders them with the model's chat template; the adapter is saved on its own and merged into the\nbf16 base later only if the serving stack needs a single checkpoint. The commented lines show the\nUnsloth swap-in.\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=\"bfloat16\")\n\n# rows: {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": ...}, {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": ...}]}\nds = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files=\"support_chats.jsonl\", split=\"train\")\n\npeft_cfg = LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05, task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n                      target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\",\n                                      \"gate_proj\", \"up_proj\", \"down_proj\"])\n\nargs = SFTConfig(\n    output_dir=\"out/support-lora\",\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4, gradient_accumulation_steps=4,   # effective batch 16\n    learning_rate=2e-4, num_train_epochs=2, lr_scheduler_type=\"cosine\", warmup_ratio=0.03,\n    bf16=True, gradient_checkpointing=True, packing=True,\n    max_length=2048,                     # older TRL releases call this max_seq_length\n    assistant_only_loss=True,            # needs a chat template that marks assistant turns;\n    logging_steps=10, save_strategy=\"epoch\",   # otherwise use prompt/completion rows\n)\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(model=model, args=args, train_dataset=ds,\n                     processing_class=tok,   # tokenizer= in older TRL releases\n                     peft_config=peft_cfg)\ntrainer.train()\ntrainer.save_model()                     # writes the adapter only\n\n# Unsloth variant: from unsloth import FastLanguageModel\n# model, tok = FastLanguageModel.from_pretrained(name, max_seq_length=2048, load_in_4bit=True)\n# model = FastLanguageModel.get_peft_model(model, r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=[...])\n# then build SFTTrainer(model=model, ...) with peft_config=None.\n```","tradeoffs":["TRL gives full control and the newest algorithms first, but you own the plumbing around the trainer (data formatting, launch, multi-GPU config); it flips toward Axolotl once a team runs many similar jobs.","Axolotl gives reproducible configs and painless multi-GPU, but a custom loss or unusual data flow means reading its source; it flips back to raw TRL when the experiment is not one of its supported shapes.","Unsloth gives real speed and memory savings on one GPU, but only for patched architectures and with single-GPU focus historically; it flips to Axolotl or plain FSDP when the job needs several GPUs.","Every layer of convenience hides a decision; the lower you go, the more you must verify yourself, and the higher you go, the more you must trust defaults you did not read."],"pitfalls":["Chat-template mismatch between training and serving; the symptom is a model that behaves in the training notebook but misbehaves or emits stray special tokens once deployed in vLLM or Ollama.","Computing the loss over prompt tokens; the symptom is suspiciously low training loss alongside a model that generates user-style questions or echoes instructions.","Packing sequences without per-example attention masking; the symptom is answers that drift into the topic of a neighbouring training example, which is context bleed across the pack.","Pinning nothing: TRL and Transformers rename arguments across releases (`tokenizer` → `processing_class`, `max_seq_length` → `max_length`); the symptom is a job that ran last month failing on an unknown keyword.","Merging a QLoRA adapter into the 4-bit base instead of the bf16 base; the symptom is a merged model that scores worse than the adapter-plus-base it came from.","Trusting Unsloth's speedup without checking the architecture list; the symptom is an error at load or a run that is no faster than stock Transformers."],"when":"Reach for TRL when you need a trainer class in your own Python, a custom reward or loss, or the newest\nmethod the day it lands. Reach for Axolotl when runs must be reproducible across people and machines,\nwhen you juggle several dataset formats, or when the job spans several GPUs. Reach for Unsloth when the\njob is a LoRA or QLoRA on one GPU and the model family is supported, because the saving in time and\nVRAM is large enough to change what fits. Whatever you pick, measure the same things: tokens per second,\npeak memory, eval score on a held-out set, and an end-to-end check through the serving stack's chat\ntemplate. In interviews, be ready to place the three on a control-versus-convenience axis and to name\nthe failure modes the tools exist to prevent.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the TRL or Axolotl config, verifies loss masking and the chat template on one example by hand, and benchmarks Unsloth against stock PEFT before committing to it.","architect":"Chooses the standard stack for the organisation so runs are reproducible and auditable, and decides where custom TRL code is allowed versus config-only training.","fde":"Gets a customer's first LoRA running on the GPU they already have, often Unsloth on one card, and leaves behind an Axolotl config the customer's team can re-run without you."},"check":{"q":"You have 3,000 reviewed support chats and a single 24 GB GPU. A colleague proposes writing a\ncustom PyTorch training loop. Which tool would you pick, why, and what decisions would still be yours?","a":"A QLoRA SFT through TRL's `SFTTrainer`, loaded with Unsloth if the model family is supported,\nbecause that fits a 7B-class model on 24 GB and removes the known bugs a hand-written loop reinvents:\ntemplate rendering, loss masking, packing, adapter injection and checkpointing. Axolotl would be the\npick if the run must be reproducible for others or move to several GPUs later. What remains yours: data\nquality and de-duplication, the chat template matching the serving stack, confirming the loss covers only\nassistant turns, LoRA rank, learning rate and epochs, a held-out eval set, and whether to serve the\nadapter or merge it into the bf16 base."},"quiz":[{"q":"How do TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth relate to each other?","options":["They are three competing training loops that cannot be combined","TRL provides the trainers; Axolotl configures them from YAML; Unsloth accelerates the kernels beneath them","Axolotl is the base library and TRL is its command-line wrapper","Unsloth replaces TRL entirely with its own trainer classes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TRL is the primitive layer. Axolotl orchestrates Transformers, TRL, PEFT and accelerate from a config file, and Unsloth patches the model's forward and backward passes, then hands the model to a TRL trainer."},{"q":"What is the actual mechanism behind Unsloth's speed and memory savings?","options":["It silently trains a smaller distilled model in place of the one you loaded","It skips the backward pass through all frozen base-model layers","Hand-written fused Triton kernels and manual backward passes that cut intermediate tensors and memory traffic","It performs all arithmetic in 4-bit integers instead of bf16"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The base weights may be stored in 4-bit, but compute still happens in 16-bit; the gain comes from fused kernels, chunked loss computation and fewer materialised intermediates, not from changing the model."},{"q":"Which symptom most strongly suggests the loss was not masked to assistant tokens?","options":["Out-of-memory errors in the first training step","A NaN loss after the warmup phase","Training loss that looks unusually low while the model generates user-style questions","Loss that never decreases from its initial value"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Averaging the loss over the easy-to-predict user turns pulls the number down, and the model learns to continue conversations in the user's voice because that is what it was trained to predict."}]},{"key":"training-adaptation/distributed-training","sources":["Rajbhandari et al., 2020 — ZeRO: Memory Optimizations Toward Training Trillion Parameter Models","Shoeybi et al., 2019 — Megatron-LM: Training Multi-Billion Parameter Language Models Using Model Parallelism","Huang et al., 2019 — GPipe: Efficient Training of Giant Neural Networks using Pipeline Parallelism","Zhao et al., 2023 — PyTorch FSDP: Experiences on Scaling Fully Sharded Data Parallel"],"why":"Two separate limits force training across GPUs: memory and time.\n\nMemory first. Mixed-precision training with Adam keeps, per parameter, a bf16 weight (2 bytes), a bf16\ngradient (2), an fp32 master weight (4) and two fp32 Adam moments (8): 16 bytes, which is the accounting\nthe ZeRO paper uses. A 7B model is therefore ~112 GB of state before a single activation, and a 70B model\nis ~1.1 TB. No single accelerator holds that. Even a LoRA fine-tune of a 70B model needs its 140 GB of\nbf16 base weights to live somewhere.\n\nThen time. Training compute is roughly 6 × parameters × tokens FLOPs. Pretraining a 7B model on one\ntrillion tokens is about 4.2 × 10²² FLOPs; at an illustrative sustained 400 TFLOP/s on one GPU (an H100's\ndense bf16 peak is about 989 TFLOP/s, and 40% utilisation is a realistic ballpark) that is ~10⁸ seconds,\nover three years. Pretraining is a thousands-of-GPUs problem by arithmetic, not ambition.\n\nThe naive fixes each solve one limit and not the other. Replicating the model on N GPUs and splitting\nthe batch (plain data parallelism) divides time by nearly N but leaves every replica holding the full\n112 GB. Putting different layers on different GPUs (naive model parallelism) divides memory but runs one\nGPU at a time while the others idle. The design space of distributed training is the set of ways to split\nboth state and work so that the GPUs stay busy and the communication they need fits the links between\nthem.","how":"### Data parallelism (DDP)\nEvery GPU holds a full replica and processes a different micro-batch. After backward, gradients are\naveraged with an all-reduce; in a ring all-reduce each GPU sends and receives about `2·(N−1)/N` times the\ngradient size, so the volume is roughly two gradient-sized transfers per step regardless of N. DDP\nbuckets gradients and starts reducing early buckets while later layers are still in backward, hiding most\nof the cost. Throughput scales with N; memory per GPU does not change. The global batch becomes\nN × micro-batch × accumulation steps, which is why learning rate and warmup must be re-tuned when the GPU\ncount changes.\n\n### Sharded data parallelism: ZeRO and FSDP\nThe waste in DDP is that all N replicas store identical optimizer state and gradients. ZeRO partitions\nthem across the data-parallel group and gathers only what each step needs. Per GPU, with Ψ parameters\nand N ranks:\n\n```\nDDP            16Ψ\nZeRO stage 1   2Ψ + 2Ψ + 12Ψ/N     optimizer state sharded\nZeRO stage 2   2Ψ + 14Ψ/N          gradients sharded too\nZeRO stage 3   16Ψ/N               parameters sharded too\n```\n\nFor 7B on 8 GPUs: 112 GB, ~38.5 GB, ~26 GB and 14 GB respectively, before activations. Stage 3 pays for\nthis by all-gathering each layer's parameters just before it is used in forward and again in backward,\nthen reduce-scattering its gradients, which is about 1.5× DDP's communication volume but is spread\nthrough the step and overlapped by prefetching the next layer. PyTorch FSDP is the native implementation\nof the same idea: `SHARD_GRAD_OP` is roughly stage 2, `FULL_SHARD` is stage 3, and `HYBRID_SHARD`\nshards within a node and replicates across nodes so the frequent all-gathers stay on fast intra-node\nlinks. The wrapping unit (usually one transformer block) sets peak memory, because one unit is held\nunsharded at a time. DeepSpeed adds ZeRO-Offload (optimizer state and gradients to CPU RAM) and\nZeRO-Infinity (NVMe) for jobs that still do not fit, at a large bandwidth cost.\n\n### Tensor parallelism\nWhen a single layer is too large, or per-GPU activation memory and latency must fall without idle time,\nsplit the matrices themselves (Megatron-LM). In the MLP, the first linear is split by columns so each GPU\ncomputes its slice and applies GELU independently; the second linear is split by rows so each GPU\nproduces a partial sum, combined with one all-reduce. Attention splits heads across GPUs the same way.\nThat is two all-reduces per layer in forward and two in backward, each moving an `s·b·h` activation,\nso tensor parallelism is bandwidth- and latency-hungry and is kept inside a node over NVLink-class\nlinks, typically at degree 2–8.\n\n### Pipeline parallelism\nAssign contiguous groups of layers to stages on different GPUs and split the batch into m micro-batches\nthat stream through so stages work concurrently (GPipe). The idle fraction, the bubble, is roughly\n`(p−1)/(m+p−1)` for p stages: four stages with sixteen micro-batches idle about 16% of the time, with\nfour micro-batches about 43%. The 1F1B schedule interleaves forward and backward to cap how many\nmicro-batches' activations are alive at once. Only stage-boundary activations cross GPUs, once per\nmicro-batch, so pipeline parallelism tolerates slower inter-node links. Its costs are the bubble, stage\nimbalance, and the engineering of partitioning a model.\n\n### 3D parallelism and the choice procedure\nLarge runs compose them: tensor parallelism inside a node, pipeline across nodes, and data or\nsharded-data parallelism over the remaining replicas, with world size = TP × PP × DP. Long contexts add\nsequence or context parallelism, which splits the sequence dimension (ring attention). The decision\norder for most teams: (1) if the state fits with FSDP or ZeRO-3 across the available GPUs, use only that;\nit covers nearly all fine-tuning. (2) If a layer or its activations still do not fit, or step latency\nmatters, add tensor parallelism within the node. (3) If the job spans many nodes with slow interconnect,\nadd pipeline parallelism rather than stretching tensor parallelism across the network.\n\n### What the frameworks provide\nPyTorch ships DDP, FSDP (and the newer `fully_shard` FSDP2 API), the `torchrun` launcher and the NCCL\nbackend. DeepSpeed provides ZeRO stages 1–3, offloading and a pipeline engine behind a JSON config, and\nplugs into the Hugging Face Trainer and Accelerate. Megatron-LM/Megatron-Core implements tensor,\npipeline and sequence parallelism for pretraining. Accelerate and Axolotl expose FSDP or DeepSpeed as\nconfiguration so the training script does not change.","example":"A minimal full-parameter fine-tune of a 7B model across eight GPUs with FSDP in `FULL_SHARD` mode (the\nZeRO-3 equivalent), wrapping per decoder block and checkpointing activations per block. The model is\nloaded in bf16 to keep the example short; production runs usually keep fp32 master weights through the\nmixed-precision policy. Launch with `torchrun --nproc_per_node=8 train.py`.\n\n```python\nimport os, functools, torch, torch.distributed as dist\nfrom torch.distributed.fsdp import FullyShardedDataParallel as FSDP, MixedPrecision, ShardingStrategy\nfrom torch.distributed.fsdp.wrap import transformer_auto_wrap_policy\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nfrom transformers.models.qwen2.modeling_qwen2 import Qwen2DecoderLayer\n\ndist.init_process_group(\"nccl\")\nrank = int(os.environ[\"LOCAL_RANK\"]); torch.cuda.set_device(rank)\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16)\nmodel.gradient_checkpointing_enable(gradient_checkpointing_kwargs={\"use_reentrant\": False})\n\nmodel = FSDP(\n    model,\n    auto_wrap_policy=functools.partial(transformer_auto_wrap_policy,\n                                      transformer_layer_cls={Qwen2DecoderLayer}),\n    sharding_strategy=ShardingStrategy.FULL_SHARD,      # params, grads, optimizer state all sharded\n    mixed_precision=MixedPrecision(param_dtype=torch.bfloat16, reduce_dtype=torch.bfloat16),\n    device_id=rank, use_orig_params=True,\n)\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-5)\n\nbatch = tok([\"Replace me with a sharded DataLoader.\"] * 4, return_tensors=\"pt\", padding=True).to(rank)\nlabels = batch[\"input_ids\"].clone(); labels[batch[\"attention_mask\"] == 0] = -100\nfor step in range(10):\n    loss = model(**batch, labels=labels).loss\n    loss.backward(); opt.step(); opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    if rank == 0: print(step, round(loss.item(), 4))\ndist.destroy_process_group()\n# Per GPU the sharded bf16 weights are ~14 GB / 8 ≈ 1.75 GB; the all-gather of one decoder block\n# plus its activations is what actually sets peak memory.\n```","tradeoffs":["DDP is the simplest and most compute-efficient scheme, but every GPU holds the full state; it flips to FSDP or ZeRO the moment weights plus optimizer state exceed one card, or batch is memory-limited.","ZeRO-3/FSDP divides memory by N but adds all-gathers on the critical path and is sensitive to the interconnect; across slow inter-node links use hybrid sharding so gathers stay inside the node.","Tensor parallelism cuts per-GPU weight and activation memory with no bubble, but needs NVLink-class bandwidth every layer; it flips to pipeline parallelism as soon as the split crosses a node boundary.","Pipeline parallelism is communication-light, but its bubble only shrinks when micro-batches far outnumber stages; it flips against you when the global batch is small or stages are unbalanced.","CPU or NVMe offload fits otherwise impossible jobs on few GPUs at a large speed cost; it pays only when wall-clock is cheap and GPUs are not."],"pitfalls":["Scaling to more GPUs without re-tuning for the larger global batch; the symptom is a loss curve that no longer matches the single-GPU run, usually because the learning rate and warmup were left unchanged.","Materialising the full model on every rank before sharding; the symptom is host-RAM or GPU OOM during initialisation of a 70B model, fixed by meta-device init and syncing weights from rank 0.","Wrapping the whole model as one FSDP unit; the symptom is OOM at the first all-gather despite `FULL_SHARD`, because the entire parameter set is gathered at once instead of one block.","Uneven data across ranks so one rank skips a collective; the symptom is a run that hangs at 100% GPU utilisation with no progress, fixed with `drop_last`, a join context or an explicit barrier.","Saving sharded checkpoints tied to one world size; the symptom is resume failing after changing the GPU count, avoided by distributed checkpoint formats that reshard or by consolidating on save.","Using tensor parallelism across GPUs connected only by PCIe or Ethernet; the symptom is single-digit model FLOPs utilisation while the links sit saturated."],"when":"If your role builds LLM applications, you need this lesson's vocabulary and arithmetic, not its practice:\nenough to read a DeepSpeed or FSDP config, to know why a 70B full fine-tune needs more than one 80 GB node,\nand to recognise that the cheapest \"distributed\" option is usually to not need it (LoRA, QLoRA, a\nsmaller base). If your role trains models, the day-to-day decisions are the ZeRO stage or sharding\nstrategy, the FSDP wrap unit, whether to add tensor parallelism inside the node, the global batch and\nlearning-rate schedule, and checkpoint format. Measure tokens per second per GPU against the single-GPU\nbaseline (scaling efficiency) and model FLOPs utilisation. In interviews, the expected depth is to say\nwhat each scheme communicates, how often, and which link it needs; and to derive the 16-bytes-per-parameter\nmemory budget on the spot.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks the sharding strategy and wrap policy, launches with `torchrun` or Accelerate, and checks scaling efficiency and MFU before trusting that eight GPUs are doing eight GPUs' worth of work.","architect":"Owns the training topology and cost model: node interconnect, GPU count, whether full fine-tuning is justified over PEFT at all, and the `6 × params × tokens` estimate behind every schedule.","fde":"Rarely runs this, but reads a customer's DeepSpeed or FSDP config to explain why their eight-GPU ZeRO-3 fine-tune will not reproduce on the four-GPU staging box they want to use."},"check":{"q":"A team wants a full-parameter SFT of a 70B model on one node of eight 80 GB GPUs. Does it fit, and\nwhat are their options if not? Show the arithmetic.","a":"With Adam in mixed precision the state is 16 bytes per parameter, so 70B × 16 ≈ 1.12 TB. ZeRO-3 or\nFSDP across eight GPUs leaves 140 GB per GPU before activations, which exceeds 80 GB, so it does not fit.\nOptions: offload the 12 bytes-per-parameter of fp32 master weights and Adam moments to CPU (about 840 GB\nof host RAM, and much slower); add a second node so sixteen GPUs hold 70 GB each, which is tight and\nneeds gradient checkpointing, a small micro-batch and hybrid sharding to keep all-gathers intra-node; or\nchange the problem to LoRA on a bf16 base (140 GB of weights sharded to ~17.5 GB per GPU plus a tiny\noptimizer state) or QLoRA. The arithmetic decides, not the framework."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which parallelism scheme communicates activations in every layer and therefore needs NVLink-class bandwidth?","options":["Data parallelism, which all-reduces gradients once per step","Tensor parallelism, which all-reduces partial results inside every layer","Pipeline parallelism, which sends activations only at stage boundaries","ZeRO stage 1, which shards only the optimizer state"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Splitting a matrix across GPUs means each layer's output must be combined before the next layer runs, so the traffic is per layer and latency-sensitive; the other schemes communicate far less often."},{"q":"A 7B model trained with Adam in mixed precision across 8 GPUs under ZeRO stage 3 needs roughly how much state per GPU, before activations?","options":["About 112 GB, the same as data parallelism","About 56 GB, because only the optimizer state is split","About 14 GB, since all 16 bytes per parameter are sharded across the 8 ranks","About 1.75 GB, since only the bf16 weights are sharded"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stage 3 shards weights, gradients and optimizer state, so per-GPU memory is 16Ψ/N = 112 GB / 8. The 1.75 GB figure counts only the bf16 weights and forgets the other 14 bytes per parameter."},{"q":"In a four-stage pipeline, what most directly reduces the idle bubble?","options":["Using fewer micro-batches so each stage finishes sooner","Using more micro-batches per step, since the bubble fraction is about (p−1)/(m+p−1)","Adding more pipeline stages to spread the work","Enabling gradient checkpointing inside each stage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bubble is the time stages wait for work to arrive; more micro-batches in flight keep every stage busy for a larger share of the step, while more stages or checkpointing make the bubble worse or leave it alone."}]}]});
